topic("pharm", "Analeptics, CNS Stimulants and Local Anaesthetics", {
  o: ["Classify CNS stimulants and analeptics", "Describe the xanthines, doxapram and strychnine", "Describe the mechanism of local anaesthetics", "Describe the esters and amides and their clinical use", "Describe the adverse effects and toxicity of local anaesthetics"],
  i: "The syllabus lists analeptics and CNS stimulants, and local anaesthetics. The first are mostly of limited modern use but important for exams. Local anaesthetics are used daily in practice for nerve blocks, epidurals and minor surgery.",
  s: "CNS stimulants excite the brain or the breathing centre. Their use is now limited, and overstimulation easily leads to convulsions. Local anaesthetics do the opposite for a small area: they block nerve conduction and remove feeling without making the animal unconscious.",
  n: [
    ["Classification of CNS stimulants", "Convulsants acting on the spinal cord (strychnine), analeptics acting on the brain stem and respiration (doxapram, nikethamide, caffeine), psychomotor stimulants (amphetamines), xanthines (caffeine, theophylline, theobromine), and others (picrotoxin, bemegride). Most were used to revive depressed breathing but are replaced by supportive care and ventilation."],
    ["Xanthines", "Caffeine, theophylline and theobromine block adenosine receptors and inhibit phosphodiesterase. Caffeine stimulates the cortex, theophylline relaxes bronchi and has a mild diuretic and cardiac stimulant action, and theobromine in chocolate is the cause of chocolate poisoning in dogs. Aminophylline is used as a bronchodilator in some cases. Narrow safety margin: tremors, tachycardia, arrhythmia, seizures."],
    ["Doxapram and respiratory stimulants", "Doxapram stimulates the respiratory centre, directly and through peripheral chemoreceptors, and is used to stimulate breathing in newborn animals and after anaesthesia in some situations. Its value is debated since it increases oxygen demand, so oxygen and ventilation come first. Over-dose causes hypertension, arrhythmia and seizures."],
    ["Strychnine and other convulsants", "Strychnine blocks inhibitory glycine receptors in the spinal cord, causing exaggerated reflexes, rigid extension and convulsions triggered by light, sound or touch, with death by respiratory muscle spasm. It has no therapeutic use today but is a poison (see toxicology). Picrotoxin blocks GABA-A channels. They are valuable for understanding spinal and brain inhibition."],
    ["Local anaesthetics: mechanism", "Local anaesthetics block voltage-gated sodium channels in the nerve membrane from the inside, so that action potentials cannot pass. Small, unmyelinated, and pain fibres are blocked first, followed by temperature, touch, pressure and motor fibres. The drug enters the nerve in its un-ionised form and works in the ionised form, so infected or acidic tissue, where more of the drug is ionised, reduces the effect."],
    ["Classification: esters and amides", "Esters (procaine, chloroprocaine, tetracaine, benzocaine, cocaine) are broken down by plasma cholinesterase and may cause allergy through the PABA metabolite. Amides (lignocaine or lidocaine, bupivacaine, mepivacaine, prilocaine, ropivacaine) are metabolised in the liver, rarely cause allergy, and are the main drugs today. Lidocaine is rapid and medium duration, bupivacaine is slow and long acting, and cardiotoxic if injected into a vessel. Cats are sensitive to lidocaine toxicity."],
    ["Adrenaline with local anaesthetics", "A vasoconstrictor such as adrenaline prolongs the effect and reduces toxicity by slowing absorption. It is avoided in areas with end arteries (ear tips, tail, penis, digits) because of the risk of tissue death, and with halothane or in animals with arrhythmia."],
    ["Techniques", "Surface (topical) anaesthesia of mucous membranes and cornea, infiltration, nerve block (cornual nerve block in cattle and goats, paravertebral blocks, inverted L block, intravenous regional anaesthesia), spinal and epidural anaesthesia (caudal epidural in cows). The surgery subject covers the techniques, and here the drugs matter."],
    ["Epidural and spinal use", "Epidural injection places the drug outside the dura to block nerves in the lumbosacral or sacrococcygeal area, giving analgesia and sometimes loss of motor function. Alpha-2 agonists and opioids are also used in the epidural space. Complications are hypotension, infection, injury to the spinal cord and unintended movement loss. Large animals may go down after high blocks."],
    ["Toxicity of local anaesthetics", "Overdose or intravascular injection gives CNS signs first (restlessness, muscle twitching, convulsions) followed by depression, and cardiovascular effects (arrhythmia, hypotension, cardiac arrest). Bupivacaine is more dangerous to the heart. Methaemoglobinaemia can occur with benzocaine and prilocaine, particularly in cats. Aspirating before injecting, using the lowest effective volume, and keeping within safe limits reduces risk."],
    ["Other uses", "Lidocaine given intravenously is an antiarrhythmic drug for ventricular arrhythmia, and is used in horses for gut motility and analgesia in some cases. Local anaesthetic splash blocks and infusions reduce postoperative pain. The multimodal analgesia plan often includes a local block."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Type", "Examples", "Metabolism", "Remarks"], r: [["Esters", "Procaine, tetracaine, benzocaine", "Plasma cholinesterase", "More allergy, short action"], ["Amides", "Lidocaine, bupivacaine, mepivacaine", "Liver", "Main drugs now; bupivacaine long acting and cardiotoxic"], ["Xanthines", "Caffeine, theophylline", "Liver", "Stimulant, bronchodilator, toxic in excess"], ["Respiratory stimulant", "Doxapram", "Liver", "Limited value, oxygen first"]] },
  df: ["Analeptic: a drug that stimulates the CNS, especially the respiratory centre.", "Local anaesthetic: a drug that reversibly blocks nerve conduction at its site of application.", "Epidural anaesthesia: injection of a drug into the epidural space to block nerves."],
  tm: [["Nerve block", "Anaesthesia of an area by blocking the supplying nerve"], ["Infiltration", "Injection into the tissue to be operated"], ["PABA", "Para-aminobenzoic acid, from ester breakdown"], ["Theobromine", "Xanthine in cocoa"], ["Strychnine", "Glycine receptor blocker"], ["Cornual block", "Nerve block of the horn"]],
  st: ["The area is prepared.", "Drug is injected near the nerve.", "It enters the nerve and blocks sodium channels.", "Pain and then other sensations are lost.", "The drug is absorbed and metabolised.", "Sensation returns."],
  ex: ["Lignocaine infiltration before suturing a wound.", "Cornual nerve block before dehorning in a calf.", "Chocolate poisoning in a dog due to theobromine."],
  k: ["Mechanism of local anaesthetics.", "Esters vs amides.", "Vasoconstrictor use and cautions.", "Toxicity signs.", "Xanthines and doxapram."],
  m: ["Local anaesthetics block sodium channels, not potassium. Infection reduces their effect. Adrenaline must not be used at end arteries."],
  sa: [["How do local anaesthetics act?", "They block voltage-gated sodium channels in the nerve membrane."], ["Why is adrenaline added to a local anaesthetic?", "To prolong the effect and reduce systemic absorption and toxicity."], ["What is the toxic compound in chocolate?", "Theobromine."]],
  la: [["Describe local anaesthetics with their classification, mechanism and uses.", "Sodium channel block, esters and amides, examples, techniques, adrenaline, toxicity."], ["Describe CNS stimulants and analeptics used in animals.", "Classification, xanthines, doxapram, strychnine, uses, limits."]],
  q: [["Lidocaine is an", ["Amide", "Ester", "Alkaloid ester", "Steroid"], 0, "Liver metabolism."],
    ["Strychnine blocks", ["Glycine receptors", "GABA-A receptors", "NMDA receptors", "Nicotinic receptors"], 0, "In the spinal cord."],
    ["Bupivacaine is", ["Long acting", "Very short acting", "A CNS stimulant", "An ester"], 0, "And cardiotoxic."],
    ["Adrenaline should be avoided with local anaesthetics in", ["Ear tips and digits", "Flank", "Muscles", "Ruminal wall"], 0, "End arteries."],
    ["Doxapram stimulates", ["Respiration", "Gut motility", "Uterus", "Bladder"], 0, "Via the respiratory centre."]],
  f: [["Analeptic", "Respiratory or CNS stimulant"], ["Ester local anaesthetic", "Broken by plasma cholinesterase"], ["Amide local anaesthetic", "Metabolised by liver"], ["Epidural", "Injection outside the dura"], ["Theobromine", "Xanthine in chocolate"]],
  d: ["Make a table of local anaesthetic agents.", "Learn the nerve blocks in cattle.", "Review cat toxicity with local anaesthetics."],
});

topic("pharm", "Skeletal Muscle Relaxants and Euthanizing Agents", {
  o: ["Classify muscle relaxants by site of action", "Describe the neuromuscular blockers: non-depolarising and depolarising", "Describe the centrally acting relaxants and dantrolene", "Describe the reversal of neuromuscular block", "Describe the principles and agents of euthanasia in animals"],
  i: "The syllabus lists muscle relaxants and euthanizing agents. Neuromuscular blockers make surgery easier but have no analgesic or sedative effect, so they need ventilation support. Euthanasia is a veterinary responsibility with ethical and legal duties.",
  s: "Muscle relaxants reduce muscle tone, either in the brain and spinal cord, or at the junction between nerve and muscle, or inside the muscle. Euthanasia is the humane ending of life with the least fear and pain, and the method is chosen by species, situation and law.",
  n: [
    ["Classification of muscle relaxants", "Neuromuscular blockers (peripheral): non-depolarising (tubocurarine, pancuronium, vecuronium, atracurium, rocuronium) and depolarising (succinylcholine, decamethonium). Centrally acting relaxants: guaifenesin, mephenesin, methocarbamol, diazepam, baclofen. Direct acting: dantrolene. Muscle relaxation is also a part of anaesthesia."],
    ["Non-depolarising blockers", "They compete with acetylcholine at the nicotinic receptors of the motor endplate and block depolarisation. Paralysis starts with small muscles of the eye, face and fingers, then limbs and trunk, and finally the diaphragm. Atracurium is broken down spontaneously in plasma (Hofmann elimination), which is useful in animals with liver or kidney disease. Tubocurarine can release histamine. Their effect is reversed by anticholinesterases like neostigmine and, for rocuronium, by sugammadex."],
    ["Depolarising blockers: succinylcholine", "Succinylcholine acts as an agonist at the motor endplate, causing initial muscle fasciculation followed by flaccid paralysis as the membrane stays depolarised. It is broken down by plasma cholinesterase, so action is short. Anticholinesterases do not reverse it and make it worse. Adverse effects: raised potassium, muscle pain, rise in intragastric and intraocular pressure, bradycardia, and malignant hyperthermia in susceptible pigs and other species. In cattle and sheep it was misused for immobilisation, which is painful and unethical as the animal is aware."],
    ["Phase II block and reversal", "After prolonged or repeated exposure to depolarising drugs, the block starts to resemble the non-depolarising type (phase II). Neostigmine reverses non-depolarising block by increasing acetylcholine, and atropine or glycopyrrolate is given to prevent the muscarinic effects. Monitoring neuromuscular function with a nerve stimulator is useful."],
    ["Safety rule for neuromuscular blockers", "These drugs paralyse without making the animal unconscious or pain free. They are used only under full general anaesthesia with controlled ventilation. Use in an awake animal is cruel. Their use for restraint of an unsedated animal is not acceptable, and also illegal in many places."],
    ["Centrally acting muscle relaxants", "Guaifenesin (glyceryl guaiacolate) is used in horses and cattle with other agents to produce relaxation and smooth induction. Methocarbamol is used in muscle spasm and some poisonings, as for strychnine or metaldehyde. Diazepam relaxes through GABA. Baclofen is a GABA-B agonist in humans for spasticity. These drugs act on interneurons in the spinal cord and brain stem."],
    ["Dantrolene", "Dantrolene acts inside the muscle fibre by blocking calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. It is the specific drug for the malignant hyperthermia syndrome, a genetic trait of some pigs, dogs and horses triggered by halothane and succinylcholine, in which muscle rigidity, high temperature and acidosis develop quickly. It is also used in some stress-related myopathies."],
    ["Euthanasia: principles", "Euthanasia means a good death. The method should cause rapid unconsciousness and death with minimal pain, fear and distress, be reliable and irreversible, be safe for the operator, be suited to the species, age and number of animals, and be aesthetically acceptable to observers. Death must be confirmed. National and professional guidelines (such as those of the AVMA) and the legal framework for animal welfare apply."],
    ["Chemical agents: injectable", "Overdose of a barbiturate (such as pentobarbitone) is the standard, given intravenously, producing deep anaesthesia and then respiratory and cardiac arrest. Combinations with other agents are used in some countries. Sedation first is advised in distressed animals. T-61 and similar products have been used but can cause distress if not given correctly, and are restricted in some regions. Potassium chloride is used only in an animal already under deep anaesthesia, never in a conscious animal."],
    ["Inhaled and other agents", "Anaesthetic gases (such as isoflurane or sevoflurane at high concentration) are used for small and young animals. Carbon dioxide is used for rodents and poultry in some situations with controversy about distress. Carbon monoxide has special uses. Physical methods (captive bolt, cervical dislocation, firearm, electrical stunning) are used in particular species or at slaughter by trained persons. The method for each species is detailed in the animal welfare and livestock subjects."],
    ["Practical and ethical points", "The veterinarian must consider the owner's feelings, give a clear explanation, handle the animal gently, and dispose of the body lawfully. Carcasses containing barbiturate residues may poison scavengers and dogs, which is a reason for proper disposal. The record of controlled drugs used must be kept, and the staff should be trained and supported in view of the stress of this work."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Drug / group", "Site of action", "Mechanism", "Key point"], r: [["Atracurium, vecuronium, pancuronium", "Motor endplate", "Competitive nicotinic block", "Reversed by neostigmine"], ["Succinylcholine", "Motor endplate", "Persistent depolarisation", "Short action, not reversed by neostigmine"], ["Guaifenesin", "Spinal cord and brain stem", "Interneuron depression", "Used in large animals"], ["Dantrolene", "Muscle fibre", "Blocks sarcoplasmic calcium release", "For malignant hyperthermia"], ["Pentobarbitone overdose", "CNS", "Deep depression", "Standard injectable euthanasia"]] },
  df: ["Neuromuscular blocker: a drug that interrupts transmission at the motor endplate, causing paralysis of skeletal muscle.", "Malignant hyperthermia: a life-threatening hypermetabolic state in skeletal muscle triggered by certain anaesthetics in susceptible animals.", "Euthanasia: the humane killing of an animal with minimal pain and distress."],
  tm: [["Motor endplate", "Specialised muscle membrane at the neuromuscular junction"], ["Fasciculation", "Muscle twitching before paralysis"], ["Hofmann elimination", "Spontaneous breakdown of atracurium"], ["Phase II block", "Non-depolarising type block after prolonged succinylcholine"], ["Sugammadex", "Agent that reverses rocuronium"], ["Captive bolt", "Physical method of stunning"]],
  st: ["The animal is under general anaesthesia.", "A neuromuscular blocker is given if needed.", "Ventilation is controlled.", "The block is monitored.", "It is reversed at the end with neostigmine and an antimuscarinic.", "Breathing returns and the animal recovers."],
  ex: ["Atracurium for a patient with kidney disease.", "Dantrolene for malignant hyperthermia in a pig.", "Barbiturate overdose for euthanasia of a dog after sedation."],
  k: ["Classification of muscle relaxants.", "Depolarising vs non-depolarising.", "Neostigmine reversal.", "Dantrolene.", "Principles of euthanasia."],
  m: ["Neuromuscular blockers are not analgesics. Neostigmine does not reverse succinylcholine. Potassium chloride must never be used in a conscious animal."],
  sa: [["How does succinylcholine act?", "It depolarises the motor endplate persistently, causing fasciculation and then paralysis."], ["What is the use of dantrolene?", "It treats malignant hyperthermia by blocking calcium release in muscle."], ["Name a drug used to euthanise dogs.", "Pentobarbitone in overdose (after sedation if needed)."]],
  la: [["Classify the skeletal muscle relaxants and describe the neuromuscular blockers.", "Types, mechanism, examples, reversal, adverse effects, safety."], ["Describe the euthanizing agents and the principles of euthanasia in animals.", "Criteria, barbiturates, inhalants, physical methods, confirmation of death, ethics, disposal."]],
  q: [["Atracurium is broken down by", ["Hofmann elimination", "Liver P450 only", "Renal excretion only", "Plasma esterase only"], 0, "Spontaneous breakdown."],
    ["Dantrolene acts by blocking", ["Calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum", "Nicotinic receptors", "Sodium channels", "GABA receptors"], 0, "Inside the muscle."],
    ["Succinylcholine is a", ["Depolarising blocker", "Non-depolarising blocker", "Sedative", "Opioid"], 0, "Short acting."],
    ["Neostigmine reverses", ["Non-depolarising block", "Phase I succinylcholine block", "Opioid effect", "Alpha-2 effect"], 0, "Raises acetylcholine."],
    ["Standard injectable euthanasia agent is", ["Pentobarbitone", "Atropine", "Xylazine", "Dantrolene"], 0, "Overdose."]],
  f: [["Neuromuscular blocker", "Blocks motor endplate"], ["Dantrolene", "Drug for malignant hyperthermia"], ["Guaifenesin", "Central muscle relaxant"], ["Euthanasia", "Humane killing"], ["Phase II block", "Late block after succinylcholine"]],
  d: ["Study euthanasia methods by species.", "Review the legal rules on controlled drugs.", "Learn the malignant hyperthermia story."],
});

topic("pharm", "Drugs Acting on the Digestive System", {
  o: ["Describe the stomachics, antacids, antiulcer drugs and prokinetics", "Describe carminatives, antizymotics and emetics", "Describe antiemetics and the mechanism of vomiting", "Describe purgatives and antidiarrhoeals", "Describe choleretics and cholagogues, and digestive enzymes"],
  i: "The syllabus lists digestive system drugs: stomachics, antacids, antiulcer drugs, prokinetics, carminatives, antizymotics, emetics, antiemetics, purgatives, antidiarrhoeals and choleretics. Gut disease is one of the commonest reasons for veterinary visits, so this is high-yield material.",
  s: "The digestive drugs either increase or reduce secretion and movement of the gut, empty it from above or below, or soothe and protect it. The choice depends on the species: a ruminant, a horse and a dog use the gut very differently, so the same drug may be useful in one and harmful in another.",
  n: [
    ["Stomachics and appetite stimulants", "Stomachics (bitters such as gentian, nux vomica in small amounts, and aromatics) are given to stimulate appetite and salivary and gastric secretions. Evidence is limited. Other appetite stimulants are cyproheptadine and mirtazapine in cats, benzodiazepines in some cases, and B vitamins. The cause of anorexia must be found."],
    ["Antacids", "Antacids neutralise gastric acid. Systemic ones (sodium bicarbonate) are absorbed and can cause alkalosis. Non-systemic ones (aluminium hydroxide, magnesium hydroxide, magnesium trisilicate, calcium carbonate) act locally, with aluminium causing constipation and magnesium diarrhoea. In ruminants, antacids (magnesium hydroxide, magnesium oxide, sodium bicarbonate) are used for acute rumen acidosis. Antacids interfere with absorption of other drugs by binding or by changing pH."],
    ["Antiulcer drugs", "H2 blockers (cimetidine, ranitidine, famotidine) and proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole, pantoprazole) reduce acid output. Sucralfate forms a protective layer on ulcers in acid. Misoprostol is a prostaglandin analogue that protects the mucosa. Bismuth compounds coat ulcers and have some antibacterial effect. Gastric ulcers in dogs, horses and pigs are associated with stress, NSAIDs, mast cell tumours and management factors."],
    ["Prokinetics", "Prokinetics increase coordinated gut movements. Metoclopramide blocks dopamine D2 receptors and stimulates 5-HT4 receptors, and is used for gastric emptying and vomiting. Cisapride and mosapride act on 5-HT4 receptors. Domperidone is a peripheral dopamine blocker. Erythromycin acts on motilin receptors. Bethanechol and neostigmine are cholinergic prokinetics. They are contraindicated in mechanical obstruction. Lidocaine infusions are used in horses with ileus."],
    ["Carminatives and antizymotics", "Carminatives (ginger, peppermint, asafoetida, fennel, ajwain and similar aromatic drugs) help expel gas and relieve spasm, and are widely used in traditional veterinary practice. Antizymotics (antifermentatives) such as turpentine oil, formalin and others reduce excess fermentation in the rumen, and surfactants like dioctyl sodium sulphosuccinate or vegetable oils and silicones (anti-foaming) help in frothy bloat. Mechanisms differ from drug to drug."],
    ["Emetics", "Emetics induce vomiting to remove ingested poison. Centrally acting apomorphine stimulates the chemoreceptor trigger zone and is used in dogs. Xylazine is used in cats. Local irritants (hydrogen peroxide, washing soda crystals, salt, mustard) are unreliable and may be dangerous. Emetics must not be used if corrosives, petroleum products or sharp objects were swallowed, if the animal is unconscious, convulsing or has a depressed gag reflex, and the time since ingestion is long. Ruminants, horses, rabbits and rodents cannot vomit effectively."],
    ["Mechanism of vomiting and antiemetics", "Vomiting is coordinated by the vomiting centre in the medulla, fed by the chemoreceptor trigger zone (dopamine D2, 5-HT3, NK1 and opioid receptors), the vestibular system (H1, muscarinic), the gut (5-HT3, vagal afferents) and the cortex. Antiemetics: phenothiazines (chlorpromazine, prochlorperazine) block D2 and H1, metoclopramide, 5-HT3 blockers (ondansetron), NK1 blockers (maropitant, the most used in small animals), antihistamines and anticholinergics for motion sickness. Treat the cause as well."],
    ["Purgatives (laxatives)", "Bulk laxatives (bran, psyllium, methylcellulose) increase volume. Lubricants (liquid paraffin) soften faeces, with the problem that they can interfere with absorption of fat-soluble vitamins and may be aspirated. Saline purgatives (magnesium sulphate, sodium sulphate) hold water by osmosis, and are used in ruminants and horses. Irritants (castor oil, aloes, senna, rhubarb, phenolphthalein) stimulate the gut wall. Castor oil is hydrolysed in the small intestine to ricinoleic acid. Dosage forms and species suitability vary, and purgatives are contraindicated in pregnancy (some), obstruction and severe dehydration."],
    ["Antidiarrhoeals", "Opioids (loperamide, diphenoxylate) reduce motility, but loperamide is dangerous in MDR1-mutant dogs and in horses and cats. Adsorbents (kaolin, pectin, activated charcoal) bind toxins. Protectants (bismuth subsalicylate, chalk). Antimotility drugs are not used in infectious diarrhoea with fever or blood. Fluid and electrolyte replacement is the main therapy. Antibiotics are used only on a clear indication, and probiotics support recovery."],
    ["Choleretics, cholagogues and digestive enzymes", "Choleretics increase bile production (bile salts, dehydrocholic acid). Cholagogues stimulate gall bladder emptying (magnesium sulphate, fats). They are of limited modern use. Pancreatic enzyme supplements are given in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency. Ursodeoxycholic acid is used in some liver and bile duct disorders."],
    ["Species points", "Ruminants: rumen function guides all drug choice (next topic). Horses: antispasmodics and NSAIDs for colic, prokinetics for ileus, and care with oral antibiotics. Dogs and cats: antiemetics, gastroprotectants, probiotics and diet. Pigs and poultry: group treatments with attention to residues. Always diagnose first: dosing details are in the formulary and clinical subjects."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Group", "Examples", "Action", "Remark"], r: [["Antacid", "Magnesium hydroxide, aluminium hydroxide, sodium bicarbonate", "Neutralise acid", "Interfere with absorption of other drugs"], ["Acid suppressant", "Ranitidine, omeprazole", "Block H2 or proton pump", "For ulcers"], ["Prokinetic", "Metoclopramide, cisapride", "Increase coordinated motility", "Contraindicated in obstruction"], ["Emetic", "Apomorphine, xylazine (cats)", "Stimulate CTZ", "Not for corrosives or in ruminants"], ["Antiemetic", "Maropitant, ondansetron, metoclopramide", "Block vomiting pathways", "Treat the cause"], ["Purgative", "Magnesium sulphate, castor oil, liquid paraffin", "Increase gut transit", "Avoid in obstruction"], ["Antidiarrhoeal", "Kaolin, loperamide, bismuth", "Adsorb or reduce motility", "Fluids are the mainstay"]] },
  df: ["Carminative: a drug that relieves flatulence by promoting expulsion of gas.", "Prokinetic: a drug that stimulates coordinated gastrointestinal motility.", "Purgative: a drug that promotes defaecation, ranging from mild laxatives to drastic cathartics."],
  tm: [["Stomachic", "Drug that stimulates appetite and digestion"], ["Antizymotic", "Drug that reduces fermentation"], ["Chemoreceptor trigger zone", "Brain centre that triggers vomiting"], ["Cholagogue", "Drug that promotes bile flow from the gall bladder"], ["Choleretic", "Drug that increases bile secretion"], ["Adsorbent", "Substance that binds toxins in the gut"]],
  st: ["The cause of gut disease is identified.", "Fluids and electrolytes are corrected.", "A drug group is selected by species and cause.", "Antacid, antiulcer, prokinetic or antiemetic is added.", "Purgative or antidiarrhoeal is used only on indication.", "Response is followed."],
  ex: ["Maropitant for vomiting in a dog.", "Magnesium sulphate as a saline purgative in a cow.", "Apomorphine to induce vomiting after toxin ingestion in a dog."],
  k: ["Antiulcer drugs.", "Prokinetics.", "Emetics and their contraindications.", "Antiemetics and the vomiting pathway.", "Purgatives and antidiarrhoeals."],
  m: ["Emetics are not safe in all poisoning. Loperamide is dangerous in MDR1 dogs. Antacids interact with other drugs."],
  sa: [["Name one prokinetic and its mechanism.", "Metoclopramide, which blocks dopamine D2 receptors and stimulates 5-HT4 receptors."], ["When is an emetic contraindicated?", "After corrosives or petroleum products, in unconscious or convulsing animals."], ["Name the action of saline purgatives.", "They hold water in the gut by osmosis."]],
  la: [["Describe the antiulcer drugs and prokinetics.", "H2 blockers, PPIs, sucralfate, misoprostol, metoclopramide, cisapride, uses and cautions."], ["Describe the purgatives and antidiarrhoeals.", "Classes, mechanisms, examples, contraindications, species points."]],
  q: [["Omeprazole inhibits the", ["Proton pump", "H2 receptor", "Muscarinic receptor", "Gastrin"], 0, "H+/K+ ATPase."],
    ["Maropitant blocks", ["NK1 receptors", "5-HT4 receptors", "D2 receptors", "H2 receptors"], 0, "Anti-emetic."],
    ["Castor oil is an", ["Irritant purgative", "Bulk laxative", "Lubricant", "Saline"], 0, "Ricinoleic acid."],
    ["Apomorphine acts on the", ["Chemoreceptor trigger zone", "Rumen", "Liver", "Kidney"], 0, "Central emetic."],
    ["Magnesium sulphate is a", ["Saline purgative", "Antacid only", "Emetic", "Prokinetic"], 0, "Osmotic action."]],
  f: [["Antacid", "Neutralises acid"], ["Prokinetic", "Promotes motility"], ["Emetic", "Induces vomiting"], ["Carminative", "Expels gas"], ["Choleretic", "Increases bile secretion"]],
  d: ["Draw the vomiting pathways.", "Make a table of purgatives by species.", "Learn contraindications for each group."],
});

topic("pharm", "Rumen Pharmacology: Drugs for the Ruminant Stomach", {
  o: ["Explain how the rumen alters the fate of drugs", "Describe the drugs used for bloat, acidosis, atony and impaction", "Describe rumen stimulants, antizymotics and anti-foaming agents", "Describe the effects of antibiotics on rumen flora", "Describe the ruminant-specific cautions for drugs and feed additives"],
  i: "The syllabus lists rumen pharmacology as a separate item, because the rumen changes drug behaviour so much. India is a major ruminant keeping country, so this is a particularly relevant topic. Anatomy and physiology of the ruminant stomach are in the earlier subjects.",
  s: "The rumen is a huge fermentation vat of microbes. A drug given by mouth to an adult cow or buffalo is diluted, bound to feed, and may be altered by the microbes. At the same time, the drug can damage the microbes and upset digestion. So ruminant drug use always asks two questions: what does the rumen do to the drug, and what does the drug do to the rumen?",
  n: [
    ["Effect of the rumen on oral drugs", "Large volume dilutes drugs, with a slow, delayed and uneven passage to the abomasum and small intestine. Binding to plant fibre and to particles reduces the free drug. Rumen microbes can destroy drugs by reduction and hydrolysis (chloramphenicol and some others are destroyed), and the rumen pH can ionise drugs. As a result, oral antibiotic therapy in adult ruminants is often unreliable, and can damage the flora. Some drugs, such as some anthelmintics, benefit from the slow release of the rumen. In the milk-fed calf the oesophageal groove sends milk straight to the abomasum, and oral drugs given in milk behave as in a monogastric animal."],
    ["Oesophageal groove reflex", "In young ruminants, suckling closes the groove, shunting liquid to the abomasum. Some drugs in solution (such as copper sulphate in older animals and certain stimuli) can trigger the reflex, a fact used to bypass the rumen. In adults, the reflex is weak. This explains why oral dosing by drench to calves can act differently."],
    ["Rumen stimulants", "Used in rumen atony. Drugs: cholinergic parasympathomimetics (neostigmine, carbachol in small safe amounts), yohimbine and similar alpha-2 antagonists in some cases, nux vomica preparations (strychnine in small amount) in traditional use, and tonics. Importantly, the cause (indigestion, hypocalcaemia, ketosis, infection, pain) is addressed first. Transfaunation (rumen fluid from a healthy cow) re-establishes the flora, and is highly effective as a practical measure."],
    ["Treatment concepts for bloat", "In frothy bloat, stable foam traps the gas. Anti-foaming agents are surfactants that break the foam: poloxalene, dimethicone (silicone), vegetable oils (groundnut, mustard, linseed oils), turpentine oil, and detergents. In free-gas bloat, a stomach tube or trocar relieves pressure, together with antizymotics. Poloxalene is also used as a preventive in animals on bloat-causing pasture. Emergency decisions belong to the medicine and surgery subjects."],
    ["Rumen acidosis", "Rapid intake of grain increases lactic acid, drops pH, kills protozoa, and shifts to lactate-producing Streptococcus bovis and lactobacilli, causing systemic acidosis and dehydration. Pharmacological principle: antacids such as magnesium hydroxide or sodium bicarbonate raise rumen pH, and intravenous fluids correct acidosis. Antibiotics that kill lactic acid producing bacteria were used. Buffers in feed prevent the problem, and gradual introduction of grain is the main prevention."],
    ["Rumen impaction and purgatives", "In impaction, fibrous mass accumulates. Rumen purgatives: saline (magnesium sulphate, sodium sulphate) in large dilute amounts, and paraffin oil. Irritant purgatives are used with care in ruminants because their gut is sensitive. Lubricants and fluids soften contents. Surgical removal may be needed. Details in the clinical subject."],
    ["Antibiotics and rumen flora", "Oral broad-spectrum antibiotics destroy rumen microbes and cause digestive upset, loss of appetite and diarrhoea. The effect is worse with repeated doses, so parenteral therapy is preferred for systemic infection. Ionophores (monensin, lasalocid), which are not used in humans, are feed additives that shift fermentation toward propionate, improving feed efficiency and reducing risk of acidosis and bloat. They are lethal to horses and toxic to other species and to cattle in overdose."],
    ["Feed additives and growth promoters", "Ionophores, urea (non-protein nitrogen, risk of ammonia toxicity in excess), bypass protein and fat, buffers (sodium bicarbonate), yeast cultures and probiotics are used to manage rumen function. Hormonal growth promoters and antibiotic growth promoters are restricted in many countries because of residue and resistance concerns. Urea use needs gradual adaptation and is covered in toxicology."],
    ["Drugs not suitable for ruminants", "Some drugs are unsafe: oral antibiotics in adults, chloramphenicol in food animals, certain nitroimidazoles, and drugs like phenylbutazone in dairy animals in many places. Alpha-2 agonists such as xylazine have special sensitivity in cattle and can cause rumen atony and bloat. Anaesthesia needs fasting and airway protection. Check the label and the legal list for each species."],
    ["Practical considerations", "Dosage form matters: boluses, drenches, pour-ons, injectables and intraruminal devices. Route selection depends on the target: parenteral for systemic disease, oral for rumen or gut action and for some anthelmintics. Withdrawal times in milk and meat must be respected. Rumen fluid can be used for transfaunation, and testing of rumen pH and protozoa helps diagnosis."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Problem", "Drug concept", "Examples", "Note"], r: [["Frothy bloat", "Anti-foaming surfactants", "Poloxalene, silicones, vegetable oils", "Relieve gas mechanically if life-threatening"], ["Rumen acidosis", "Antacids and fluids", "Magnesium hydroxide, bicarbonate", "Prevent by gradual grain introduction"], ["Rumen atony", "Stimulants and transfaunation", "Cholinergics, rumen liquor", "Treat the cause"], ["Impaction", "Saline purgatives, lubricants", "Magnesium sulphate, paraffin oil", "Surgery may be needed"], ["Feed efficiency", "Ionophores", "Monensin, lasalocid", "Toxic to horses"]] },
  df: ["Rumen pharmacology: the study of drug behaviour in the ruminant forestomach and the effects of drugs on rumen function.", "Transfaunation: transfer of rumen fluid from a healthy animal to a sick one to restore rumen microbes.", "Ionophore: an antibiotic feed additive that alters ion transport in microbes and shifts rumen fermentation."],
  tm: [["Oesophageal groove", "Muscular groove carrying milk straight to the abomasum"], ["Anti-foaming agent", "Surfactant that breaks foam in bloat"], ["Rumen atony", "Loss of rumen motility"], ["Rumen acidosis", "Low rumen pH from excess grain"], ["Drench", "Liquid medicine given by mouth"], ["Poloxalene", "Non-ionic surfactant for bloat"]],
  st: ["Oral drug enters the rumen and is diluted.", "It binds to fibre and may be degraded by microbes.", "Part passes on to the abomasum and intestine.", "Microbes are affected by the drug.", "Digestion may be disturbed.", "A parenteral route is chosen for systemic disease."],
  ex: ["Anti-foaming agent used in a calf with bloat.", "Transfaunation for rumen atony after antibiotics.", "Monensin in a ration improves feed efficiency, but is lethal to horses."],
  k: ["How the rumen changes oral drugs.", "Oesophageal groove.", "Bloat and acidosis drugs.", "Ionophores.", "Drugs to avoid."],
  m: ["Oral antibiotics are not reliable in adult ruminants. Calves before rumen development respond like monogastrics. Ionophores are not safe for horses."],
  sa: [["What is transfaunation?", "Transfer of rumen fluid from a healthy animal to restore microbial flora."], ["Name an anti-foaming agent.", "Poloxalene (or a silicone or vegetable oil)."], ["Why is oral antibiotic therapy poor in adult ruminants?", "Dilution, binding and destruction in the rumen, and damage to the microbial flora."]],
  la: [["Describe rumen pharmacology and the factors influencing drug action in ruminants.", "Rumen effect on drugs, oesophageal groove, antibiotics and flora, parenteral routes, cautions."], ["Describe the drugs used in the treatment of bloat, acidosis and atony.", "Anti-foaming agents, antacids, stimulants, purgatives, transfaunation, preventive measures."]],
  q: [["Poloxalene is used for", ["Frothy bloat", "Mastitis", "Fever", "Tetany"], 0, "Surfactant."],
    ["Monensin is a", ["Ionophore", "Anthelmintic", "Antacid", "Purgative"], 0, "Feed additive."],
    ["The oesophageal groove is functional in", ["Suckling young ruminants", "Adult buffaloes only", "Horses", "Dogs"], 0, "Directs milk to the abomasum."],
    ["Transfaunation uses", ["Rumen fluid", "Blood", "Saliva only", "Bile"], 0, "Restores flora."],
    ["Rumen acidosis is mainly caused by", ["Excess grain", "Excess hay", "Water deficiency", "Salt deficiency"], 0, "Lactic acid builds up."]],
  f: [["Transfaunation", "Rumen fluid transfer"], ["Ionophore", "Rumen modifier antibiotic"], ["Oesophageal groove", "Milk passage to abomasum"], ["Anti-foaming agent", "Breaks bloat foam"], ["Rumen atony", "Loss of rumen motility"]],
  d: ["Compare rumen vs monogastric drug fate.", "Learn the bloat treatment drugs.", "Study the effects of antibiotics on flora."],
});
