unit("pharm", "Syllabus additions: cardiovascular, respiratory, urogenital, fluids, hormones and skin");

topic("pharm", "Cardiovascular Drugs: Cardiotonics, Antiarrhythmics, Vasodilators and Antihypertensives", {
  o: ["Describe the cardiac glycosides and other positive inotropes", "Classify antiarrhythmic drugs by the Vaughan Williams scheme", "Describe the vasodilators and diuretic support in heart failure", "Describe the antihypertensive drug groups", "Explain the pharmacological approach to congestive heart failure in small animals"],
  i: "The syllabus lists cardiotonics, antiarrhythmics, vasodilators and antihypertensives. Heart disease is common in ageing dogs and cats, and these drugs have narrow safety margins. Only mechanisms and the logic of use are given here; doses belong to your formulary.",
  s: "In heart failure, the heart cannot pump enough blood, so the body holds on to salt and water and tightens its vessels, which makes things worse. Cardiac drugs try to strengthen the beat, slow or steady the rhythm, widen the vessels and remove the excess fluid. Each has a narrow margin, so understanding mechanism protects the patient.",
  n: [
    ["Cardiac glycosides: digoxin", "Digoxin inhibits the Na+/K+ ATPase in cardiac muscle. Intracellular sodium rises, calcium leaves less through the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger, and calcium inside the cell increases, strengthening contraction (positive inotropy). It also increases vagal tone, slows conduction through the AV node and so slows the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation. The source plants are foxglove (Digitalis purpurea and lanata), and squill, oleander and strophanthus contain related glycosides."],
    ["Digoxin: narrow margin and toxicity", "Digoxin has a low therapeutic index. Toxicity gives anorexia, vomiting, diarrhoea, and arrhythmias of any kind, and low potassium makes it worse, while high potassium reduces the effect. Clearance is by the kidney, so kidney disease raises levels, and drug interactions (quinidine, some antibiotics) matter. Blood level monitoring is advised. Cats are more sensitive, horses clear it quickly, and there are breed variations in dogs. It is now less used than before since newer drugs have taken over many of its roles."],
    ["Other positive inotropes", "Pimobendan is an inodilator: it sensitises the myofilaments to calcium and inhibits phosphodiesterase III, so it strengthens the heart and dilates vessels. It is widely used in canine heart failure from valve disease or dilated cardiomyopathy. Dobutamine and dopamine are short-term intravenous inotropes in critical care. Amrinone and milrinone are phosphodiesterase inhibitors in intensive care."],
    ["Antiarrhythmic drugs: classification", "Vaughan Williams: Class I block sodium channels (IA quinidine, procainamide; IB lidocaine, mexiletine; IC flecainide). Class II are beta-blockers (propranolol, atenolol). Class III prolong repolarisation and block potassium channels (amiodarone, sotalol). Class IV are calcium channel blockers (verapamil, diltiazem). Others: digoxin, atropine for bradyarrhythmia, and magnesium. Arrhythmias must be diagnosed by ECG first, because treatment is different for each kind."],
    ["Drugs for specific arrhythmias", "Ventricular tachycardia in dogs: lidocaine intravenously, with procainamide or mexiletine for ongoing control, sotalol and beta-blockers for chronic cases. Supraventricular tachycardia and atrial fibrillation: diltiazem or digoxin to slow the ventricular rate, and beta-blockers. Bradycardia and heart block: atropine and glycopyrrolate (vagal), or a pacemaker. Quinidine is used for atrial fibrillation in horses. Antiarrhythmic drugs can themselves cause arrhythmias (proarrhythmic effect)."],
    ["Vasodilators", "Arterial dilators (hydralazine, amlodipine) reduce afterload. Venodilators (nitroglycerin and isosorbide) reduce preload. Balanced dilators (sodium nitroprusside, ACE inhibitors, prazosin) act on both. In left heart failure with valve leakage, dilating the arteries reduces regurgitation. Nitroglycerin is used topically for pulmonary oedema in dogs in some countries. Hypotension is the main adverse effect."],
    ["ACE inhibitors and diuretics in heart failure", "ACE inhibitors (enalapril, benazepril, ramipril) reduce angiotensin II and aldosterone, lower vessel resistance and fluid retention, and slow cardiac remodelling. Diuretics (furosemide, thiazides, spironolactone) remove excess fluid to relieve oedema and effusions. Spironolactone blocks aldosterone and adds benefit in some dogs. Furosemide is the key drug for pulmonary oedema but can cause dehydration, low potassium and kidney problems, so it is used at the lowest effective dose."],
    ["Calcium channel blockers and beta-blockers in the heart", "Verapamil and diltiazem block L-type calcium channels, reducing heart rate through the AV node and relaxing vessels. Amlodipine is mainly a vascular dilator and is used for hypertension in cats. Beta-blockers reduce rate and force, and help hypertrophic cardiomyopathy by improving filling. They must be started with care in a decompensated heart."],
    ["Antihypertensive drugs", "Systemic hypertension in cats and dogs is usually secondary to kidney disease, hyperthyroidism or endocrine disease. Groups: ACE inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers (telmisartan), calcium channel blockers (amlodipine, the first-line drug in cats), beta-blockers, alpha-blockers (prazosin, phenoxybenzamine), diuretics and vasodilators (hydralazine). Blood pressure measurement and treatment of the underlying disease are central. Sudden fall in pressure is to be avoided."],
    ["Principles in congestive heart failure", "Acute left-sided failure with pulmonary oedema: oxygen, diuretic, vasodilator, a positive inotrope, and sedation to ease stress, with minimal handling. Chronic management combines ACE inhibitors, pimobendan, spironolactone and diuretics, along with diet and exercise control. Drug choices vary by the cause: valve disease, cardiomyopathy, heartworm and so on. Details and doses are taught in the medicine subject."],
    ["Drugs harmful to the heart", "Anthracycline anticancer drugs (doxorubicin) cause cardiomyopathy. Halothane with catecholamines, xylazine in some arrhythmias, many antiarrhythmics, and ionophore feed additives (monensin) are toxic to the heart. Plants such as oleander, yew, and foxglove have cardiac glycosides. Bufo toad toxins act like digoxin. These are discussed under toxicology."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Group", "Examples", "Mechanism", "Notes"], r: [["Cardiac glycoside", "Digoxin", "Inhibits Na+/K+ ATPase, more calcium inside cell", "Narrow margin, kidney clearance, hypokalaemia raises toxicity"], ["Inodilator", "Pimobendan", "Calcium sensitiser + PDE III inhibitor", "Canine heart failure"], ["Class I antiarrhythmic", "Lidocaine, procainamide, quinidine", "Block sodium channels", "Ventricular arrhythmia"], ["Class II", "Propranolol, atenolol", "Beta block", "Tachyarrhythmias, cardiomyopathy"], ["Class III", "Amiodarone, sotalol", "Block potassium channels", "Prolong repolarisation"], ["Class IV", "Diltiazem, verapamil", "Block calcium channels", "Slow AV conduction"], ["Vasodilators / ACE inhibitors", "Enalapril, amlodipine, hydralazine", "Reduce preload and afterload", "Watch blood pressure and kidney"]] },
  df: ["Cardiotonic: a drug that increases the force of contraction of the heart muscle.", "Antiarrhythmic: a drug that prevents or corrects abnormal heart rhythm.", "Afterload: the resistance against which the ventricle ejects blood; preload is the filling volume."],
  tm: [["Inotropic", "Affecting the force of contraction"], ["Chronotropic", "Affecting heart rate"], ["Dromotropic", "Affecting conduction speed"], ["Proarrhythmia", "Arrhythmia caused by an antiarrhythmic drug"], ["Preload", "Ventricular filling volume"], ["Afterload", "Resistance to ejection"]],
  st: ["The heart fails to pump enough.", "The kidney retains salt and water and vessels tighten.", "ACE inhibitor and diuretic reduce the load.", "An inotrope strengthens the beat.", "An antiarrhythmic corrects the rhythm if needed.", "Response is monitored with examination, ECG and blood pressure."],
  ex: ["Pimobendan and an ACE inhibitor in a dog with valve disease.", "Amlodipine in a cat with hypertension.", "Lidocaine for ventricular arrhythmia during surgery."],
  k: ["Digoxin mechanism and toxicity.", "Vaughan Williams classes.", "Vasodilators and loads.", "Antihypertensives in cats.", "Pimobendan."],
  m: ["Digoxin toxicity is worsened by low potassium. Diltiazem and beta-blockers together can cause serious bradycardia. Plants like oleander contain cardiac glycosides."],
  sa: [["How does digoxin increase the force of the heartbeat?", "It inhibits the Na+/K+ ATPase, raising intracellular sodium and then calcium."], ["What is pimobendan?", "An inodilator that sensitises myofilaments to calcium and inhibits PDE III."], ["Which drug is first choice for feline hypertension?", "Amlodipine."]],
  la: [["Classify the antiarrhythmic drugs with examples.", "Vaughan Williams classes I to IV and others, mechanisms, uses, adverse effects."], ["Describe the drugs used in congestive heart failure.", "Digoxin, pimobendan, ACE inhibitors, diuretics, vasodilators, spironolactone, principles."]],
  q: [["Digoxin inhibits", ["Na+/K+ ATPase", "Calcium channel", "Beta receptor", "ACE"], 0, "Raises intracellular calcium."],
    ["Lidocaine is a Vaughan Williams class", ["IB", "II", "III", "IV"], 0, "Sodium channel blocker."],
    ["Amiodarone is class", ["III", "I", "II", "IV"], 0, "Potassium channel block."],
    ["Diltiazem is a", ["Calcium channel blocker", "Beta-blocker", "ACE inhibitor", "Diuretic"], 0, "Slows AV node."],
    ["Hypokalaemia makes digoxin toxicity", ["Worse", "Better", "Unchanged", "Absent"], 0, "Potassium and digoxin compete."]],
  f: [["Inotropic", "Force of contraction"], ["Chronotropic", "Heart rate"], ["Preload", "Filling"], ["Afterload", "Resistance"], ["Inodilator", "Inotrope plus vasodilator"]],
  d: ["Draw the Vaughan Williams table.", "Link ECG patterns to drug choice.", "Learn plants that contain cardiac glycosides."],
});

topic("pharm", "Haematopoietics, Coagulants and Anticoagulants", {
  o: ["Describe the haematinics: iron, copper, cobalt, vitamin B12 and folic acid", "Describe erythropoietin and other drugs for anaemia", "Describe the coagulants and haemostatics", "Describe the anticoagulants and antiplatelet drugs", "Describe thrombolytics and the control of bleeding in veterinary practice"],
  i: "The syllabus lists haematopoietics, coagulants and anticoagulants. Anaemia from parasites and poor nutrition is common in Indian livestock, and bleeding disorders such as warfarin rodenticide poisoning need quick understanding. The chapter covers mechanism and use, not doses.",
  s: "Blood needs raw materials to be made, and a working clotting system to stay in the vessels. Haematinics supply raw materials. Coagulants help clotting, and anticoagulants slow it down to prevent harmful clots. The balance is delicate: too little clotting causes bleeding, and too much causes thrombosis.",
  n: [
    ["Iron and its preparations", "Iron is essential for haemoglobin, myoglobin and enzymes. Ferrous salts (sulphate, fumarate, gluconate) are given orally, and iron dextran, gleptoferron and other forms parenterally. Suckling piglets are born with low stores and the milk is poor in iron, so injection of iron in the first days of life prevents anaemia. Overdose or fast injection can cause shock and tissue damage, and iron poisoning is serious. Gastric irritation limits oral use. Pigs, calves and anaemic animals are common targets."],
    ["Copper, cobalt and other trace minerals", "Copper is needed for iron use and haemoglobin synthesis, and its deficiency in ruminants gives anaemia, ill-thrift and changes of coat colour. Cobalt is used by rumen microbes to make vitamin B12, so a deficiency of cobalt gives a B12 deficiency in sheep and cattle with anaemia and wasting. Excess copper is toxic, especially to sheep, so supplementation must be carefully balanced. Molybdenum and sulphur interfere with copper absorption."],
    ["Vitamin B12 and folic acid", "Vitamin B12 (cyanocobalamin) and folic acid are needed for DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing cells, and deficiency leads to macrocytic anaemia. They are used in deficiency states and as general tonics. Intrinsic factor and bacterial synthesis play roles. Pyridoxine and other B vitamins support blood formation. Haematinic tonics are commonly used in practice, but the cause of anaemia must be investigated."],
    ["Erythropoietin and bone marrow stimulants", "Erythropoietin from the kidney drives red cell production, and recombinant forms are used for anaemia from kidney disease, but can lead to antibodies to the animal's own hormone in dogs and cats. Anabolic steroids and nandrolone have been used as marrow stimulants. Colony-stimulating factors (filgrastim) raise neutrophils. Blood transfusion remains the treatment for acute severe anaemia."],
    ["Local and systemic haemostatics", "Local agents stop bleeding at the surface: gauze, gelatin sponge, thrombin, adrenaline pledgets, silver nitrate, ferric chloride and alum (styptics), and vasoconstrictors. Systemic agents include vitamin K, tranexamic acid and aminocaproic acid (antifibrinolytics), and desmopressin in von Willebrand disease. Treatment of bleeding includes finding the cause, replacing losses and giving clotting factors by plasma or blood."],
    ["Vitamin K and the coagulation cascade", "Vitamin K is the cofactor of the liver enzyme that carboxylates clotting factors II, VII, IX and X (and proteins C and S), which makes them active. Deficiency or antagonism of vitamin K leads to bleeding. Vitamin K1 (phytonadione) is the clinical form and is used as the antidote for poisoning by anticoagulant rodenticides (warfarin, bromadiolone, brodifacoum) and dicoumarol in spoiled sweet clover. Second-generation rodenticides last a long time, so treatment must be prolonged."],
    ["Anticoagulants: heparin", "Heparin is a large sulphated polysaccharide found in mast cells. It enhances antithrombin III and so inhibits thrombin and factor Xa. It is given intravenously or subcutaneously, acts immediately, and does not cross the placenta. Low-molecular-weight heparins (enoxaparin, dalteparin) act mainly on Xa and have predictable action. Adverse effects are bleeding and thrombocytopenia, and protamine sulphate neutralises heparin by ionic combination. Heparin is used in thromboembolism, DIC and to keep catheters clear, and in vitro as an anticoagulant for blood samples."],
    ["Oral anticoagulants and in-vitro anticoagulants", "Warfarin and other coumarins block vitamin K epoxide reductase, so the factors are made in an inactive form, with a delay of days before the effect starts and ends. They are affected by many drugs and the diet. Rivaroxaban and related direct oral anticoagulants inhibit factor Xa directly. For blood collection, EDTA, citrate, oxalate and heparin are used, each suitable for particular tests: EDTA for haematology, citrate for coagulation tests, and fluoride-oxalate for glucose."],
    ["Antiplatelet drugs and thrombolytics", "Aspirin irreversibly acetylates platelet COX and prevents thromboxane A2 formation, and is used in cats at risk of thromboembolism in some cases. Clopidogrel blocks the platelet ADP receptor. Thrombolytics (streptokinase, urokinase, tissue plasminogen activator) convert plasminogen to plasmin, which dissolves fibrin, used in acute thrombosis in some patients, with a risk of bleeding. Antifibrinolytics oppose them."],
    ["Anticoagulant rodenticide poisoning as an example", "Rodenticides of the coumarin class cause delayed bleeding, in the gut, chest, joints and under the skin. Signs appear days after exposure because existing factors need to be used up. The pharmacology (vitamin K antagonism) explains the logic of the antidote, and fresh plasma replaces factors in severe cases. Secondary poisoning of pets and wildlife that eat poisoned rodents occurs. See the toxicology chapter."],
    ["Blood and plasma substitutes", "Whole blood, packed red cells, plasma and platelet products supply what the animal lacks. Cross-matching is advised, especially in repeated transfusion and in cats with their blood types. Dextran, hydroxyethyl starch and gelatins act as plasma expanders. These are also discussed in the fluid therapy chapter."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Need", "Drug or agent", "Mechanism", "Example of use"], r: [["Iron deficiency", "Iron dextran, ferrous sulphate", "Raw material for haemoglobin", "Piglet anaemia"], ["B12 deficiency", "Cyanocobalamin, cobalt salts", "DNA synthesis, rumen B12 formation", "Cobalt deficiency in sheep"], ["Clotting support", "Vitamin K1", "Activates factors II, VII, IX, X", "Rodenticide poisoning"], ["Anticoagulant (rapid)", "Heparin", "Enhances antithrombin III", "Thromboembolism, DIC"], ["Anticoagulant (oral)", "Warfarin", "Blocks vitamin K recycling", "Rarely used in animals"], ["Antiplatelet", "Aspirin, clopidogrel", "COX or ADP receptor block", "Feline thromboembolism"], ["Thrombolytic", "Streptokinase, tPA", "Converts plasminogen to plasmin", "Acute thrombosis"]] },
  df: ["Haematinic: a substance that increases haemoglobin or red blood cell formation.", "Anticoagulant: a drug that prevents blood clotting.", "Styptic: an agent that stops bleeding by contraction of tissue and vessels."],
  tm: [["Haemostatic", "Agent that stops bleeding"], ["Antifibrinolytic", "Drug that blocks fibrin breakdown"], ["Protamine sulphate", "Heparin antagonist"], ["EDTA", "Chelating anticoagulant for blood samples"], ["Macrocytic anaemia", "Anaemia with large red cells"], ["Plasmin", "Fibrin-dissolving enzyme"]],
  st: ["The type of anaemia or bleeding disorder is diagnosed.", "Haematinics are given for deficiency.", "Vitamin K or plasma are given for clotting factor loss.", "Heparin is used to prevent clots.", "Protamine can neutralise heparin.", "Blood tests monitor the response."],
  ex: ["Iron injection in a newborn piglet.", "Vitamin K1 in a dog poisoned by a rodenticide.", "Heparin in a horse with thromboembolic disease."],
  k: ["Iron preparations and cautions.", "Cobalt and B12.", "Vitamin K and clotting factors.", "Heparin vs warfarin.", "Antiplatelet drugs."],
  m: ["Heparin acts immediately but warfarin takes days. Excess copper is toxic to sheep. Vitamin K is the antidote for coumarin rodenticide, not heparin overdose."],
  sa: [["Why are piglets given iron?", "Sow milk is low in iron and piglets are born with small iron stores."], ["What is the antidote to heparin?", "Protamine sulphate."], ["How does warfarin act?", "It blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, so clotting factors are made inactive."]],
  la: [["Describe the haematinics used in veterinary practice.", "Iron, copper, cobalt, B12, folic acid, erythropoietin, uses, cautions."], ["Describe the anticoagulants and coagulants with their mechanisms.", "Vitamin K, heparin, warfarin, antiplatelet, thrombolytics, antidotes, in-vitro anticoagulants."]],
  q: [["Cobalt is needed for synthesis of", ["Vitamin B12", "Vitamin C", "Folic acid", "Vitamin D"], 0, "By rumen microbes."],
    ["Heparin enhances", ["Antithrombin III", "Vitamin K", "Plasmin", "Thrombin"], 0, "Inhibits thrombin and Xa."],
    ["EDTA is used for", ["Haematology samples", "Glucose tests", "Coagulation tests", "Serum collection"], 0, "Chelates calcium."],
    ["Warfarin acts by", ["Blocking vitamin K recycling", "Enhancing antithrombin", "Dissolving fibrin", "Blocking platelets"], 0, "Delayed onset."],
    ["Streptokinase", ["Activates plasminogen", "Inhibits thrombin", "Stops bleeding", "Raises iron"], 0, "Thrombolytic."]],
  f: [["Haematinic", "Blood-forming nutrient"], ["Heparin", "Rapid anticoagulant"], ["Vitamin K", "Clotting factor cofactor"], ["Protamine", "Heparin antagonist"], ["Thrombolytic", "Dissolves clots"]],
  d: ["Make a table of anticoagulant tubes.", "Learn nutritional anaemias in livestock.", "Study the rodenticide cascade."],
});

topic("pharm", "Respiratory Pharmacology: Expectorants, Antitussives, Bronchodilators and Mucolytics", {
  o: ["Describe the expectorants and their mechanisms", "Describe the antitussives: central and peripheral", "Describe bronchodilators and their classes", "Describe mucolytics and respiratory stimulants", "Explain the management concept in cough and airway disease in animals"],
  i: "The syllabus lists expectorants, antitussives, respiratory stimulants, bronchodilators and mucolytics. Respiratory disease is common in all species, and many of these drugs are used in the field. Their evidence is modest in some cases, so rational use is the lesson.",
  s: "Cough is a protective reflex that clears the airway. Sometimes it should be helped (productive cough) and sometimes suppressed (dry, painful cough). Bronchodilators open the airway, mucolytics thin the mucus, and expectorants help bring it up. Using the right drug for the right cough is the skill.",
  n: [
    ["Mucociliary clearance and cough", "Mucus traps particles and cilia move it up and out. Mucus can become thick in infection or allergy. Cough is triggered by receptors in the larynx, trachea and bronchi, through the vagus to the cough centre in the medulla. Treating the cause (infection, allergy, heart failure, lungworms, foreign body) is more important than suppressing the cough."],
    ["Expectorants", "Expectorants increase respiratory tract fluid or make mucus easier to cough up. Sedative expectorants act through gastric irritation and reflex increase in bronchial secretion: ammonium chloride, ammonium carbonate, ipecacuanha, squill. Stimulant expectorants: volatile oils (eucalyptus, turpentine, menthol, camphor) act directly on the bronchial glands as inhalations or as medicines. Potassium iodide and sodium iodide increase fluid in secretions and are used in respiratory and other conditions in large animals, with iodism as an adverse effect. Guaifenesin acts as an expectorant, with a mild central relaxant action."],
    ["Mucolytics", "Mucolytics break down mucus. Bromhexine and its metabolite ambroxol make secretions thinner and increase ciliary clearance, and are used in horses, dogs and cattle. Acetylcysteine breaks the disulphide bonds of mucus proteins and is also used as an antidote for paracetamol poisoning through glutathione restoration (mechanism). Dembrexine is used in horses. Steam inhalation and good hydration help all types."],
    ["Antitussives", "Central antitussives depress the cough centre: codeine, hydrocodone, butorphanol, and dextromethorphan. They are opioids with sedation, constipation and dependence potential, and are controlled drugs in some forms. Peripheral antitussives (demulcents such as syrup, glycerin, honey; local anaesthetics such as lidocaine sprays; benzonatate) reduce sensory input. Antitussives should not be used in productive cough or severe infection, since they trap secretions."],
    ["Bronchodilators: beta-2 agonists", "Salbutamol (albuterol), terbutaline, clenbuterol and salmeterol act on beta-2 receptors, increase cAMP and relax bronchial muscle. They are used as inhalers in cats and horses, with oral clenbuterol in horses. Adverse effects are tremor, tachycardia, restlessness and in food animals residue concerns. Tolerance can develop with continuous use."],
    ["Bronchodilators: methylxanthines and anticholinergics", "Theophylline and aminophylline inhibit phosphodiesterase and block adenosine, relaxing bronchial muscle and stimulating breathing and the heart, with a narrow margin of safety. Anticholinergics (atropine, ipratropium) block muscarinic bronchoconstriction and reduce secretions, and can thicken mucus. Both groups have a place in recurrent airway obstruction (heaves) in horses and feline asthma."],
    ["Corticosteroids and other anti-inflammatory drugs in the airway", "Inhaled and systemic corticosteroids are the mainstay for allergic airway disease (heaves, feline asthma), reducing inflammation and mucus. Mast cell stabilisers (sodium cromoglicate) prevent attacks. Leukotriene blockers have limited use in animals. Environmental control, such as dust-free feed and bedding, is as important as drugs."],
    ["Respiratory stimulants", "Doxapram stimulates the respiratory centre and is used in neonates and in some cases of anaesthetic depression. Caffeine and similar stimulants have been used. Oxygen and assisted ventilation are the main supports. Respiratory depressants (opioids, barbiturates) are reversed with specific antagonists in some cases."],
    ["Antimicrobials and other drugs in respiratory disease", "Bacterial pneumonia needs an antibiotic that reaches the lung (such as tetracyclines, macrolides, fluoroquinolones, florfenicol, tulathromycin in cattle), chosen according to likely pathogens. Lungworm requires anthelmintics. Nebulisation delivers drugs directly to the lungs. Combination cough mixtures with many ingredients are common in the market but often irrational."],
    ["Inhalation therapy and devices", "Metered dose inhalers with spacers and face masks, nebulisers, and steam inhalation (often with eucalyptus or tincture benzoin) are used in small animals and horses. Particle size matters for reaching the small airways. Humidification, mild exercise and clean air support all treatments."],
    ["Rational use summary", "Productive cough: expectorants, mucolytics and treating the cause. Dry cough: antitussives (not in infection). Bronchospasm: bronchodilators and anti-inflammatory drugs. Respiratory failure: oxygen, ventilation, and sometimes stimulants. Many traditional mixtures are low in evidence and should be used with discretion."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Group", "Examples", "Mechanism", "Use"], r: [["Expectorants", "Ammonium chloride, potassium iodide, guaifenesin", "Increase secretions or reflex stimulation", "Productive cough"], ["Mucolytics", "Bromhexine, ambroxol, acetylcysteine", "Thin mucus", "Thick secretions"], ["Central antitussives", "Codeine, butorphanol, dextromethorphan", "Depress cough centre", "Dry cough"], ["Beta-2 agonists", "Salbutamol, clenbuterol", "Relax bronchial muscle", "Bronchospasm"], ["Methylxanthines", "Theophylline, aminophylline", "PDE inhibition, adenosine block", "Bronchodilation, narrow margin"], ["Anticholinergics", "Atropine, ipratropium", "Block muscarinic constriction", "Heaves, reduce secretions"]] },
  df: ["Expectorant: a drug that promotes the expulsion of respiratory secretions.", "Antitussive: a drug that suppresses cough.", "Mucolytic: a drug that reduces the viscosity of mucus."],
  tm: [["Mucociliary escalator", "Ciliary movement of mucus out of the airway"], ["Demulcent", "Soothing film-forming substance"], ["Iodism", "Toxicity from excess iodide"], ["Nebulisation", "Delivery of a drug as a fine mist"], ["Heaves", "Recurrent airway obstruction in horses"], ["Bronchospasm", "Constriction of bronchial muscle"]],
  st: ["The type of cough and its cause are identified.", "Productive cough receives mucolytic or expectorant.", "Dry cough may need an antitussive.", "Bronchospasm is treated by a bronchodilator and anti-inflammatory.", "Infection gets a suitable antibiotic.", "Environment is improved."],
  ex: ["Clenbuterol for recurrent airway obstruction in horses.", "Bromhexine for thick mucus.", "Butorphanol as a cough suppressant in a dog with a dry cough."],
  k: ["Expectorant types.", "Antitussive cautions.", "Beta-2 agonists and methylxanthines.", "Mucolytics.", "Rational selection."],
  m: ["Antitussives are not for productive cough. Expectorants are not proven in all species. Clenbuterol has residue and misuse concerns."],
  sa: [["Name two mucolytics.", "Bromhexine and acetylcysteine (also ambroxol, dembrexine)."], ["Why are antitussives avoided in productive cough?", "They trap secretions and can worsen infection."], ["Name a beta-2 agonist used in horses.", "Clenbuterol."]],
  la: [["Describe the expectorants and mucolytics with their mechanisms.", "Sedative and stimulant expectorants, iodides, guaifenesin, bromhexine, acetylcysteine, uses."], ["Describe the bronchodilators and antitussives used in animals.", "Beta-2 agonists, xanthines, anticholinergics, central and peripheral antitussives, cautions."]],
  q: [["Bromhexine is a", ["Mucolytic", "Antitussive", "Bronchoconstrictor", "Emetic"], 0, "Thins secretions."],
    ["Codeine is a", ["Central antitussive", "Expectorant", "Mucolytic", "Bronchoconstrictor"], 0, "Opioid."],
    ["Iodism is caused by", ["Excess iodide", "Excess copper", "Excess zinc", "Excess sodium"], 0, "In expectorant use."],
    ["Salbutamol acts on", ["Beta-2 receptors", "Alpha-1 receptors", "Muscarinic receptors", "Opioid receptors"], 0, "Bronchodilation."],
    ["Heaves is", ["Recurrent airway obstruction in horses", "Heart disease in dogs", "Mastitis", "Bloat"], 0, "Allergic."]],
  f: [["Expectorant", "Eases mucus expulsion"], ["Antitussive", "Suppresses cough"], ["Mucolytic", "Thins mucus"], ["Bronchodilator", "Opens airways"], ["Iodism", "Iodide toxicity"]],
  d: ["Make a table of cough types and drugs.", "Study heaves treatment principles.", "Read about nebulisation."],
});

topic("pharm", "Urogenital Pharmacology: Diuretics, Urinary pH, Ecbolics and Tocolytics", {
  o: ["Classify diuretics and describe their sites and mechanisms of action", "Describe the use of urinary acidifiers and alkalinisers and ion trapping", "Describe drugs affecting the urinary tract and bladder", "Describe the ecbolics (oxytocic drugs) and their uses and cautions", "Describe tocolytics and other drugs for the female genital tract"],
  i: "The syllabus lists diuretics, drugs affecting urinary pH and tubular transport, ecbolics and tocolytics. Diuretics are used in heart failure, oedema and kidney disease, and ecbolics are used in parturition and uterine disorders. These need a solid understanding of the nephron and the uterus, both covered earlier.",
  s: "Diuretics make the kidney pass more salt and water, which removes oedema but can upset electrolytes. Changing the pH of urine can trap or free drugs and prevent stones. Ecbolics make the uterus contract, which helps expel the fetus, placenta or uterine contents, while tocolytics do the opposite.",
  n: [
    ["Classification of diuretics by site", "Proximal tubule: osmotic diuretics (mannitol), carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (acetazolamide). Loop of Henle: loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide) which block the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter. Distal tubule: thiazides (hydrochlorothiazide, chlorothiazide) which block the Na+/Cl- cotransporter. Collecting duct: potassium-sparing diuretics (spironolactone, an aldosterone antagonist; amiloride and triamterene, sodium channel blockers). Matching site to effect explains the whole group."],
    ["Loop diuretics: furosemide", "Furosemide is a powerful, quick, short-acting diuretic acting from the inside of the tubule, producing massive loss of sodium, chloride, potassium and water. It also dilates veins and reduces preload within minutes, which helps in pulmonary oedema. Adverse effects: dehydration, hypokalaemia, hypochloraemic alkalosis, ototoxicity in high doses, and kidney problems when volume is lost. In horses, it is used to treat bleeding from the lungs, though this is controversial, and in other cases for udder oedema in cows."],
    ["Thiazides, osmotics and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors", "Thiazides are moderate diuretics, also reducing urinary calcium loss, and are used for udder oedema, mild heart failure and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. Mannitol is freely filtered but not reabsorbed, holds water in the tubule, and also reduces brain oedema and intraocular pressure and protects the kidney in early acute failure when the vascular volume is adequate. Acetazolamide blocks carbonic anhydrase, reducing bicarbonate reabsorption (causing acidosis) and aqueous humour formation in glaucoma."],
    ["Potassium-sparing diuretics", "Spironolactone competes with aldosterone and reduces sodium reabsorption and potassium loss. It is a weak diuretic but valuable in heart failure and ascites with liver disease. The risk is hyperkalaemia, especially when combined with ACE inhibitors or in kidney disease."],
    ["Uses and cautions of diuretics", "Uses: pulmonary oedema, ascites, heart failure, udder and limb oedema, acute kidney failure (with fluids), hypercalcaemia (furosemide with saline), brain oedema (mannitol), and in poisoning to speed excretion by forced diuresis. Contraindicated in dehydration, severe electrolyte disturbance and urinary obstruction. Monitor hydration, electrolytes and kidney values."],
    ["Urinary pH and tubular transport", "Weak acids (such as aspirin, phenobarbital) are excreted faster in alkaline urine, and weak bases (amphetamine, quinidine) in acid urine. Alkalinisation with sodium bicarbonate promotes excretion of acids, and acidifiers (ammonium chloride, ascorbic acid, methionine) are used to dissolve struvite stones and in ruminants to prevent urolithiasis in feedlot animals. Probenecid blocks tubular secretion of organic acids and prolongs penicillin action. Excretory pathway interactions are important."],
    ["Drugs for the urinary tract", "Urinary antiseptics (methenamine, nitrofurantoin) act in the urine. Antibiotics are chosen on culture. Bladder tone is increased by bethanechol or decreased by anticholinergics and spasmolytics (flavoxate, oxybutynin). Phenoxybenzamine and prazosin relax the internal urethral sphincter. Phenylpropanolamine increases sphincter tone in urinary incontinence of spayed bitches. Allopurinol reduces uric acid in some stone disease. Urolith diets are part of treatment."],
    ["Ecbolics: oxytocin", "Oxytocin, from the posterior pituitary, contracts the uterus, especially at term and after parturition when receptors are plentiful, and causes milk let-down by contracting myoepithelial cells. It is used to improve uterine contractions in selected cases of weak labour when there is no obstruction, to hasten expulsion of retained placenta and uterine contents, in post-partum haemorrhage and agalactia. Contraindicated in obstructed delivery and malposition, because the uterus may rupture. It has a short half-life."],
    ["Ergot alkaloids and prostaglandins as ecbolics", "Ergometrine and ergonovine cause a sustained uterine contraction and are used to control post-partum bleeding. Prostaglandin F2 alpha and analogues (cloprostenol, dinoprost) cause luteolysis and contraction, used to treat pyometra, retained placenta, mummified fetus, to induce parturition or abortion and for oestrus synchronisation. Calcium and sparteine have been used in traditional practice. Uterine tone also depends on calcium and magnesium status."],
    ["Tocolytics (uterine relaxants)", "Beta-2 agonists (clenbuterol, isoxsuprine, salbutamol, terbutaline) relax the uterus and are used to delay labour, to facilitate obstetric manipulation or fetal correction, and in uterine prolapse in some cases. Other options: progesterone and its analogues for maintenance of pregnancy, atropine in some cases, epidural anaesthesia, and magnesium sulphate. Their benefit depends on the timing and the cause."],
    ["Other reproductive drugs", "Hormonal drugs (oestrogens, progestins, gonadotrophins, GnRH) are described with hormones in the next chapters. Antiprogestins and other agents are used in some species to induce abortion. Drugs for the male tract: testosterone and its analogues, GnRH and so on. Residues and the safety of handlers (pregnant women should not handle prostaglandins) are essential considerations."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Diuretic", "Site", "Mechanism", "Main adverse effect"], r: [["Mannitol", "Proximal tubule and loop", "Osmotic", "Volume overload, dehydration"], ["Acetazolamide", "Proximal tubule", "Carbonic anhydrase inhibition", "Metabolic acidosis"], ["Furosemide", "Thick ascending limb", "Blocks Na+/K+/2Cl- carrier", "Dehydration, hypokalaemia"], ["Thiazides", "Distal tubule", "Block Na+/Cl- carrier", "Hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia"], ["Spironolactone", "Collecting duct", "Aldosterone antagonist", "Hyperkalaemia"]] },
  df: ["Diuretic: a drug that increases the volume of urine by promoting loss of salt and water.", "Ecbolic: a drug that stimulates uterine contraction to expel its contents.", "Tocolytic: a drug that relaxes the uterus and inhibits uterine contractions."],
  tm: [["Natriuresis", "Excretion of sodium in urine"], ["Oxytocic", "Drug that stimulates uterine contraction"], ["Luteolysis", "Regression of the corpus luteum"], ["Ion trapping", "Concentration of a drug in a compartment by pH"], ["Urolithiasis", "Stone formation in the urinary tract"], ["Milk let-down", "Ejection of milk by oxytocin"]],
  st: ["Kidney or uterine condition is identified.", "Drug class is chosen by site of action.", "Diuretic removes salt and water, or ecbolic contracts uterus.", "Contraindications (obstruction, dehydration) are checked.", "Electrolytes or progress of labour are monitored.", "Treatment is stopped when the aim is reached."],
  ex: ["Furosemide in a dog with pulmonary oedema.", "Oxytocin after normal delivery for retained placenta.", "Prostaglandin F2 alpha in a cow with a persistent corpus luteum."],
  k: ["Diuretics by site.", "Furosemide effects.", "Urinary pH and excretion.", "Oxytocin uses and contraindications.", "Tocolytics."],
  m: ["Oxytocin must not be used in obstructive dystocia. Spironolactone is weak but potassium-sparing. Prostaglandins are hazardous to pregnant women handling them."],
  sa: [["Where do loop diuretics act?", "At the thick ascending limb, blocking the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter."], ["What is an ecbolic?", "A drug that stimulates uterine contraction."], ["Name a tocolytic.", "Clenbuterol (or isoxsuprine, terbutaline)."]],
  la: [["Classify diuretics and describe the actions and uses of furosemide.", "Sites, classes, mechanisms, uses, adverse effects, contraindications."], ["Describe the ecbolics and tocolytics used in animals.", "Oxytocin, ergot alkaloids, prostaglandins, beta-agonists, uses, cautions."]],
  q: [["Furosemide blocks the", ["Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter", "Na+/Cl- cotransporter", "Aldosterone receptor", "Carbonic anhydrase"], 0, "Loop of Henle."],
    ["Spironolactone causes", ["Hyperkalaemia risk", "Hypokalaemia", "Hypocalcaemia", "Hypoglycaemia"], 0, "Potassium sparing."],
    ["Oxytocin is contraindicated in", ["Obstructive dystocia", "Retained placenta", "Agalactia", "Post-partum bleeding"], 0, "Risk of rupture."],
    ["Mannitol is a", ["Osmotic diuretic", "Loop diuretic", "Thiazide", "Aldosterone antagonist"], 0, "Not reabsorbed."],
    ["Clenbuterol is used as a", ["Tocolytic", "Ecbolic", "Diuretic", "Purgative"], 0, "Beta-2 agonist."]],
  f: [["Diuretic", "Increases urine output"], ["Ecbolic", "Contracts uterus"], ["Tocolytic", "Relaxes uterus"], ["Natriuresis", "Sodium loss in urine"], ["Oxytocin", "Uterine contraction and milk let-down"]],
  d: ["Draw the nephron with diuretic sites.", "Learn indications of each ecbolic.", "Make a table of urinary pH changes."],
});

topic("pharm", "Pharmacological Basis of Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid-Base Therapy", {
  o: ["Describe the body fluid compartments in relation to fluid therapy", "Describe the crystalloids and colloids and their composition", "Describe the routes of administering fluids and the rule of caution", "Describe the electrolyte supplements and the correction of acid-base imbalance", "Describe oral rehydration solutions and the principles for ruminants and neonates"],
  i: "The syllabus lists fluid therapy as a unit of its own. Many sick animals are dehydrated or have electrolyte disturbances, and fluids are the first and often most life-saving treatment. The medicine subject explains clinical assessment; this chapter gives the drug and solution basis. Rates and volumes are given in clinical subjects.",
  s: "Fluid therapy means giving water, salts, sugar or protein-like solutions to replace what the body has lost or cannot take in. The right fluid depends on what was lost: pure water, salt water, blood, or acid and base. Choosing wrongly can do harm, so the composition of each solution should be known.",
  n: [
    ["Fluid compartments and losses", "Total body water is about 60 percent of body weight, two-thirds inside cells and one-third outside (plasma and interstitial fluid). Dehydration is loss of water, hypovolaemia loss of circulating volume, and shock a failure of perfusion. Losses occur through vomiting, diarrhoea, polyuria, haemorrhage, burns and sweat. The sodium content of extracellular fluid determines the distribution of water."],
    ["Crystalloids", "Crystalloids are solutions of salts and sugars that pass freely across vessel walls. Isotonic solutions: 0.9 percent sodium chloride (normal saline), Ringer's solution, lactated Ringer's (Hartmann's) solution and other balanced solutions. Normal saline is acidifying in large volumes, and lactated Ringer's is mildly alkalinising as lactate is converted to bicarbonate by the liver. Hypotonic solutions (dextrose 5 percent, which becomes free water as the sugar is used; half-strength saline) hydrate cells. Hypertonic saline (7 percent) rapidly raises plasma volume in shock for a short period."],
    ["Colloids and blood products", "Colloids contain large molecules that stay in the vessels and hold water by oncotic pressure: dextran, hydroxyethyl starch, gelatin, plasma and albumin. They expand the plasma volume with less fluid than crystalloids, but have risks (coagulation effects, kidney injury, allergy). Whole blood or packed red cells replace oxygen carriers when the anaemia is acute. Selection is by the cause of loss."],
    ["Dextrose and nutritional fluids", "Dextrose solutions (5 percent isotonic, 25 or 50 percent hypertonic) supply calories and treat hypoglycaemia (as in ketosis, pregnancy toxaemia, neonatal and hunting dog hypoglycaemia). Strong dextrose is irritant to veins and must be given slowly into a large vein. Amino acid and lipid solutions are used in parenteral nutrition of critical patients. Insulin must be considered when dextrose is given repeatedly."],
    ["Electrolyte supplements", "Potassium chloride is given in hypokalaemia, always diluted and slowly, since fast intravenous potassium can stop the heart. Calcium borogluconate is used for hypocalcaemia (milk fever, eclampsia), given slowly with heart monitoring as fast injection can cause cardiac arrest. Magnesium solutions treat grass tetany. Sodium bicarbonate treats acidosis, and sodium chloride corrects hyponatraemia, in controlled ways. Phosphate solutions treat hypophosphataemia in some cases."],
    ["Acid-base correction", "Metabolic acidosis needs the cause treated, with sodium bicarbonate or lactate-containing fluids in severe cases. Metabolic alkalosis (vomiting, abomasal displacement) is treated with saline and potassium. Respiratory problems are treated by improving ventilation. Overcorrection can lead to alkalosis, hypokalaemia and paradoxical cerebral acidosis. Blood gas results guide therapy where available."],
    ["Routes of fluid administration", "Intravenous is fastest and standard in emergencies. Subcutaneous is for mild dehydration in small animals and young. Intraperitoneal in small animals and neonates when veins are unavailable. Intraosseous in neonates and small exotic animals. Oral for mild dehydration and in diarrhoea of calves, piglets, kittens and others. Fluids should be warmed, given aseptically, and at a rate suited to the condition and species."],
    ["Oral rehydration solutions", "These contain sodium, glucose, potassium and a base (bicarbonate, acetate or citrate). Glucose helps absorb sodium and water in the gut (sodium-glucose co-transport), even in diarrhoea. They are used for calf scours, piglet diarrhoea and in many other cases and form the main field treatment in India. The formula and water amount must be correct: too strong a solution can cause hypernatraemia. Milk feeding should usually not be stopped."],
    ["Monitoring and hazards", "Overload with fluid can cause pulmonary oedema, particularly in heart and kidney disease, and in neonates. Check hydration, urine output, packed cell volume, total protein, electrolytes, and body weight, and watch for swelling of the eyelids and lungs sounds. Phlebitis, infection, air embolism and extravasation are risks of intravenous therapy."],
    ["Ruminants and neonates", "Adult ruminants may lose large volumes into the rumen or abomasum, so fluids are given by the intravenous route in large amounts or by rumen tube (oral rehydration in the rumen is effective for mild loss). Calves with diarrhoea have metabolic acidosis, so alkalinising fluids are needed, and mildly hypertonic solutions are used with care. Neonates are small, lose heat easily and have limited glycogen, so warm, dextrose-containing fluids are used."],
    ["Fluid therapy planning (the logic)", "The plan has three parts: replace the deficit, supply daily maintenance needs and replace continuing losses, each worked out from clinical signs and body weight. The type of fluid is matched to what is lost. The actual volumes and rates are in the clinical subject, and they depend on species, age and disease."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Fluid", "Type", "Effect", "Use / caution"], r: [["0.9% saline", "Isotonic crystalloid", "Expands extracellular fluid", "Large volumes acidify"], ["Lactated Ringer's", "Balanced isotonic", "Replaces fluid and mild alkalinising", "Avoid in severe liver failure"], ["5% dextrose", "Hypotonic, calories", "Hydrates cells", "Not a volume expander"], ["7% hypertonic saline", "Hypertonic", "Rapid plasma volume expansion", "Short acting, follow with isotonic fluid"], ["Hydroxyethyl starch, dextran", "Colloid", "Holds water in vessels", "Risk to kidney and clotting"], ["Oral rehydration solution", "Oral electrolyte with glucose", "Corrects mild to moderate dehydration", "Correct mixing needed"]] },
  df: ["Crystalloid: a solution of small solute molecules that equilibrates across the vessel wall.", "Colloid: a solution containing large molecules that remain in the circulation and exert oncotic pressure.", "Oral rehydration solution: a glucose-electrolyte solution that uses sodium-glucose transport to restore fluid by mouth."],
  tm: [["Isotonic", "Same osmolality as plasma"], ["Hypertonic", "Higher osmolality than plasma"], ["Oncotic pressure", "Pressure from plasma proteins and colloids"], ["Maintenance fluid", "Fluid needed daily to keep balance"], ["Extravasation", "Leakage of fluid into tissue outside the vein"], ["Hypovolaemia", "Low circulating blood volume"]],
  st: ["Dehydration and its cause are assessed.", "The type of fluid is chosen.", "The route is chosen.", "Deficit, maintenance and ongoing losses are replaced.", "Electrolytes and acid-base are corrected.", "Response is monitored and fluids tapered."],
  ex: ["Oral rehydration solution for a calf with diarrhoea.", "Calcium borogluconate slowly in a cow with milk fever.", "Lactated Ringer's in a dog with vomiting."],
  k: ["Crystalloids and colloids.", "Dextrose uses.", "Potassium and calcium safety.", "Oral rehydration solution.", "Fluid overload."],
  m: ["Dextrose 5 percent is not a volume expander. Potassium must not be given as a rapid injection. Calcium is given slowly with the heart monitored."],
  sa: [["Why does oral rehydration solution contain glucose?", "Glucose helps absorb sodium and water through sodium-glucose co-transport."], ["Name two crystalloids.", "Normal saline and lactated Ringer's solution."], ["What is a colloid?", "A solution with large molecules which stay in vessels and hold water by oncotic pressure."]],
  la: [["Describe the fluids used in fluid therapy and their indications.", "Crystalloids, colloids, dextrose, hypertonic saline, electrolytes, acid-base, blood products."], ["Describe the oral rehydration therapy and the precautions of fluid therapy.", "Composition, mechanism, uses in calves, overload, monitoring, rate of potassium and calcium."]],
  q: [["Lactated Ringer's is", ["A balanced isotonic crystalloid", "A colloid", "A hypertonic solution", "A blood product"], 0, "Contains lactate."],
    ["Fast intravenous potassium can", ["Stop the heart", "Raise sugar", "Cause bleeding", "Dissolve clots"], 0, "Hyperkalaemic arrest."],
    ["Hypertonic saline is", ["Short acting volume expander", "Oral therapy", "A colloid", "A nutrient"], 0, "Needs isotonic follow-up."],
    ["Dextrose 5 percent is effectively", ["Free water", "Isotonic saline", "A colloid", "A buffer"], 0, "Glucose is used up."],
    ["Calcium borogluconate treats", ["Milk fever", "Ketosis", "Bloat", "Acidosis"], 0, "Hypocalcaemia."]],
  f: [["Crystalloid", "Small solute fluid"], ["Colloid", "Large molecule fluid"], ["ORS", "Oral rehydration solution"], ["Isotonic", "Same osmolality as plasma"], ["Maintenance", "Daily needs"]],
  d: ["Make a table of fluids and their composition.", "Study fluid overload signs.", "Learn the ORS formula from your textbook."],
});

topic("pharm", "Hormones in Pharmacotherapy: Pituitary, Thyroid, Adrenal, Pancreatic and Sex Hormones", {
  o: ["Describe the drugs related to the hypothalamic and pituitary hormones", "Describe thyroid hormones and antithyroid drugs", "Describe the corticosteroids and their therapeutic uses and hazards", "Describe insulin and the oral antidiabetic drugs", "Describe sex hormones, anabolic agents and drugs for reproductive control"],
  i: "The syllabus includes hormones and hormone antagonists. Corticosteroids are among the most used and most abused drugs in practice, and endocrine diseases such as diabetes and hypothyroidism are common in dogs. This chapter is about mechanisms, drug groups and cautions. Hormone physiology is in the earlier subjects.",
  s: "Hormone drugs either replace a missing hormone, block an overactive gland, or use a hormone's strong effects for a different purpose (corticosteroids for inflammation). Because hormones control everything from sugar to pregnancy, their drug use needs care: effects are widespread and some are long lasting.",
  n: [
    ["Hypothalamic and pituitary drugs", "GnRH and its analogues (buserelin, gonadorelin, deslorelin) stimulate release of LH and FSH, used for ovulation induction, cystic ovarian disease and suppression with long-acting implants. Gonadotrophins (eCG or PMSG, hCG, FSH) are used for superovulation and induction of oestrus. Oxytocin and vasopressin (ADH) and its analogue desmopressin are used for diabetes insipidus. Growth hormone (somatotropin, bovine somatotropin) increases milk yield in cows in some countries and is controversial. Dopamine agonists (cabergoline) suppress prolactin."],
    ["Thyroid hormones and antithyroid drugs", "Levothyroxine replaces thyroid hormone in hypothyroidism in dogs, and is monitored by blood levels. Antithyroid drugs (methimazole or carbimazole, propylthiouracil) block thyroid peroxidase and hormone synthesis, used for feline hyperthyroidism. Propylthiouracil is toxic to cats in some ways. Radioiodine is also used for definitive treatment in cats. Iodides in excess cause goitre (Wolff-Chaikoff effect). Goitrogenic plants such as cabbage and cassava interfere with iodine use."],
    ["Corticosteroids: actions", "Glucocorticoids (cortisol, prednisolone, dexamethasone, betamethasone, triamcinolone) bind intracellular receptors and change gene expression. They are strongly anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive, increase gluconeogenesis and blood glucose, break down protein, redistribute fat, and cause sodium retention to a limited degree. Prednisolone is intermediate acting, and dexamethasone is long acting, with high potency and little mineralocorticoid action. Mineralocorticoids (fludrocortisone, desoxycortone) replace aldosterone in Addison's disease."],
    ["Corticosteroids: uses", "Allergic and inflammatory skin disease, immune-mediated conditions (haemolytic anaemia, pemphigus), shock in some cases, spinal injuries in some settings, as an adjunct to cancer chemotherapy (lymphoma), to induce parturition or in ketosis in cattle, and replacement therapy in adrenal insufficiency. Dexamethasone is used for induction of parturition in cattle. They should not be used for fever of unknown cause without reason since they mask disease."],
    ["Corticosteroids: adverse effects", "Immunosuppression with infection, delayed wound healing, gastric ulcers (especially combined with NSAIDs), muscle wasting, polyuria, polydipsia and polyphagia, iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome and adrenal suppression with abrupt stop, diabetes mellitus, laminitis in horses, abortion in late pregnancy of cattle, and hepatopathy in dogs. Alternate-day dosing, lowest effective dose and gradual withdrawal reduce the risk. They are not for routine use as general tonics."],
    ["Insulin and antidiabetic drugs", "Insulin of regular, intermediate and long-acting types, and analogues, treats diabetes mellitus in dogs and cats. The risk is hypoglycaemia, with weakness, seizures and collapse, and the need for consistent feeding. Oral drugs in cats (glipizide, a sulphonylurea) have limited use, and metformin and others are used in humans. Glucagon is a hormone that raises glucose in hypoglycaemia. In cows, insulin and glucose treat ketosis in some regimes."],
    ["Oestrogens and progestogens", "Oestrogens (oestradiol, diethylstilbestrol, ethinyl estradiol) were used for pyometra and misalliance, and have serious toxic effects: bone marrow suppression in dogs, uterine infection and so on. Progestogens (progesterone, medroxyprogesterone, megestrol, altrenogest) maintain pregnancy, control oestrus and are used for behaviour in some cases, with a risk of pyometra and diabetes in dogs. Anti-oestrogens and prostaglandins are discussed elsewhere. Residues of hormones in animal products are a public health matter."],
    ["Androgens and anabolic steroids", "Testosterone and its esters, nandrolone and stanozolol have anabolic and androgenic effects. They are used in debilitated animals and anaemia, but have abuse potential in racing and as growth promoters in livestock, where they are banned or controlled in many countries. Anti-androgens and 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors treat prostatic enlargement in dogs. Misuse in sport horses and greyhounds is a doping issue."],
    ["Hormonal control of reproduction in practice", "Prostaglandins and progestogens control the oestrous cycle for synchronisation, GnRH induces ovulation, gonadotrophins cause superovulation for embryo transfer, and oxytocin supports parturition and milk let-down. Oestrus induction in anoestrus animals, treatment of cystic ovaries, and fixed-time artificial insemination programmes rely on these combinations. The protocols are in the gynaecology subject."],
    ["Hormones in animal production and the public health view", "Hormonal growth promoters, anabolic steroids and some beta-agonists have been used to improve growth and carcass quality. They are banned in the European Union and are limited in many other regions, due to residue risks. In India, rules govern the use of hormones, and oxytocin misuse in dairy animals to increase milk letdown has been restricted. A veterinarian has to follow the laws and ethical standards."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Hormone group", "Examples", "Main use (concept)", "Main hazard"], r: [["GnRH and gonadotrophins", "Buserelin, eCG, hCG", "Ovulation and oestrus control", "Cystic ovaries, hormonal imbalance"], ["Thyroid hormone / antithyroid", "Levothyroxine / methimazole", "Hypothyroidism / hyperthyroidism", "Over-treatment, cat side effects"], ["Glucocorticoids", "Prednisolone, dexamethasone", "Inflammation, allergy, immune disease", "Immunosuppression, ulcers, Cushing-like state"], ["Mineralocorticoids", "Fludrocortisone", "Addison's disease", "Hypertension, hypokalaemia"], ["Insulin", "Regular, NPH, long-acting", "Diabetes mellitus", "Hypoglycaemia"], ["Sex steroids", "Oestradiol, progesterone, testosterone", "Reproduction control, anabolic use", "Pyometra, marrow toxicity, abuse"]] },
  df: ["Corticosteroid: a steroid hormone of the adrenal cortex or its synthetic analogue.", "Iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome: the signs of excess glucocorticoid caused by drug treatment.", "Anabolic steroid: a synthetic androgen with tissue-building effects."],
  tm: [["Glucocorticoid", "Steroid affecting metabolism and inflammation"], ["Mineralocorticoid", "Steroid affecting sodium and potassium balance"], ["Adrenal suppression", "Loss of adrenal function after long steroid use"], ["Goitrogen", "Substance that causes goitre"], ["Superovulation", "Stimulated release of many ova"], ["Doping", "Illegal use of drugs to affect performance"]],
  st: ["The hormone disorder is diagnosed.", "Replacement or blocking drug is chosen.", "The lowest effective dose is used.", "Blood tests and clinical signs are followed.", "Treatment is adjusted or tapered.", "Adverse effects and residues are considered."],
  ex: ["Levothyroxine for a hypothyroid dog.", "Prednisolone for allergic dermatitis, with the lowest dose and gradual withdrawal.", "Insulin for a diabetic dog, with regular feeding."],
  k: ["Glucocorticoid effects and adverse effects.", "Antithyroid drugs.", "Insulin and hypoglycaemia.", "Sex steroid uses and risks.", "Hormone use in production and its rules."],
  m: ["Dexamethasone has little mineralocorticoid action. Abrupt withdrawal of long-term steroids can be dangerous. Oestrogens are not safe in dogs for pyometra prevention."],
  sa: [["Name two adverse effects of long term corticosteroids.", "Immunosuppression with infection, and gastric ulcers (also muscle wasting and Cushing-like signs)."], ["Which drug is used for feline hyperthyroidism?", "Methimazole (or carbimazole)."], ["What is the main risk of insulin?", "Hypoglycaemia."]],
  la: [["Describe the corticosteroids with their actions, uses and adverse effects.", "Glucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids, mechanism, uses, hazards, withdrawal."], ["Describe the sex hormones and the drugs used to control reproduction.", "Oestrogens, progestogens, androgens, GnRH, gonadotrophins, prostaglandins, risks, rules."]],
  q: [["Methimazole inhibits", ["Thyroid peroxidase", "Insulin release", "ACE", "Aldosterone"], 0, "Blocks hormone synthesis."],
    ["Dexamethasone is", ["Long acting glucocorticoid", "Mineralocorticoid", "Anabolic steroid", "Oestrogen"], 0, "Potent."],
    ["Hypoglycaemia is the main risk of", ["Insulin", "Prednisolone", "Levothyroxine", "Oxytocin"], 0, "Too much insulin."],
    ["Anabolic steroids use in racing is", ["Doping", "Routine tonic", "Safe", "Legal everywhere"], 0, "Prohibited."],
    ["Fludrocortisone is a", ["Mineralocorticoid", "Glucocorticoid", "Androgen", "Thyroid drug"], 0, "For Addison's disease."]],
  f: [["Glucocorticoid", "Anti-inflammatory steroid"], ["Methimazole", "Antithyroid drug"], ["Insulin", "Antidiabetic hormone"], ["Progestogen", "Maintains pregnancy"], ["Anabolic steroid", "Tissue building androgen"]],
  d: ["Make a table of corticosteroids by potency.", "Learn the endocrine diseases of dogs and cats.", "Check the rules on hormone use in India."],
});

topic("pharm", "Drugs for the Skin and Mucous Membranes: Emollients, Astringents, Irritants and Others", {
  o: ["Describe the dermatological vehicles and bases", "Describe emollients, demulcents, protectives and adsorbents", "Describe astringents, irritants, counter-irritants and caustics", "Describe keratolytics, antiseborrhoeics, antipruritics and keratoplastics", "Describe the drugs used in the eye, ear and mucous membranes in outline"],
  i: "The syllabus lists drugs acting on skin and mucous membranes. Skin disease is the commonest complaint in small animal clinics, and ointments and lotions are the daily tools of the village veterinarian. The topic ties in with the practical syllabus on compounding of ointments, liniments and lotions.",
  s: "The skin is a barrier, so a drug put on it must reach the right layer and stay there. The base or vehicle (cream, ointment, lotion) is as important as the active ingredient. Some drugs soothe, some shrink and dry tissue, some irritate on purpose to bring blood to the part, and some destroy abnormal tissue.",
  n: [
    ["Vehicles and bases", "Ointments are semi-solid greasy preparations (petrolatum, lanolin, wax) which are occlusive and hydrate the skin. Creams are emulsions of oil and water that are less greasy. Pastes contain a high proportion of powder, are stiff and protect the area. Lotions are liquids applied without much rubbing, and liniments are rubbed into the skin with an oily or alcoholic vehicle. Gels, powders, shampoos, sprays and pour-ons are other forms. The choice depends on the stage of the lesion: wet lesions need drying lotions or powders, dry scaly ones need ointments."],
    ["Emollients, demulcents and protectives", "Emollients (lanolin, petrolatum, liquid paraffin, vegetable oils, glycerin) soften and soothe the skin. Demulcents are soothing film-forming substances for mucous membranes (gum acacia, mucilage, starch, methylcellulose). Protectives (zinc oxide, talc, calamine, kaolin, bismuth) form a physical barrier over inflamed skin. Adsorbents such as activated charcoal and kaolin take up toxins and irritants."],
    ["Astringents", "Astringents precipitate protein on the surface of tissue, shrink it, reduce discharge and harden the skin. Examples: tannic acid and tannin-containing plants (catechu, oak bark), salts of metals (zinc sulphate, silver nitrate, copper sulphate, alum, lead acetate), and potassium permanganate solution. Used in wounds with discharge, hoof care, mild diarrhoea (internally) and to stop minor bleeding as styptics. Concentrated solutions are caustic."],
    ["Irritants and counter-irritants", "Irritants cause local reddening and inflammation. Counter-irritants are applied over an inflamed deeper structure to relieve its pain by causing a milder surface inflammation (rubefacients such as turpentine oil, camphor, methyl salicylate, menthol, mustard, capsicum; vesicants such as cantharides and mercuric iodide that cause blisters). They are used in traditional practice for sprains, tendon and joint problems, and muscle pain, with the effect depending on the species, area and care. Overuse may cause blistering, loss of hair, or scarring. They should not be used on open wounds."],
    ["Caustics and escharotics", "Caustics destroy tissue by chemical action. Silver nitrate (lunar caustic), copper sulphate, zinc chloride, phenol, trichloroacetic acid and potassium hydroxide are used to remove warts, proud flesh (excess granulation tissue), foot rot lesions and to cauterise small bleeding points. Concentrated acids and alkalis must be handled with care, protecting surrounding skin."],
    ["Keratolytics, keratoplastics and antiseborrhoeics", "Keratolytics soften and remove the surface layer (salicylic acid, sulphur, resorcinol, urea). Keratoplastics encourage normal keratinisation (coal tar, sulphur and salicylic acid in low strengths). Antiseborrhoeic shampoos (selenium sulphide, tar, benzoyl peroxide, sulphur and salicylic acid) treat greasy or scaly seborrhoea in dogs. Some are irritant, drying or staining, and should not be used in cats where licking leads to ingestion of toxic drugs such as tar and salicylates."],
    ["Antipruritics and anti-inflammatory topical drugs", "Itching is relieved by topical corticosteroids, calamine, menthol and camphor (in low strengths), and local anaesthetics (not for long use). Systemic antihistamines and the newer drugs (oclacitinib, lokivetmab for dogs) help in allergic dermatitis. Topical steroids are absorbed and may cause systemic effects and skin thinning with prolonged use."],
    ["Topical antimicrobials and parasiticides", "Antibacterial: chlorhexidine, povidone iodine, silver sulphadiazine, mupirocin, fusidic acid, neomycin and bacitracin in ointment. Antifungal: miconazole, clotrimazole, ketoconazole, enilconazole, lime sulphur dips, natamycin. Ectoparasiticides: benzyl benzoate, amitraz, synthetic pyrethroids, organophosphate and ivermectin forms, sulphur and others (see the chemotherapy chapters). Many animals lick their skin, so ingestion is a consideration."],
    ["Wound healing agents", "Wound care aims at a clean, moist, protected bed. Cleansing with saline or dilute antiseptic, removal of dead tissue, use of non-toxic agents, and avoidance of strong antiseptics which kill fibroblasts. Honey, aloe vera, sugar and tannin products are used in practice and have some evidence. Maggot wounds (myiasis) need larvicidal agents and debridement. Details in the surgery subject."],
    ["Eye, ear and mucous membrane preparations (outline)", "Ophthalmic drugs: topical antibiotics, antivirals, mydriatics (atropine, tropicamide), miotics (pilocarpine), anti-inflammatory drugs (corticosteroids are avoided in corneal ulcers), tear substitutes and local anaesthetics. Otic preparations: ceruminolytics, antibiotic-antifungal-steroid combinations, and ear cleansers, with care when the eardrum is ruptured. Mucous membrane use: mouth washes, vaginal and uterine preparations, and nasal drops. Sterility and correct vehicle matter."],
    ["Safety and practical points", "Wear gloves when using topical drugs, avoid absorption through damaged skin, and watch for allergy. Use an Elizabethan collar to prevent licking. Withhold milk or meat according to the label for products that can be absorbed. Treatment of wet lesions by dry preparations and of dry lesions by moist ones is the basic rule."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Group", "Examples", "Action", "Use"], r: [["Emollient", "Lanolin, petrolatum, liquid paraffin", "Softens and soothes", "Dry skin"], ["Protective", "Zinc oxide, calamine, talc", "Physical barrier", "Raw inflamed skin"], ["Astringent", "Tannic acid, zinc sulphate, alum, silver nitrate (dilute)", "Precipitates protein, reduces discharge", "Wet wounds, hoof care"], ["Rubefacient / counter-irritant", "Turpentine, camphor, methyl salicylate", "Superficial irritation, relief of deep pain", "Sprains, muscle pain"], ["Caustic", "Silver nitrate, copper sulphate, phenol", "Destroys tissue", "Warts, proud flesh"], ["Keratolytic", "Salicylic acid, sulphur", "Removes surface layer", "Seborrhoea, scaling"]] },
  df: ["Astringent: a substance that precipitates protein and shrinks tissue, reducing discharge.", "Counter-irritant: a substance that produces superficial irritation to relieve deeper pain or inflammation.", "Emollient: a soothing agent that softens and protects the skin."],
  tm: [["Rubefacient", "Agent that reddens the skin"], ["Vesicant", "Agent that causes blisters"], ["Escharotic", "Caustic that forms a scab"], ["Keratolytic", "Agent that softens keratin"], ["Liniment", "Preparation rubbed into the skin"], ["Ceruminolytic", "Agent that dissolves ear wax"]],
  st: ["The lesion type (wet or dry) is identified.", "A suitable base is selected.", "The active drug is matched to the aim.", "The area is cleaned and the drug applied.", "The animal is prevented from licking.", "Progress is checked and the drug changed."],
  ex: ["Zinc oxide paste on raw inflamed skin.", "Salicylic acid shampoo for seborrhoea in a dog.", "A counter-irritant liniment on a horse's strained tendon, used with care."],
  k: ["Vehicles and bases.", "Astringents.", "Counter-irritants and vesicants.", "Caustics.", "Keratolytics and shampoos."],
  m: ["Strong antiseptics delay wound healing. Salicylates and tars are risky in cats. Counter-irritants should not be used on open wounds."],
  sa: [["Define an astringent.", "A substance that precipitates protein and shrinks tissue, reducing discharge."], ["Give two rubefacients.", "Turpentine oil, camphor (also methyl salicylate, menthol)."], ["Which base suits a wet lesion?", "A drying lotion or powder, not a greasy ointment."]],
  la: [["Describe the drugs acting on the skin with suitable examples.", "Bases, emollients, protectives, astringents, irritants, caustics, keratolytics, antipruritics."], ["Describe the counter-irritants and caustics used in veterinary practice.", "Rubefacients, vesicants, caustics, examples, uses, precautions."]],
  q: [["Zinc oxide is a", ["Protective", "Caustic", "Vesicant", "Keratolytic"], 0, "Barrier."],
    ["Silver nitrate is used as a", ["Caustic and astringent", "Emollient", "Demulcent", "Rubefacient"], 0, "Depending on strength."],
    ["Salicylic acid is a", ["Keratolytic", "Emollient", "Demulcent", "Vasoconstrictor"], 0, "Removes scales."],
    ["Cantharides is a", ["Vesicant", "Emollient", "Astringent", "Protective"], 0, "Causes blisters."],
    ["Wet lesions need", ["Drying lotions or powders", "Greasy ointments", "Oils", "Poultices only"], 0, "Basic rule."]],
  f: [["Emollient", "Softens skin"], ["Astringent", "Shrinks tissue"], ["Rubefacient", "Reddens skin"], ["Caustic", "Destroys tissue"], ["Keratolytic", "Removes keratin"]],
  d: ["Practise compounding of ointments and lotions.", "Make a table of bases.", "Learn the skin drugs hazardous to cats."],
});
