topic("nutr", "Water: Role and Requirement, Metabolic Water; Major Minerals", {
  o: ["Describe the role and requirement of water", "Explain metabolic water", "Describe the functions of calcium, phosphorus, magnesium, sodium, potassium, chlorine and sulphur", "Describe deficiency signs in outline", "Describe supplementation of major minerals"],
  i: "Water is the most critical nutrient: an animal can lose almost all its fat and half its protein and live, but a loss of about 10 per cent of body water is dangerous and 20 per cent usually fatal. Major minerals build bone and run electrolyte balance.",
  s: "For each mineral, learn three things: what it does, which feeds are short of it, and what shows when it is lacking.",
  n: [
    ["Functions of water", "Solvent for nutrients; medium for digestion and absorption; transport in blood and lymph; heat regulation (evaporation, sweating, panting); lubrication of joints and gut; maintaining cell volume and blood volume; carrying wastes out in urine, faeces and sweat; milk is about 87 per cent water, eggs about 65 per cent; and metabolic reactions (hydrolysis). Water is also a structural part of tissues (body of adult 55 to 65 per cent)."],
    ["Sources and requirement", "Sources: drinking water, water in feed (green fodder 70 to 90 per cent, hay 10 to 15 per cent), and metabolic water. Requirement depends on dry matter intake, milk yield, temperature and humidity, salt and protein content of the ration, and species. Rough guides often quoted: a lactating cow may need roughly 4 to 5 litres of water per litre of milk plus maintenance (check the textbook and standards); water intake in poultry is about two times feed intake by weight at moderate temperatures and rises in heat. Cleanliness and free access matter as much as the amount: restricted water reduces feed intake and production."],
    ["Metabolic water", "Water produced by oxidation of nutrients. 100 g of fat gives about 107 g of water, 100 g of carbohydrate about 55 g, and 100 g of protein about 41 g (approximate values: check your textbook). Camels, desert rodents and migrating birds depend partly on metabolic water; fat storage in the hump gives water on oxidation, though extra oxygen is needed and respiratory water loss rises, so the camel's real advantage lies in other adaptations."],
    ["Water loss and deprivation", "Routes: urine, faeces, sweat, evaporation from lungs and skin, milk, eggs. Dehydration signs: dry mucous membranes, sunken eyes, reduced skin elasticity, reduced feed intake, dark scanty urine, then weakness and death. Salt poisoning (water deprivation sodium ion toxicosis) can occur when water is restored suddenly after deprivation, especially in pigs. Water quality problems: high salinity, nitrates, algal toxins, bacteria, and high sulphate."],
    ["Calcium and phosphorus", "Calcium: about 98 per cent in bone and teeth; also needed for blood clotting, muscle contraction, nerve function, enzyme activity, and milk and eggshell formation. Phosphorus: about 80 per cent in bones; also in ATP, nucleic acids, phospholipids and buffers. The Ca to P ratio in diets should be about 1:1 to 2:1 (check standards; laying hens need much more Ca for the shell). Absorption depends on vitamin D, the ratio, phytate (phytic phosphorus is poorly available to monogastrics), and dietary levels. Deficiency: rickets in young, osteomalacia in adults, osteoporosis, milk fever (low blood calcium) in dairy cows at calving, pica (phosphorus deficiency in cattle on pasture), poor fertility and slow growth. Sources: limestone, dicalcium phosphate, bone meal, legumes (rich in Ca), cereals (poor in Ca, phytate P)."],
    ["Magnesium, sodium, potassium and chlorine", "Magnesium: bone, enzyme cofactor, neuromuscular function; deficiency causes grass tetany (hypomagnesaemic tetany) in grazing ruminants on lush pasture and in lactating cows. Sodium, potassium and chloride: electrolytes maintaining osmotic pressure, acid-base balance, nerve impulses, and nutrient transport; sodium chloride (common salt) is supplied as a lick, since herbivores crave salt. Salt deficiency causes licking, poor growth and reduced yield. Excess salt with limited water causes poisoning. Potassium is usually adequate in forages but may be low in high-concentrate diets and heat-stressed cows. Dietary cation-anion difference (DCAD) is used to manage milk fever risk in dry cows."],
    ["Sulphur", "Part of sulphur amino acids (methionine, cystine), vitamins (thiamine, biotin), and some hormones. Ruminants need sulphur for microbial protein synthesis, especially when urea is fed (nitrogen to sulphur ratio about 10:1 to 12:1; check standards). Excess sulphur in water or feed can cause polioencephalomalacia and reduce copper absorption. Sources: protein feeds, sulphate salts."],
    ["Supplementation of major minerals", "Provide area-specific mineral mixtures (BIS and ICAR have standards), common salt, and special sources like limestone for layers. Check the labels and the proportion of Ca, P and other minerals; follow the manufacturer's inclusion rate. Overfeeding minerals is costly and can cause imbalance (excess Ca reduces Zn and P absorption; excess P affects Ca). Observe the herd: bone health, appetite, milk yield, fertility."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Mineral", "Key functions", "Deficiency or problem", "Common sources"], r: [["Calcium", "Bone, clotting, muscle", "Rickets, milk fever, thin shells", "Limestone, legumes, DCP"], ["Phosphorus", "Bone, energy (ATP)", "Pica, poor fertility, weak bones", "DCP, bone meal, bran"], ["Magnesium", "Enzymes, nerves", "Grass tetany", "Magnesium oxide, forages"], ["Sodium and chlorine", "Osmotic balance, nerves", "Salt hunger, poor growth", "Common salt"], ["Potassium", "Electrolyte balance", "Rare, weakness", "Forages"], ["Sulphur", "Amino acids, vitamins", "Poor use of NPN", "Protein feeds, sulphates"]] },
  df: ["Metabolic water: water formed by oxidation of nutrients in the body.", "Pica: craving for abnormal materials, often due to phosphorus deficiency.", "Milk fever: low blood calcium disease of dairy cows around calving."],
  tm: [["Rickets", "Defective bone mineralisation in young"], ["Osteomalacia", "Softening of adult bone"], ["Grass tetany", "Hypomagnesaemic tetany"], ["Phytate", "Poorly available phosphorus form"], ["DCAD", "Dietary cation-anion difference"], ["Salt lick", "Block of common salt for animals"], ["Hydration", "Water balance in the body"], ["Pica", "Craving for non-food items"]],
  st: ["Water and minerals are consumed.", "Absorption depends on vitamins and ratios.", "Bones store and release calcium and phosphorus.", "Electrolytes keep balance.", "Deficiency signs show first in young and high producers.", "Mineral mixtures correct the diet."],
  ex: ["Cow with milk fever near calving.", "Calves with rickets.", "Cattle on lush pasture with grass tetany."],
  k: ["Water functions.", "Metabolic water.", "Ca:P ratio.", "Mg and tetany.", "Salt."],
  m: ["Water loss of 20 per cent is usually fatal. Phytate P is poorly available to non-ruminants. Check ratio and amounts with your feeding standards."],
  sa: [["What is metabolic water?", "Water produced by the oxidation of nutrients."], ["Name a disease of low magnesium.", "Grass tetany."], ["Why is a Ca:P ratio important?", "Imbalance reduces absorption and bone formation."]],
  la: [["Describe the functions and requirement of water and metabolic water.", "Roles, sources, loss, requirement factors, deprivation."], ["Describe the major minerals, their functions and deficiency signs.", "Ca, P, Mg, Na, K, Cl, S."]],
  q: [["98 per cent of body calcium is in", ["Bone and teeth", "Blood", "Muscle", "Liver"], 0, "Skeleton."],
    ["Fat oxidation gives per 100 g about", ["107 g water", "10 g water", "None", "500 g water"], 0, "Highest metabolic water."],
    ["Hypomagnesaemic tetany is called", ["Grass tetany", "Milk fever", "Bloat", "Ketosis"], 0, "Lush pasture."],
    ["Phytate phosphorus is poorly used by", ["Poultry and pigs", "Ruminants", "All equally", "None"], 0, "Lack phytase."],
    ["Salt is needed as", ["Sodium and chloride source", "Calcium source", "Vitamin source", "Energy source"], 0, "Electrolytes."]],
  f: [["DCP", "Dicalcium phosphate"], ["DCAD", "Dietary cation-anion difference"], ["Ca:P", "Calcium to phosphorus ratio"], ["ATP", "Adenosine triphosphate"], ["BIS", "Bureau of Indian Standards"]],
  d: ["Revise Physiology of calcium regulation.", "List five area-specific mineral deficiencies in India.", "Read the BIS mineral mixture standard."],
});

topic("nutr", "Trace Minerals: Functions, Deficiency, Toxicity and Supplementation", {
  o: ["Describe functions of iron, copper, zinc, manganese, iodine, cobalt, selenium, molybdenum, fluorine and others", "Describe deficiency and toxicity patterns in outline", "Explain mineral interactions", "Describe area-specific mineral mixtures", "Describe safe supplementation practice"],
  i: "Trace minerals are needed in milligram or microgram amounts but control enzymes, hormones and immunity. Many Indian soils and fodders are deficient in some trace elements, which is why area-specific mineral mixture programmes exist.",
  s: "Use a table in the exam: element, role, deficiency, toxic signs, key interaction.",
  n: [
    ["Iron and copper", "Iron: haemoglobin, myoglobin and cytochromes; deficiency causes anaemia, especially in piglets raised on milk and indoors without iron source. Sources: green forage, supplements; excess iron is toxic and lowers copper and phosphorus use. Copper: enzymes of iron use, pigmentation (wool and hair pigment loss), collagen cross linking, and myelin; deficiency in ruminants causes anaemia, achromotrichia (loss of coat colour), diarrhoea, swayback (ataxia) in lambs, bone disorders and poor fertility. Copper toxicity is common in sheep (very low tolerance) and is enhanced by low molybdenum. Molybdenum and sulphur lower copper absorption (induced copper deficiency)."],
    ["Zinc and manganese", "Zinc: part of many enzymes (carbonic anhydrase, alkaline phosphatase), skin and hair integrity, immunity, reproduction and taste. Deficiency: parakeratosis (scaly skin) in pigs fed high calcium diets, poor growth, hair loss, poor wound healing, and testicular atrophy. Phytate and excess calcium reduce zinc availability. Manganese: bone matrix, cartilage, and reproduction; deficiency causes perosis (slipped tendon) in chicks, delayed oestrus and abnormal bone growth in calves; manganese needs are high in laying birds for shell strength."],
    ["Iodine and cobalt", "Iodine: part of thyroid hormones T3 and T4; deficiency causes goitre (enlarged thyroid), hairless or weak newborns, abortion and stillbirth, low growth and poor metabolic rate. Goitrogens (in cabbage, mustard, raw soybean, cassava) worsen the problem. Iodised salt is the usual supplement. Cobalt: component of vitamin B12 made by rumen microbes; deficiency in ruminants causes anaemia, wasting, poor appetite ('pining', 'bush sickness'), and vitamin B12 deficiency. Non-ruminants need preformed B12."],
    ["Selenium and vitamin E link", "Selenium is a part of glutathione peroxidase, an antioxidant enzyme, and works with vitamin E to protect cell membranes. Deficiency: white muscle disease (nutritional muscular dystrophy) in lambs and calves, exudative diathesis in chicks, mulberry heart disease in pigs, retained placenta and reduced immunity in cows. Selenium is toxic at not much higher levels: blind staggers, alkali disease (hoof and hair loss) in livestock grazing selenium-accumulating plants. Selenium supplementation is regulated and must follow the legal limits. No doses are given here."],
    ["Fluorine, chromium, molybdenum and others", "Fluorine: needed in tiny amounts for teeth; excess causes fluorosis (mottled teeth, bone exostoses, lameness), as in areas with high fluoride in water or phosphate supplements made from rock phosphate. Chromium: glucose tolerance factor; interest in heat stress and stress reduction. Molybdenum: enzymes (xanthine oxidase); excess causes copper deficiency (teart pastures). Nickel, vanadium, silicon, boron, lithium and others are of research interest. Heavy metals (lead, cadmium, mercury, arsenic) are toxic and have no nutritional role."],
    ["Mineral interactions", "Calcium and zinc, copper and molybdenum and sulphur, iron and copper, selenium and sulphur, phosphorus and magnesium, and calcium and magnesium all affect each other's absorption. Antagonism is more likely where a diet contains a high level of one element. Organic (chelated) trace minerals may have higher bioavailability in some situations; evidence varies. Supplement by balanced mixtures, not single elements."],
    ["Area-specific mineral mixtures and supplementation", "Soil, water and fodder analysis shows local deficiencies. India has regional deficiencies mapped by ICAR institutes (the National Dairy Research Institute and others), and area-specific mineral mixture (ASMM) recommendations for states. BIS has standards for mineral mixtures for cattle, buffalo, sheep, goats and poultry (check the current standards). Mixed with concentrate or fed as lick blocks; the amount depends on production level. Do not exceed legal and safe limits; excess is wasteful and toxic."],
    ["Diagnosis and monitoring", "Tools: soil and fodder analysis, serum, liver and hair concentrations, enzyme activity (for example glutathione peroxidase for selenium), and response to supplementation. Clinical signs are non-specific, so lab work is needed. Prevention is cheaper than treatment."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Element", "Key role", "Deficiency", "Toxicity or interaction"], r: [["Iron", "Haemoglobin", "Anaemia in piglets", "Excess lowers copper"], ["Copper", "Enzymes, pigment, myelin", "Swayback, achromotrichia", "Sheep sensitive; Mo and S antagonise"], ["Zinc", "Enzymes, skin, immunity", "Parakeratosis", "High Ca lowers availability"], ["Manganese", "Bone, cartilage", "Perosis in chicks", "Needs high in layers"], ["Iodine", "Thyroid hormones", "Goitre", "Goitrogens worsen"], ["Cobalt", "Vitamin B12", "Pining in ruminants", "Rumen microbes need it"], ["Selenium", "Glutathione peroxidase", "White muscle disease", "Narrow safe range"], ["Fluorine", "Teeth", "Not usually deficient", "Fluorosis from excess"]] },
  df: ["Trace mineral: mineral needed in very small amounts, usually mg/kg or less in the diet.", "Goitrogen: substance that interferes with thyroid iodine use.", "Perosis: slipped tendon disease in chicks from manganese or choline deficiency."],
  tm: [["Parakeratosis", "Zinc deficiency skin disease"], ["Goitre", "Enlarged thyroid"], ["Swayback", "Copper deficiency ataxia in lambs"], ["Pining", "Cobalt deficiency wasting"], ["White muscle disease", "Selenium and vitamin E deficiency"], ["Fluorosis", "Excess fluoride"], ["ASMM", "Area-specific mineral mixture"], ["Teart pasture", "High molybdenum pasture"]],
  st: ["Soil and plants hold trace minerals.", "Animals eat and absorb them.", "Interactions modify availability.", "Deficiency or excess shows as disease.", "Tests confirm the problem.", "Area-specific mixtures correct it."],
  ex: ["Lambs with swayback in a copper deficient area.", "Pigs with parakeratosis on a high calcium diet.", "Goitre in newborn calves from iodine deficiency."],
  k: ["Fe and Cu.", "Zn and Mn.", "I and Co.", "Se and vitamin E.", "Interactions."],
  m: ["Sheep tolerate very little copper. Selenium has a narrow safe range. Always check legal and BIS limits and avoid giving doses from memory."],
  sa: [["What disease results from selenium deficiency in lambs?", "White muscle disease."], ["What is the role of cobalt in ruminants?", "It is needed by rumen microbes to make vitamin B12."], ["Why are goitrogens a problem?", "They block thyroid iodine use and can cause goitre."]],
  la: [["Describe the functions and deficiency signs of the important trace minerals.", "Fe, Cu, Zn, Mn, I, Co, Se, F."], ["Discuss mineral interactions and area-specific mineral mixtures.", "Antagonisms, soil mapping, supplements, safety."]],
  q: [["Swayback in lambs is due to deficiency of", ["Copper", "Iron", "Iodine", "Zinc"], 0, "Neonatal ataxia."],
    ["Cobalt is part of", ["Vitamin B12", "Thyroxine", "Haemoglobin", "Insulin"], 0, "Rumen microbes."],
    ["Skin scaling in pigs with high Ca diet is", ["Parakeratosis", "Rickets", "Goitre", "Perosis"], 0, "Zinc."],
    ["Mulberry heart disease in pigs is linked to", ["Selenium and vitamin E", "Iron", "Iodine", "Cobalt"], 0, "Antioxidant system."],
    ["Goitrogens affect", ["Thyroid", "Bone", "Skin", "Liver only"], 0, "Iodine use."]],
  f: [["ASMM", "Area-specific mineral mixture"], ["GPx", "Glutathione peroxidase"], ["T3", "Triiodothyronine"], ["T4", "Thyroxine"], ["Se", "Selenium"]],
  d: ["Revise Pathology of nutritional diseases.", "Find your district's mineral map.", "Read the BIS mineral mixture standard."],
});

topic("nutr", "Vitamins: Fat-soluble and Water-soluble, Functions, Requirements and Supplementation", {
  o: ["Classify vitamins", "Describe the functions of vitamins A, D, E and K", "Describe the B-complex vitamins and vitamin C", "Describe deficiency signs in outline", "Describe sources and supplementation"],
  i: "Vitamins are organic compounds needed in tiny amounts to regulate metabolism. Ruminants make many of them in the rumen, so deficiency patterns differ between ruminants and non-ruminants.",
  s: "Link each vitamin with one function, one deficiency disease and one best source. That is the exam skeleton.",
  n: [
    ["Classification and general points", "Fat-soluble: A, D, E, K (stored in the liver and fat; excess can be toxic, especially A and D). Water-soluble: B-complex (thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, pantothenic acid, pyridoxine, biotin, folic acid, cobalamin B12, choline) and C (not essential in most farm animals as they synthesise it; guinea pigs, primates and fruit bats need it). Rumen microbes make B vitamins and vitamin K, so adult ruminants rarely need them in the diet (young calves before rumen development do). Poultry and pigs need most B vitamins in feed."],
    ["Vitamin A", "Forms: retinol, retinal, retinoic acid; provitamin carotenes (beta-carotene) from green plants. Functions: vision (rhodopsin), epithelial integrity, growth, reproduction, immunity. Deficiency: night blindness, xerophthalmia, keratinisation of epithelium, rough coat, poor growth, infertility, abortion, stillbirth, and congenital defects. In poultry, deficiency causes pustules in oesophagus and respiratory infections. Sources: green fodder, yellow maize, carrots, fish liver oils, supplements. Stores in the liver last for months in cattle. Excess causes toxicity, so use authorised premixes."],
    ["Vitamin D", "Forms: D2 (ergocalciferol, in sun-cured forage) and D3 (cholecalciferol, made in skin by UV light from 7-dehydrocholesterol). Active form 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D (calcitriol) raises intestinal calcium and phosphorus absorption and bone mineralisation. Deficiency: rickets, osteomalacia, weak eggshells and leg weakness in poultry (indoor birds need supplements). Excess causes soft tissue calcification. Sources: sunlight, sun-cured hay, supplements."],
    ["Vitamin E and selenium", "Alpha-tocopherol is the main antioxidant in cell membranes, protecting polyunsaturated fatty acids from peroxidation; it works with selenium. Deficiency: white muscle disease (nutritional myopathy) in calves and lambs, encephalomalacia (crazy chick disease), exudative diathesis, mulberry heart disease in pigs, and reproductive failure. The need rises with high polyunsaturated fat and rancid feed. Sources: green forage, cereal germ, oils, supplements."],
    ["Vitamin K", "Needed for synthesis of clotting factors II, VII, IX and X. Forms: K1 (phylloquinone in plants), K2 (menaquinone made by gut bacteria), and K3 (menadione, synthetic). Deficiency: haemorrhages and prolonged clotting time, seen in poultry, in animals on dicoumarol in spoiled sweet clover hay (mouldy sweet clover disease) and after sulphonamide or antibiotic use that kills gut flora. Not usually a problem in ruminants."],
    ["B-complex vitamins", "Thiamine (B1): carbohydrate metabolism; deficiency causes polyneuritis and star-gazing in poultry, and polioencephalomalacia in ruminants. Riboflavin (B2): energy metabolism; curled toe paralysis in chicks. Niacin: NAD and NADP; black tongue in dogs, pellagra-like signs in pigs and poultry (maize is low in available niacin and tryptophan). Pantothenic acid: coenzyme A; dermatitis, goose-stepping gait in pigs. Pyridoxine (B6): amino acid metabolism. Biotin: carboxylase reactions; foot pad dermatitis in poultry, hoof problems in pigs, raw egg white (avidin) causes deficiency. Folic acid: nucleic acid synthesis; anaemia. Cobalamin (B12): propionate and methionine metabolism; made only by microbes, needs cobalt. Choline: lipid transport; fatty liver and perosis. Do not memorise every sign: link each vitamin with its main metabolic role."],
    ["Vitamin C and others", "Ascorbic acid: collagen synthesis, antioxidant; synthesised by most farm animals; supplementation in heat stress in poultry is studied. Others: carnitine and inositol are vitamin-like compounds. Antivitamins: avidin (egg white), thiaminase (bracken fern, some fish), dicoumarol (mouldy sweet clover), and antagonists in raw feeds. Heat, light, rancid fat and storage reduce vitamin potency; pelleting and long storage need overages by manufacturers."],
    ["Requirements and supplementation", "Requirements depend on species, age, production, stress and diet. Premixes are used for poultry and pigs; ruminants need A, D and E in particular when on dry or stored feed. Methods: vitamin premixes added to feed, water-soluble products in water, injectable forms under veterinary supervision. Follow feeding standards and product labels. Never rely on memory for doses. Overdoses of A and D are toxic."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Vitamin", "Main function", "Deficiency example", "Source"], r: [["A", "Vision, epithelium", "Night blindness, xerophthalmia", "Green fodder, carrots, yellow maize"], ["D", "Ca and P absorption", "Rickets, soft shells", "Sunlight, sun-cured hay"], ["E", "Antioxidant", "White muscle disease, encephalomalacia", "Green fodder, grains, oils"], ["K", "Clotting factors", "Haemorrhage", "Green plants, gut bacteria"], ["B1", "Carbohydrate metabolism", "Polyneuritis, star-gazing", "Grains, bran, yeast"], ["B2", "Energy metabolism", "Curled toe paralysis", "Green feed, milk, yeast"], ["Biotin", "Carboxylation", "Foot pad dermatitis", "Oilseeds, yeast"], ["B12", "Propionate metabolism", "Anaemia, pining", "Animal proteins, rumen synthesis"]] },
  df: ["Vitamin: organic compound needed in small amounts for normal metabolism.", "Provitamin: precursor converted to a vitamin, such as carotene.", "Antivitamin: substance that destroys or blocks a vitamin."],
  tm: [["Retinol", "Vitamin A alcohol"], ["Carotene", "Provitamin A"], ["Cholecalciferol", "Vitamin D3"], ["Tocopherol", "Vitamin E"], ["Menadione", "Synthetic vitamin K3"], ["Avidin", "Biotin-binding egg white protein"], ["Thiaminase", "Enzyme destroying thiamine"], ["Xerophthalmia", "Dry eye from vitamin A lack"]],
  st: ["Vitamins come from feed, sunlight or microbes.", "They act as coenzymes and regulators.", "Stores are built in liver and fat.", "Lack causes characteristic disease.", "Antivitamins or spoilage make it worse.", "Premixes and good feed prevent it."],
  ex: ["Night blindness in cattle on dry fodder.", "Rickets in indoor-housed calves.", "Curled toe paralysis in chicks lacking riboflavin."],
  k: ["Fat-soluble four.", "B vitamin roles.", "Rumen synthesis.", "Antivitamins.", "Premix use."],
  m: ["Vitamins A and D are toxic in excess. Vitamin B12 contains cobalt. Do not quote doses from memory."],
  sa: [["Name the fat-soluble vitamins.", "A, D, E and K."], ["Why do adult ruminants rarely need B vitamins in feed?", "Rumen microbes synthesise them."], ["What is avidin?", "A protein in egg white that binds biotin."]],
  la: [["Describe the functions and deficiency signs of fat-soluble vitamins.", "A, D, E, K with examples."], ["Describe the B-complex vitamins in animal nutrition.", "Roles, deficiency, sources, ruminant synthesis."]],
  q: [["Rickets results from lack of", ["Vitamin D", "Vitamin A", "Vitamin K", "Vitamin C"], 0, "Calcium use."],
    ["Dicoumarol interferes with", ["Vitamin K", "Vitamin D", "Vitamin A", "Vitamin E"], 0, "Sweet clover."],
    ["Curled toe paralysis is due to", ["Riboflavin deficiency", "Biotin excess", "Vitamin A excess", "Zinc excess"], 0, "Chicks."],
    ["Vitamin synthesised only by microbes is", ["B12", "A", "C", "D"], 0, "Needs cobalt."],
    ["Retinol is", ["Vitamin A", "Vitamin E", "Vitamin K", "Niacin"], 0, "Vision vitamin."]],
  f: [["RBP", "Retinol-binding protein"], ["NAD", "Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide"], ["FAD", "Flavin adenine dinucleotide"], ["IU", "International unit"], ["B12", "Cobalamin"]],
  d: ["Revise Biochemistry of vitamins.", "Make a vitamin mind map.", "Compare vitamin content of green and dry fodder."],
});

topic("nutr", "Feeds and Fodders: Classification, Availability and Importance for Livestock and Poultry", {
  o: ["Classify feeds and fodders", "Describe common roughages, concentrates and agro-industrial by-products", "Describe unconventional feeds", "Describe availability and importance in India", "Describe nutrient content patterns"],
  i: "Indian livestock depend mainly on crop residues and grazing, with a shortage of green fodder and concentrate. A veterinarian needs to know the feed resources of the region, their nutrient content, and how to improve their use.",
  s: "Group feeds by use: bulky or concentrated, energy or protein, conventional or unconventional. Then attach a few typical nutrient values to each group.",
  n: [
    ["Classification of feeds", "Roughages (more than 18 per cent crude fibre): dry (straws, stovers, hay) and succulent (green fodder, silage, roots). Concentrates (less than 18 per cent crude fibre): energy rich (cereal grains, brans, molasses, fats) and protein rich (oilcakes and meals, pulses, animal proteins). Additives and supplements: minerals, vitamins, NPN. A second classification (Harris' international system) groups feeds into eight classes: dry forages, pasture, silages, energy feeds, protein supplements, mineral supplements, vitamin supplements and additives. The Indian system follows Roughage and Concentrate with by-products."],
    ["Green fodders", "Cereal fodders: maize, sorghum, bajra, oats; leguminous: lucerne (alfalfa), berseem, cowpea, guar, stylo; perennial grasses: Napier, para grass, guinea grass, Cenchrus, Setaria; tree fodders: subabul (leucaena; contains mimosine), Sesbania, Gliricidia, Moringa, fodder trees of local areas. Legumes give higher crude protein (15 to 20 per cent), cereal fodders have more energy. Nutrients fall as plants mature (more lignin, less protein): cut at the right stage. Green fodder supplies carotene, minerals and palatability."],
    ["Dry roughages and crop residues", "Paddy straw, wheat straw (bhusa), ragi straw, sorghum and maize stovers, groundnut haulms, pulse straws, sugarcane tops and bagasse. They are low in protein (3 to 5 per cent in cereal straws), high in fibre and lignin, low in digestibility and minerals, but they form the main bulk of ruminant feed in India. Pulse and groundnut haulms are better than cereal straws. Paddy straw has high silica and oxalate; improved by treatment (see roughage improvement chapter). Hay: dried green fodder with 10 to 15 per cent moisture."],
    ["Energy-rich concentrates", "Cereal grains (maize, sorghum, bajra, barley, wheat, rice): high starch, low protein (8 to 12 per cent), low in lysine and tryptophan (maize low in lysine and tryptophan). Brans and by-products: rice bran (high fat, rancidity risk; de-oiled rice bran common), wheat bran (good phosphorus but poor in calcium), rice polish, maize gluten feed, molasses (sugar source, laxative at high level; palatable binder), tapioca (low protein, cyanogenic glucosides), and fats and oils for energy density."],
    ["Protein-rich concentrates", "Oilcakes and meals: soybean meal (best plant protein, about 44 to 48 per cent CP after heat treatment), groundnut cake (about 40 to 45 per cent, aflatoxin risk), cottonseed cake (contains gossypol), mustard and rapeseed cake (glucosinolates), sunflower cake, sesame, coconut, palm kernel, linseed cake. Pulses and chunies. Animal proteins: fish meal (60 per cent or more CP, rich in lysine and methionine, salt content to check), meat meal, blood meal, feather meal (hydrolysed; low in lysine and methionine), meat and bone meal (restrictions in ruminant feed because of BSE rules). Single cell protein: yeast, algae, bacteria. Values here are typical, not standards: check tables of feed composition."],
    ["Agro-industrial by-products and unconventional feeds", "Sugar industry: molasses, bagasse, press mud. Brewing and distillery: spent grains, DDGS (distillers dried grains with solubles). Fruit and vegetable processing wastes, tamarind seed powder, mango seed kernel, cashew by-products, sunflower, rubber seed cake, neem cake (needs detoxification), kitchen waste, poultry litter (processed; safety rules apply), azolla, duckweed, leucaena leaf meal, insects (black soldier fly larvae), and seaweed. These feeds can cut costs but need quality checks, processing and an eye on anti-nutritional factors."],
    ["Availability in India and importance", "India has the largest livestock population in the world but only a small area (about 4 to 5 per cent) under fodder crops; there are deficits in green fodder, dry fodder and concentrate (commonly cited: deficit in green fodder is 35 per cent or more; check the latest figures). Remedies: improved fodder varieties, intercropping, better use of crop residues, silage and hay making, fodder banks, non-conventional resources, and common grazing land management. Feed costs and seasonal shortages drive many nutrition programmes."],
    ["Nutrient content patterns", "Typical crude protein: green legumes 15 to 20 per cent, green grasses 8 to 12 per cent, cereal straw 3 to 5 per cent, cereal grains 8 to 12 per cent, oilcakes 25 to 50 per cent, fish meal 55 to 65 per cent. Typical TDN: green fodder 55 to 65 per cent of DM, straw 35 to 45 per cent, grains 75 to 85 per cent, oilcakes 70 to 80 per cent. Calcium: legumes high, grains low. Phosphorus: brans high, straws low. These are rough ranges; the exam expects typical values from a table of feed composition."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Feed group", "Examples", "Typical features"], r: [["Cereal fodders", "Maize, sorghum, oats", "More energy, less protein"], ["Legume fodders", "Berseem, lucerne, cowpea", "High protein, calcium, palatable"], ["Dry roughages", "Paddy and wheat straw, stovers", "High fibre, low protein, low digestibility"], ["Energy concentrates", "Maize, bajra, rice bran", "High starch or fat, low protein"], ["Protein concentrates", "Soybean meal, groundnut cake, fish meal", "High protein, watch toxins"], ["By-products", "Molasses, DDGS, brans", "Cheap, variable quality"], ["Unconventional", "Azolla, leucaena, insect meal", "Useful but need processing and checks"]] },
  df: ["Roughage: bulky feed with more than 18 per cent crude fibre.", "Concentrate: feed with low fibre and high energy or protein.", "By-product: material left after processing of a primary product, used as feed."],
  tm: [["Bhusa", "Wheat straw chaff"], ["Stover", "Dry stalks of maize or sorghum"], ["Oilcake", "Residue after oil extraction"], ["DDGS", "Distillers dried grains with solubles"], ["Molasses", "Sugar industry syrup"], ["Mimosine", "Toxic amino acid in leucaena"], ["Gossypol", "Cottonseed pigment toxin"], ["Azolla", "Aquatic fern fed as protein source"]],
  st: ["Crops and by-products supply feed.", "Feeds are classified by fibre and nutrients.", "Quality varies with maturity and processing.", "Rations combine roughage and concentrate.", "Shortages need unconventional feeds.", "Quality checks protect animals."],
  ex: ["Berseem fed to dairy cows.", "Paddy straw treated with urea.", "Soybean meal in poultry feed."],
  k: ["Roughage versus concentrate.", "Legume versus cereal fodder.", "Oilcake toxins.", "By-products.", "India's fodder deficit."],
  m: ["Fibre cut-off is 18 per cent. Values vary with stage and region; always use feed tables. Raw soybean has trypsin inhibitors."],
  sa: [["Name two energy rich concentrates.", "Maize and rice bran."], ["Which oilcake carries a risk of aflatoxin?", "Groundnut cake."], ["What is a roughage?", "A bulky feed with more than 18 per cent crude fibre."]],
  la: [["Classify feeds and fodders with examples.", "Roughage, concentrate, additives, subgroups, examples."], ["Describe feed resources of India and their limitations.", "Residues, green fodder, deficit, by-products, unconventional feeds."]],
  q: [["Best plant protein source is usually", ["Soybean meal", "Rice straw", "Maize", "Molasses"], 0, "High quality."],
    ["Leucaena contains", ["Mimosine", "Gossypol", "Solanine", "Tannin only"], 0, "Toxic amino acid."],
    ["Cottonseed contains", ["Gossypol", "Mimosine", "Lathyrogen", "Glucosinolates"], 0, "Toxic pigment."],
    ["DDGS is a", ["Distillery by-product", "Fodder grass", "Mineral", "Vitamin"], 0, "Protein and energy source."],
    ["Dry roughages are mainly low in", ["Protein and digestibility", "Fibre", "Lignin", "Bulk"], 0, "Straws."]],
  f: [["DDGS", "Distillers dried grains with solubles"], ["CP", "Crude protein"], ["TDN", "Total digestible nutrients"], ["CF", "Crude fibre"], ["SCP", "Single cell protein"]],
  d: ["Make a feed map of your district.", "Read a table of feed composition.", "Visit a cattle feed shop."],
});
/* ================= NUTRITION: Unit 1 principles, part B ================= */
topic("nutr", "Measures of Food Energy: GE, DE, ME, NE, TDN, Starch Equivalent, Food Units and Physiological Fuel Value", {
  o: ["Trace the partition of feed energy", "Define GE, DE, ME and NE", "Define TDN and calculate it", "Describe starch equivalent, food unit and physiological fuel value", "Compare the energy systems and their applications"],
  i: "Energy is the nutrient needed in the largest amount. Because feeds differ in how much energy actually reaches the animal, nutritionists use different measures that remove the losses step by step.",
  s: "Picture a waterfall: gross energy at the top, then faeces, urine and methane take their share, and heat increment takes another; what is left is net energy for the animal.",
  n: [
    ["Partition of feed energy", "Gross energy (GE) minus faecal energy gives digestible energy (DE). DE minus urinary energy and combustible gases (mainly methane in ruminants) gives metabolisable energy (ME). ME minus heat increment (heat of fermentation and nutrient metabolism) gives net energy (NE), which is used for maintenance (NEm) and production (NEg for gain, NEl for lactation, NEp for pregnancy and eggs). The heat increment is lost as heat but is useful in cold weather."],
    ["Gross energy (GE)", "The total heat released when a feed is completely burned in a bomb calorimeter with oxygen. Approximate values: carbohydrates about 4.1 to 4.2 kcal per gram, proteins about 5.6 kcal per gram, fats about 9.4 kcal per gram. Fats have the highest energy density. GE says nothing about how much the animal can use; wood and sugar have a similar GE per gram but very different digestibility."],
    ["Digestible energy (DE)", "DE = GE of feed minus GE of faeces (faecal energy includes undigested feed and metabolic faecal matter). Determined by digestion trials. DE of roughages is low because of poor digestibility; DE of grains is high. DE is easy to measure and widely used for pigs and horses. For ruminants, DE overestimates the value of roughages because they lose more energy as methane and heat."],
    ["Metabolisable energy (ME)", "ME = DE minus urinary energy minus gaseous (methane) energy. In ruminants about 82 per cent of DE is ME, as a general approximation (check current standards); for poultry, nitrogen corrected apparent metabolisable energy (AMEn) is used because faeces and urine are voided together. ME is the main system for poultry feeds in India and for ruminants in many countries (ARC, AFRC, ICAR standards in Mcal or MJ ME per day). ME values in feed tables must be matched with requirements expressed the same way."],
    ["Net energy (NE) and heat increment", "NE = ME minus heat increment (HI). NE is the most accurate measure of the energy value of a feed for the animal, but it is hard to measure (needs calorimetry) and its value varies with the purpose: roughages are used more efficiently for maintenance than for gain. Efficiency of ME use: for maintenance about 70 to 75 per cent, for lactation about 60 to 65 per cent, for growth and fattening about 40 to 50 per cent (rough figures: check the standards). NE systems are used in the USA (NEl for dairy cows), France and the Netherlands."],
    ["Total digestible nutrients (TDN)", "TDN = digestible crude protein + digestible crude fibre + digestible nitrogen free extract + 2.25 x digestible ether extract (all in per cent). The factor 2.25 accounts for the higher energy of fat. One kg TDN is about 4.4 Mcal of digestible energy (an approximation commonly used; check your textbook). Example concept: if a feed has 20 per cent digestible protein, 15 per cent digestible fibre, 40 per cent digestible NFE and 2 per cent digestible fat, TDN = 20 + 15 + 40 + 4.5 = 79.5. TDN is the main system in Indian rations (ICAR standards are given as DCP and TDN). Weakness: it treats all digestible nutrients as equal sources of net energy, so it overvalues roughages."],
    ["Starch equivalent, food units and physiological fuel value", "Starch equivalent (Kellner): the amount of pure starch that would produce the same fattening effect as 100 kg of a feed (given as kg starch equivalent per 100 kg feed). Based on fattening trials in adult oxen; used in Europe in the past. One kg of starch equivalent corresponds to about 2.36 Mcal of net energy. Food unit (Scandinavian): the net energy of 1 kg of good barley is taken as one feed unit. Physiological fuel value (PFV): the energy available to the body per gram of nutrient after correction for digestion and urinary losses, as in human nutrition: carbohydrate 4, protein 4, fat 9 kcal per gram (Atwater factors). Also called Rubner factors in some books."],
    ["Comparison and use", "GE: laboratory only. DE: pigs, horses. ME: poultry, ruminants (UK, India). NE: most accurate; used for production feeding of cows in the USA. TDN: practical and traditional in India. Starch equivalent and food unit: of historical value. For examination, be ready to define each, state the formula, and give one merit and one demerit."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Measure", "Formula or meaning", "Merit", "Demerit"], r: [["GE", "Heat of complete combustion", "Easy to measure", "No idea of use by animal"], ["DE", "GE minus faecal energy", "Simple, from digestion trial", "Ignores urine and methane losses"], ["ME", "DE minus urinary and gaseous energy", "Good for formulation", "Ignores heat increment"], ["NE", "ME minus heat increment", "Most accurate", "Hard to determine"], ["TDN", "DCP + DCF + DNFE + 2.25 DEE", "Easy, traditional", "Overvalues roughages"], ["Starch equivalent", "Fattening value compared with starch", "Concept of fattening effect", "Based on adult ox only"]] },
  df: ["Gross energy: total heat of combustion of a feed.", "Digestible energy: gross energy of feed minus faecal energy.", "Metabolisable energy: digestible energy minus urinary and gaseous energy.", "Net energy: metabolisable energy minus heat increment.", "TDN: sum of digestible protein, fibre, NFE and 2.25 times digestible fat."],
  tm: [["Bomb calorimeter", "Instrument for gross energy"], ["Heat increment", "Heat produced in digestion and metabolism"], ["NEm", "Net energy for maintenance"], ["NEl", "Net energy for lactation"], ["AMEn", "Nitrogen corrected apparent metabolisable energy"], ["Atwater factor", "4, 4, 9 kcal per gram"], ["TDN", "Total digestible nutrients"], ["Faecal energy", "Energy lost in faeces"]],
  st: ["Feed is burned: gross energy.", "Faeces are removed: digestible energy.", "Urine and methane are removed: metabolisable energy.", "Heat increment is removed: net energy.", "Net energy supports maintenance and production.", "TDN offers a practical summary."],
  ex: ["TDN computation from digestible fractions.", "Poultry feeds listed in kcal ME per kg.", "Methane loss in a cow."],
  k: ["Energy partition.", "TDN formula.", "2.25 factor.", "Atwater factors.", "ME and NE uses."],
  m: ["TDN includes 2.25 times digestible fat. ME is DE minus urine and methane. Check your textbook for exact conversion factors."],
  sa: [["Define metabolisable energy.", "Digestible energy minus the energy of urine and combustible gases."], ["Give the formula for TDN.", "DCP + DCF + DNFE + 2.25 x DEE."], ["What is heat increment?", "Heat produced during digestion and metabolism of feed."]],
  la: [["Describe the partition of dietary energy and the measures of food energy.", "GE, DE, ME, NE, HI, TDN, SE, PFV with merits."], ["Compare TDN and net energy systems.", "Definition, determination, merits, demerits, applications."]],
  q: [["Energy remaining after subtracting faecal energy is", ["DE", "ME", "NE", "GE"], 0, "Digestible energy."],
    ["Energy factor for fat in TDN is", ["2.25", "1.25", "4", "9"], 0, "Higher energy."],
    ["Atwater factors for carbohydrate, protein and fat are", ["4, 4, 9", "4, 9, 4", "9, 4, 4", "5, 5, 9"], 0, "kcal per gram."],
    ["Methane energy loss is subtracted in", ["ME", "DE", "GE", "TDN"], 0, "Ruminants."],
    ["Most accurate energy measure is", ["NE", "GE", "DE", "TDN"], 0, "Includes heat increment."]],
  f: [["GE", "Gross energy"], ["DE", "Digestible energy"], ["ME", "Metabolisable energy"], ["NE", "Net energy"], ["HI", "Heat increment"]],
  d: ["Work five TDN calculations.", "Revise Biochemistry of fuels.", "Draw the energy partition diagram."],
});

topic("nutr", "Calorimetry, Balance Studies and Energy Metabolism: Direct and Indirect Calorimetry, Carbon and Nitrogen Balance", {
  o: ["Describe direct calorimetry", "Describe indirect calorimetry and the respiratory quotient", "Describe carbon and nitrogen balance studies", "Calculate balances in a simple example", "Explain how these methods measure energy needs"],
  i: "Calorimetry measures the heat an animal produces; balance studies show what is retained from what is eaten. Together they are the experimental basis of feeding standards.",
  s: "Heat measured directly or calculated from gas exchange tells how fast the animal burns fuel. Balances tell where the food went.",
  n: [
    ["Direct calorimetry", "Direct measurement of the heat given off by an animal housed in an insulated chamber. Types: water-flow or gradient layer calorimeters. Heat from the animal warms circulating water or air; the rise in temperature multiplied by flow gives heat production. Accurate but costly and slow; used in research stations (human and small animal calorimeters). The bomb calorimeter is a separate instrument for feed."],
    ["Indirect calorimetry", "Heat production is calculated from oxygen consumption and carbon dioxide (and methane) production, because each litre of oxygen used yields roughly 4.8 to 5.0 kcal. Methods: closed circuit (animal in a closed chamber), open circuit (air flows through), respiration masks and hoods, ventilated chambers, and the doubly labelled water method for free-living animals. Respiratory quotient (RQ) = volume of CO2 produced divided by volume of O2 used. RQ of carbohydrate 1.0, fat about 0.7, protein about 0.8; RQ above 1.0 occurs in fat synthesis from carbohydrate. RQ shows the fuel being burned."],
    ["Metabolic rate and its measurement", "Basal metabolic rate (BMR): heat production of a fasted resting animal in a thermoneutral environment; fasting heat production in animals is measured after the effect of feed has gone. Metabolic body size: body weight raised to 0.75 (kg^0.75) is used to compare species (Kleiber's law, about 70 kcal x W^0.75 per day for basal metabolism). Factors raising metabolic rate: feeding, activity, cold, pregnancy, lactation, and growth."],
    ["Nitrogen balance", "Nitrogen intake minus nitrogen in faeces minus nitrogen in urine (and in milk, eggs, wool, scurf). Positive balance means retention in body protein (growth, pregnancy); zero means maintenance; negative means loss (starvation, disease). Nitrogen is measured by Kjeldahl method; protein = N x 6.25. Used to find protein requirement for maintenance (the intake that gives zero balance after correction for endogenous losses) and the biological value of protein."],
    ["Carbon balance and energy retention", "Carbon intake minus carbon in faeces, urine, carbon dioxide and methane gives carbon retained in body protein and fat. Combined with nitrogen balance, it tells how much fat is stored, since protein contains a known proportion of carbon. Energy retained = ME intake minus heat production. These data give the efficiency of use of ME for maintenance and production, which is the basis of net energy values."],
    ["Comparative slaughter and other methods", "Comparative slaughter: animals are slaughtered at start and end of a feeding trial and body composition compared to compute energy retained. Used in beef and sheep research. Other: dilution methods for body water, ultrasound for fat, and doubly labelled water. Different methods suit different purposes; calorimetry is the reference method."],
    ["Simple illustration", "Suppose a growing calf eats a diet giving 60 g nitrogen per day, loses 20 g in faeces and 25 g in urine: nitrogen retained = 60 minus 20 minus 25 = 15 g per day; this equals about 94 g protein (15 x 6.25), a positive balance indicating growth. Such figures are only illustrative."],
    ["Applications", "Calorimetry and balance data give requirements for maintenance and production, the efficiency of nutrient use, the energy value of feeds, the effect of environment (cold stress, heat stress), and the cost of activity. They underlie the ICAR and NRC feeding standards."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Method", "What is measured", "Output"], r: [["Direct calorimetry", "Heat lost from the body", "Heat production"], ["Indirect calorimetry", "O2 used, CO2 and CH4 produced", "Heat production and RQ"], ["Nitrogen balance", "N intake minus N excreted", "Protein retention"], ["Carbon balance", "C intake minus C excreted", "Fat and protein deposited"], ["Comparative slaughter", "Body composition before and after", "Energy retention"]] },
  df: ["Calorimetry: measurement of heat production of animals.", "Respiratory quotient: ratio of CO2 produced to O2 used.", "Nitrogen balance: nitrogen intake minus nitrogen excretion."],
  tm: [["RQ", "Respiratory quotient"], ["BMR", "Basal metabolic rate"], ["Kleiber's law", "Metabolic rate scales with W^0.75"], ["Kjeldahl", "Nitrogen estimation method"], ["Respiration chamber", "Closed chamber for gas exchange"], ["Positive balance", "Net retention"], ["Endogenous N", "Metabolic nitrogen losses"], ["Doubly labelled water", "Isotope method for energy expenditure"]],
  st: ["Animal eats a measured diet.", "Heat and gases or excreta are measured.", "Balances are calculated.", "Retention is derived.", "Efficiency of use is computed.", "Requirements are set."],
  ex: ["Cow in a respiration chamber.", "Nitrogen balance in a growing pig.", "RQ near 1.0 on a grain diet."],
  k: ["Direct versus indirect.", "RQ values.", "W^0.75.", "N balance sign.", "Carbon balance."],
  m: ["RQ: carbohydrate 1.0, fat 0.7, protein 0.8. Metabolic size is W^0.75."],
  sa: [["What is RQ?", "The ratio of carbon dioxide produced to oxygen consumed."], ["What does positive nitrogen balance mean?", "Nitrogen is being retained in body tissues as in growth."], ["Define metabolic body size.", "Body weight raised to the power 0.75."]],
  la: [["Describe direct and indirect calorimetry.", "Principles, methods, RQ, applications."], ["Describe nitrogen and carbon balance studies and their uses.", "Method, calculation, interpretation, requirements."]],
  q: [["RQ of fat oxidation is about", ["0.7", "1.0", "1.5", "0.2"], 0, "Fat uses more O2."],
    ["Kleiber's metabolic size is", ["W^0.75", "W^2", "W^0.5", "W^1"], 0, "Scaling law."],
    ["Direct calorimetry measures", ["Heat lost", "Oxygen only", "Nitrogen", "Carbon"], 0, "Chamber method."],
    ["Nitrogen is estimated by", ["Kjeldahl method", "Soxhlet", "Muffle furnace", "Bomb"], 0, "Protein analysis."],
    ["Negative nitrogen balance occurs in", ["Starvation", "Growth", "Pregnancy", "Fattening"], 0, "Net loss."]],
  f: [["RQ", "Respiratory quotient"], ["BMR", "Basal metabolic rate"], ["FHP", "Fasting heat production"], ["CH4", "Methane"], ["W", "Body weight"]],
  d: ["Revise Physiology of metabolism.", "Work three N balance sums.", "Read how a respiration chamber works."],
});

topic("nutr", "Protein Evaluation: Biological Value, PER, NPU, DCP, Protein Equivalent, Nutritive Ratio and Calorie Protein Ratio", {
  o: ["Describe measures of protein quality in non-ruminants", "Describe measures in ruminants", "Calculate DCP and nutritive ratio", "Explain the calorie protein ratio", "Interpret limitations of each measure"],
  i: "Crude protein tells you only the nitrogen content. To know how useful a protein is, you must measure digestibility and how well the absorbed amino acids are used.",
  s: "Digestibility and biological value are two steps in one story: first absorb, then retain.",
  n: [
    ["Crude protein and digestibility", "Crude protein (CP) = N x 6.25. Digestible crude protein (DCP) = CP x digestibility coefficient of protein (from a digestion trial). Apparent protein digestibility = (N intake minus faecal N) divided by N intake. True digestibility corrects for metabolic faecal nitrogen. For roughages, DCP can be estimated from CP by regression equations because protein digestibility depends on its percentage in the feed; ICAR tables list DCP values."],
    ["Biological value (BV)", "BV = (nitrogen retained divided by nitrogen absorbed) x 100. It measures how well absorbed protein is used. Egg and milk proteins have BV of about 90 to 100; fish meal about 75 to 85; soybean about 70 to 80; cereals lower (50 to 65); gelatin near zero as it lacks essential amino acids. BV depends on essential amino acid pattern, the energy supply, the level of protein intake and the animal's physiological state. Method (Mitchell): N intake, faecal N and urinary N with correction for endogenous losses."],
    ["Net protein utilisation (NPU) and PER", "NPU = BV x digestibility = nitrogen retained divided by nitrogen intake (x 100). It combines digestion and use. Protein efficiency ratio (PER) = gain in body weight (g) divided by protein consumed (g), usually measured in growing rats over 4 weeks; simple but ignores maintenance needs. Other: gross protein value (chick growth), amino acid score and chemical score (compare amino acid pattern with an ideal protein), and ileal digestibility of amino acids (modern poultry and pig practice)."],
    ["Protein evaluation in ruminants", "Because microbes change dietary protein, ruminant systems use rumen degradable protein (RDP), undegradable protein (UDP or bypass) and metabolisable protein (MP: true protein absorbed from the small intestine from microbial protein and UDP). Microbial protein yield depends on fermentable energy (ATP). Systems: crude protein (older), DCP (India), Cornell (CNCPS), NRC, AFRC (UK) and French PDI. Degradability measured in sacco (nylon bag in a fistulated animal), in vitro, or from tables. Rapid degradation of protein with poor energy supply wastes N as ammonia."],
    ["Protein equivalent and NPN", "Protein equivalent of non-protein nitrogen: the amount of true protein that the NPN could replace in ruminant rations. Urea contains about 46 per cent nitrogen, so its crude protein equivalent is 46 x 6.25 = 287 per cent (about 280 to 290). Some texts also define protein equivalent as digestible crude protein plus digestible NPN counted as protein; check the definition your textbook uses. NPN is useful only in ruminants with enough fermentable energy."],
    ["Nutritive ratio (NR)", "NR = digestible non-nitrogenous nutrients divided by digestible protein = (TDN minus DCP) : DCP. Often written as 1 : x, where x = (TDN - DCP) / DCP. Narrow ratio (protein rich, for example 1 : 4 or less in young animals, lactating cows on high yield): wide ratio (energy rich, for example 1 : 8 or more in work and fattening animals). A commonly used limit is: narrow when the ratio is narrower than 1:6; wide when wider than 1:6. A ration for adult maintenance has a NR of about 1:8 or wider; check ICAR standards. Example concept: TDN 60 and DCP 10 gives NR = (60 - 10)/10 = 5, written 1:5."],
    ["Calorie protein ratio and energy to protein relationships", "Used mainly for poultry and pigs: ME (kcal per kg diet) divided by crude protein (per cent) = calorie protein ratio, for example 3000 kcal ME and 20 per cent CP gives 150. As energy density rises, protein and amino acids must rise to keep intake balanced. Birds eat to meet energy needs, so protein level is tuned to energy. In ruminants, the energy to protein relationship appears as the NR."],
    ["Comparative view and limits", "CP: simple but crude. DCP: includes digestibility. BV, NPU and PER: for monogastric quality. RDP/UDP/MP: for ruminants. Ideal protein: amino acid ratios for poultry and swine. Always keep the species in mind. These figures are examples; exact values come from feed tables."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Measure", "Formula", "Used mainly for"], r: [["DCP", "CP x digestibility", "Indian ruminant rations"], ["BV", "N retained / N absorbed x 100", "Quality of absorbed protein"], ["NPU", "N retained / N intake x 100 (BV x digestibility)", "Overall protein use"], ["PER", "Weight gain / protein intake", "Growing rats and chicks"], ["Nutritive ratio", "(TDN - DCP) : DCP", "Energy to protein balance, ruminants"], ["Calorie protein ratio", "ME (kcal/kg) / CP (%)", "Poultry and pigs"], ["RDP, UDP, MP", "Rumen degradation and intestinal supply", "Modern ruminant systems"]] },
  df: ["Biological value: percentage of absorbed nitrogen that is retained.", "Nutritive ratio: ratio of digestible non-nitrogenous nutrients to digestible protein.", "Net protein utilisation: percentage of nitrogen intake that is retained."],
  tm: [["Endogenous N", "Metabolic nitrogen losses in faeces and urine"], ["Chemical score", "Amino acid pattern compared with reference"], ["Ideal protein", "Perfect amino acid pattern"], ["In sacco", "Nylon bag degradability method"], ["Metabolisable protein", "Protein absorbed from small intestine"], ["Narrow NR", "Protein rich ratio"], ["Wide NR", "Energy rich ratio"], ["PDI", "French protein system"]],
  st: ["Feed protein is eaten.", "Digestibility determines absorbed nitrogen.", "Amino acid balance determines use.", "BV and NPU measure retention.", "Ruminants add rumen degradation.", "Ratios tune energy and protein."],
  ex: ["Egg protein BV near 100.", "Urea has 287 per cent CP equivalent.", "A ration of 1:6 NR."],
  k: ["DCP formula.", "BV formula.", "NR formula.", "NPU.", "CPR."],
  m: ["BV uses absorbed N; NPU uses intake N. NR uses TDN minus DCP. Check your textbook for the cut-off between narrow and wide."],
  sa: [["Define biological value.", "The proportion of absorbed nitrogen retained by the body."], ["What is the nutritive ratio?", "The ratio of digestible non-nitrogenous nutrients to digestible protein, (TDN - DCP) : DCP."], ["What is the CP equivalent of urea?", "About 287 per cent, from 46 per cent nitrogen x 6.25."]],
  la: [["Describe the measures of protein quality in non-ruminants and ruminants.", "BV, NPU, PER, DCP, RDP, UDP, MP."], ["Explain nutritive ratio and calorie protein ratio with examples.", "Formula, narrow and wide, uses."]],
  q: [["BV equals", ["N retained / N absorbed x 100", "N retained / N intake x 100", "Gain / protein", "TDN / DCP"], 0, "Biological value."],
    ["NR is calculated as", ["(TDN - DCP) : DCP", "TDN : CP", "DCP : TDN", "CP : DM"], 0, "Energy to protein."],
    ["Urea CP equivalent is about", ["287 per cent", "46 per cent", "100 per cent", "16 per cent"], 0, "46 x 6.25."],
    ["PER uses", ["Weight gain per protein eaten", "Faecal N", "Urinary N", "Energy"], 0, "Rat assay."],
    ["Poultry energy to protein is shown by", ["Calorie protein ratio", "NR only", "BV", "PER"], 0, "ME per CP."]],
  f: [["BV", "Biological value"], ["NPU", "Net protein utilisation"], ["PER", "Protein efficiency ratio"], ["NR", "Nutritive ratio"], ["CPR", "Calorie protein ratio"]],
  d: ["Practise DCP and NR sums.", "Revise Physiology of protein digestion.", "Compare BV of five proteins."],
});

topic("nutr", "Proximate Analysis and Detergent Methods: Weende System, Van Soest Fractions and Their Meaning", {
  o: ["Describe the steps of the Weende system", "State the principle of each fraction", "Describe the Van Soest detergent method", "Point out limitations of proximate analysis", "Use analysis results in feed evaluation"],
  i: "Every feed table starts with the proximate analysis. It breaks a feed into fractions by simple chemistry. It is old, but you will use it for as long as you work with feeds.",
  s: "Moisture, ash, protein, fat, fibre, NFE: six fractions, with NFE obtained by difference, which means it collects all the errors of the others.",
  n: [
    ["Weende system: overview", "Henneberg and Stohmann (Weende Experimental Station, Germany, 1860s). A feed sample is analysed for moisture (dry matter), crude protein, ether extract, crude fibre and total ash; nitrogen free extract (NFE) is calculated by difference: NFE = 100 - (moisture + ash + CP + EE + CF). The fractions are crude because each includes mixed compounds."],
    ["Moisture and dry matter", "Moisture is determined by drying a weighed sample in a hot air oven (about 100 to 105 °C) to constant weight; loss in weight is moisture. Dry matter = 100 - moisture. Hay and silage with volatile acids lose some volatiles on oven drying, so toluene distillation or freeze drying is used for silage. Feed values are compared on a dry matter basis, since moisture varies."],
    ["Total ash and acid insoluble ash", "Ash is the residue after burning organic matter in a muffle furnace (about 550 to 600 °C). It represents the total minerals. Acid insoluble ash (insoluble in hot dilute hydrochloric acid) estimates sand and silica contamination, an adulteration check, and also serves as an internal marker in digestibility trials. Organic matter = DM - ash."],
    ["Crude protein (Kjeldahl)", "Digestion of the sample with concentrated sulphuric acid and a catalyst converts organic nitrogen into ammonium sulphate; steam distillation with alkali releases ammonia, collected in boric acid or standard acid and titrated. Nitrogen x 6.25 = crude protein. Crude protein includes true protein and non-protein nitrogen. Because it measures only nitrogen, it can be fooled by urea and melamine-type adulteration. Safety: concentrated acid and alkali need proper care."],
    ["Ether extract (crude fat)", "Extraction of the dry sample with petroleum ether or diethyl ether in a Soxhlet apparatus; the weight lost or extracted is the ether extract. It includes true fats, oils, waxes, pigments and fat-soluble vitamins. Flammable solvents need safety precautions."],
    ["Crude fibre", "The residue left after successive boiling of the fat-free sample in dilute sulphuric acid and dilute sodium hydroxide, then ashing (loss on ashing is crude fibre). Includes mostly cellulose with some lignin and hemicellulose; underestimates true fibre because part of hemicellulose and lignin dissolves. Therefore crude fibre has poor relationship with digestibility for some feeds."],
    ["Nitrogen free extract", "NFE is calculated: it includes sugars, starch, hemicellulose and some lignin. Its accuracy is low because it carries the errors of all the other determinations. It is nevertheless used for TDN calculations, since it is usually the largest fraction in feeds."],
    ["Detergent (Van Soest) system", "Goering and Van Soest improved the separation of fibre. Neutral detergent fibre (NDF) is the residue after boiling in neutral detergent solution and equals cell wall (cellulose, hemicellulose, lignin). Acid detergent fibre (ADF) is the residue after boiling in acid detergent solution and includes cellulose and lignin. Acid detergent lignin (ADL) is lignin after treatment with 72 per cent sulphuric acid. Hemicellulose = NDF - ADF; cellulose = ADF - ADL (approximately). NDF predicts intake (bulk limits intake), and ADF predicts digestibility (higher ADF means lower digestibility). Cell contents (soluble: sugars, protein, starch, lipids) = 100 - NDF. These are widely used in forage quality evaluation (relative feed value index)."],
    ["Limitations and uses", "Weende gives no information on individual amino acids, sugars, starch or lignin; NFE is by difference; crude fibre is imperfect. Detergent analysis solves part of this but is not an energy value. Modern methods include near infrared reflectance spectroscopy (NIRS) for rapid analysis, amino acid analysers, and HPLC for vitamins. Feed analysis supports ration formulation, feed trade, adulteration checks and research."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Fraction", "Method", "Contains"], r: [["Moisture", "Oven drying", "Water and some volatiles"], ["Ash", "Muffle furnace", "Total minerals"], ["Crude protein", "Kjeldahl, N x 6.25", "True protein and NPN"], ["Ether extract", "Soxhlet", "Fats, oils, waxes, pigments"], ["Crude fibre", "Acid and alkali digestion", "Cellulose and part of lignin and hemicellulose"], ["NFE", "By difference", "Sugars, starch, hemicellulose"], ["NDF", "Neutral detergent", "Total cell wall"], ["ADF", "Acid detergent", "Cellulose plus lignin"]] },
  df: ["Proximate analysis: division of feed into moisture, ash, crude protein, ether extract, crude fibre and NFE.", "NDF: feed residue after neutral detergent, representing cell wall.", "NFE: the fraction obtained by difference after other fractions are subtracted."],
  tm: [["Kjeldahl", "Nitrogen method"], ["Soxhlet", "Fat extraction apparatus"], ["Muffle furnace", "High temperature furnace for ash"], ["NIRS", "Near infrared reflectance spectroscopy"], ["Lignin", "Indigestible plant polymer"], ["Hemicellulose", "NDF minus ADF"], ["Relative feed value", "Index from ADF and NDF"], ["Acid insoluble ash", "Silica and sand marker"]],
  st: ["Sample is dried.", "Ash is burned.", "Nitrogen is digested and titrated.", "Fat is extracted.", "Fibre is boiled in acid and alkali.", "NFE is calculated by difference."],
  ex: ["Feed label showing CP, EE, CF and ash.", "Silage tested for NDF and ADF.", "Adulteration check by acid insoluble ash."],
  k: ["Six fractions.", "Kjeldahl steps.", "NFE by difference.", "Van Soest.", "Limitations."],
  m: ["NFE is not measured; it is calculated. CF underestimates fibre. Always report on dry matter basis."],
  sa: [["How is NFE obtained?", "By subtracting moisture, ash, CP, EE and CF from 100."], ["What does ADF include?", "Cellulose and lignin."], ["Which apparatus is used for ether extract?", "Soxhlet apparatus."]],
  la: [["Describe the proximate analysis of feeds.", "Fractions, methods, principles, limitations."], ["Describe detergent fibre analysis and its advantages.", "NDF, ADF, ADL, calculations, uses."]],
  q: [["Crude protein factor is", ["6.25", "4.5", "5.0", "3.25"], 0, "N x 6.25."],
    ["Fat extraction uses", ["Soxhlet", "Kjeldahl", "Muffle", "Bomb"], 0, "Ether."],
    ["Hemicellulose is estimated as", ["NDF - ADF", "ADF - ADL", "DM - ash", "CF - EE"], 0, "Van Soest."],
    ["Acid insoluble ash indicates", ["Sand and silica", "Protein", "Fat", "Water"], 0, "Contamination."],
    ["NFE is obtained", ["By difference", "By titration", "By ashing", "By drying"], 0, "Calculated."]],
  f: [["CP", "Crude protein"], ["EE", "Ether extract"], ["CF", "Crude fibre"], ["NFE", "Nitrogen free extract"], ["NDF", "Neutral detergent fibre"]],
  d: ["Revise Practical I below.", "Read a feed label and recalculate DM basis.", "Compare CF and NDF of three feeds."],
});

topic("nutr", "Feed Technology: Feed Industry, Processing of Concentrates and Roughages, Complete Feeds and Quality Control", {
  o: ["Describe the structure of the Indian feed industry", "Describe processing methods for concentrates", "Describe processing methods for roughages", "Describe complete feeds, feed blocks and pelleting", "Describe feed quality control and storage"],
  i: "Feed technology converts raw ingredients into safe, stable, palatable and nutritionally balanced feed. India's feed industry has grown sharply with the poultry and dairy sectors; a veterinarian often advises on feed quality, storage and mill practice.",
  s: "Think of a feed mill as a flow: receive, check, clean, grind, mix, process, pack, store. Quality control is checking at every step.",
  n: [
    ["The feed industry", "Segments: poultry feed (the largest share), cattle and buffalo feed (compound cattle feed), pig feed, aqua feed, pet food, and premix and additive companies. Cooperative dairy unions and large private mills produce compound cattle feed; the poultry sector depends on integrators and feed mills. BIS standards exist for cattle feed (IS 2052), poultry feed (IS 1374) and other feeds; the Food Safety and Standards Authority and state rules govern feed safety, and the Livestock and Livestock Products Act and feed regulations are under development; check current rules. Raw material price swings of maize and soybean meal strongly affect the sector."],
    ["Feed mill flow", "Receiving and sampling raw materials, storage in silos or godowns, cleaning (magnets, sieves), grinding (hammer mill or roller mill), weighing and batching, mixing (horizontal ribbon or vertical mixer), conditioning with steam, pelleting or crumbling, cooling, sieving, coating (fat, vitamins), packing and dispatch. Mash feed is ground and mixed; pellets are compressed under steam through a die; crumbles are broken pellets for chicks. Records and traceability are part of quality systems (GMP, HACCP)."],
    ["Processing of concentrates", "Grinding: reduces particle size and improves digestion of grains in monogastrics; excess fine grinding causes ulcers in pigs and acidosis in ruminants. Pelleting: improves feed intake, reduces wastage and segregation, destroys some pathogens (Salmonella) with heat, but reduces some vitamins and enzymes. Extrusion and expansion: high heat and pressure, gelatinise starch, destroy antinutritional factors, used for pet and fish feeds. Roasting, flaking, steam rolling: for ruminant and calf feeds. Heat treatment of soybean meal inactivates trypsin inhibitors (underheating leaves them; overheating damages lysine). Solvent extraction versus expeller pressing for oilcakes. Soaking, germination and fermentation improve palatability and reduce antinutritional factors."],
    ["Processing of roughages", "Chaffing (cutting into small pieces) reduces wastage and improves intake, but too fine chaff speeds passage. Grinding and pelleting of straw (densification) helps transport. Soaking, steaming and boiling softens straw. Complete feed blocks and pellets combine roughage and concentrate (total mixed ration in compressed form), used for scarcity periods and transport of fodder. Densified feed blocks (urea molasses mineral blocks, UMMB) supply NPN and minerals to ruminants. Baling of hay and straw for storage and transport. Details of chemical and biological treatment are in the next chapter."],
    ["Complete feeds and total mixed ration", "A complete feed supplies all nutrients so that nothing else is needed. For ruminants, a total mixed ration (TMR) combines roughage, concentrate, minerals and additives mixed thoroughly so that each mouthful is balanced; it reduces selective feeding and helps rumen stability in high yielding cows. TMR needs mixer wagons and good ration records. Complete pelleted rations are used in sheep fattening and for transport."],
    ["Quality control", "Raw materials: visual inspection, odour, moisture, sieve tests, and checks for foreign matter, adulterants and mould; laboratory testing for proximate composition, aflatoxin, urea (for adulteration), and microbial counts. Finished feed: moisture, nutrient levels against label, pellet durability, mixing uniformity (coefficient of variation test), and absence of pathogens. Keep retained samples. Use quality assurance programmes. Reject feed with high aflatoxin or other toxins."],
    ["Storage and spoilage", "Store in clean, dry, ventilated godowns on pallets; stack away from walls; follow first in first out; control rodents, insects and mould. Moisture over 12 to 14 per cent raises mould risk (maize safe moisture about 12 to 14 per cent; check textbook). Rancidity of rice bran and fats is reduced with antioxidants. Fumigation should follow safety rules. Poor storage causes vitamin loss, mycotoxins and nutritional diseases."],
    ["Veterinary role in feed industry", "Advise on formulation, quality, safe use of additives and medicated feeds, withdrawal periods (residue prevention), mycotoxin management, biosecurity of the mill, and farm-level feeding. Medicated feed use must follow the law and prescription. Antibiotic growth promoters are restricted; check current regulations (some, such as colistin, are banned in India)."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Process", "Purpose", "Note"], r: [["Grinding", "Reduce particle size", "Too fine causes ulcers and acidosis"], ["Mixing", "Uniform distribution", "Test with CV of marker"], ["Pelleting", "Densify, reduce wastage", "Steam and heat; vitamin loss"], ["Extrusion", "Gelatinise starch, cook", "Pet and fish feed"], ["Chaffing", "Reduce waste of roughage", "Do not cut too fine"], ["UMMB", "Supply urea, molasses, minerals", "Introduce gradually"], ["TMR", "Mix all feeds", "Needs mixer and ration records"]] },
  df: ["Feed technology: science and practice of processing feed ingredients into usable feeds.", "Complete feed: feed supplying all nutrients without other supplement.", "TMR: mixture of roughage, concentrate and supplements fed as one ration."],
  tm: [["Mash", "Ground mixed feed"], ["Pellet", "Compressed feed through a die"], ["Crumble", "Broken pellets"], ["Premix", "Concentrated additive mix"], ["GMP", "Good manufacturing practice"], ["Hammer mill", "Grinding machine"], ["UMMB", "Urea molasses mineral block"], ["IS 2052", "BIS standard for cattle feed"]],
  st: ["Raw materials are received and checked.", "They are cleaned and ground.", "Batches are weighed and mixed.", "Feed is conditioned and pelleted if needed.", "It is packed with labels.", "It is stored and monitored."],
  ex: ["Broiler starter crumbles.", "UMMB for dry-season cattle.", "A dairy farm feeding TMR."],
  k: ["Mill flow.", "Pelleting.", "Roughage processing.", "TMR.", "Quality control."],
  m: ["Underheated soybean meal keeps trypsin inhibitors. Check current BIS and regulatory references for standards."],
  sa: [["What is a pellet?", "Feed compressed through a die after steam conditioning."], ["What is TMR?", "Total mixed ration, a mixture of all feeds fed together."], ["Why test mixer efficiency?", "To ensure each portion has the same nutrient content."]],
  la: [["Describe the processing of concentrates and roughages.", "Grinding, pelleting, extrusion, chaffing, blocks, effects."], ["Describe the working and quality control of a feed mill.", "Flow, tests, storage, standards."]],
  q: [["Feed compressed through a die is", ["Pellet", "Mash", "Meal", "Crumble"], 0, "Pelleting."],
    ["BIS standard for cattle feed is", ["IS 2052", "IS 1374", "IS 10500", "IS 4"], 0, "Compound feed."],
    ["Too fine grinding in pigs can cause", ["Gastric ulcers", "Rickets", "Anaemia", "Goitre"], 0, "Particle size effect."],
    ["Extrusion mainly", ["Gelatinises starch", "Adds water", "Removes protein", "Dries hay"], 0, "Pet and fish feed."],
    ["UMMB supplies", ["Urea, molasses and minerals", "Vitamins only", "Fat only", "Water"], 0, "Lick block."]],
  f: [["TMR", "Total mixed ration"], ["UMMB", "Urea molasses mineral block"], ["GMP", "Good manufacturing practice"], ["HACCP", "Hazard analysis and critical control points"], ["CV", "Coefficient of variation"]],
  d: ["Visit a feed mill.", "Read IS 2052 headings.", "Draw a mill flow chart."],
});

topic("nutr", "Improving the Nutritive Value of Inferior Roughages: Physical, Chemical and Biological Methods", {
  o: ["Explain why straws are poor feeds", "Describe physical treatments", "Describe chemical treatments such as alkali, ammonia and urea", "Describe biological treatments", "Compare methods and practical limits"],
  i: "Crop residues are the main bulk feed of Indian ruminants but they are lignified and low in protein. Treatments open the fibre or add nitrogen so microbes can digest more.",
  s: "The goal is to loosen the lignin-cellulose bond and add fermentable nitrogen, at a cost the farmer can pay.",
  n: [
    ["Why inferior roughages are poor", "Straws and stovers have high cell wall (70 to 80 per cent of DM), lignification, silica, low crude protein (2 to 5 per cent), low minerals and vitamins, low digestibility (40 to 50 per cent) and low intake. They give maintenance at best. Improvement raises digestibility, intake and nitrogen supply."],
    ["Physical methods", "Chopping or chaffing (improves intake, less waste), grinding (increases surface area, but speeds passage and may reduce digestibility in ruminants unless pelleted), soaking in water (softens), steaming or boiling (improves digestibility moderately), and pelleting or making blocks (easy transport). Irradiation and steam explosion are laboratory and industrial techniques of limited farm use. Physical methods mainly raise intake rather than digestibility."],
    ["Chemical methods: alkali treatment", "Sodium hydroxide (NaOH) treatment (Beckmann process) dissolves part of the hemicellulose-lignin bonds and raises digestibility by about 10 to 20 percentage units, but costs, handling hazard and sodium excess limit farm use. Calcium hydroxide (lime) and wood ash are safer, cheaper but less effective. Neutralisation or washing is sometimes done to reduce sodium."],
    ["Chemical methods: ammonia and urea treatment", "Urea-ammoniation is the preferred farm method in India. Urea is dissolved in water and sprinkled on chopped or whole straw; the straw is stacked, covered with plastic sheets and sealed airtight for a period (around 3 to 4 weeks in warm weather, longer in cold), during which urease from the straw hydrolyses urea to ammonia, which swells and loosens the fibre and adds nitrogen; the treated straw is aired for a day or two before feeding, and animals are gradually adapted. Typical recipes use about 4 kg urea in 40 to 50 litres of water per 100 kg of straw (check your textbook and ICAR extension manual; treat these as illustrative figures). Result: crude protein rises (to about 8 to 10 per cent), digestibility and intake increase, and milk and growth responses follow. Cautions: avoid use in young calves, keep free of mould, never feed immediately after opening, and prevent the animals from eating urea directly."],
    ["Biological methods", "Treatment with white rot fungi (Pleurotus, Phanerochaete, Trametes) degrades lignin but is slow and may consume digestible nutrients; solid state fermentation with fungal cultures and mushroom cultivation on straw (spent mushroom substrate can be fed). Enzymes: cellulase and xylanase preparations. Microbial inoculants and probiotics added to improve fibre digestion. Biological methods are at the research and small-scale stage in India, being friendlier to environment but variable."],
    ["Supplementation of roughage-based diets", "Besides treatment, improve straw diets by adding a nitrogen source (urea molasses block, oilcake, legume fodder), minerals, and a small amount of bypass protein or energy. Strategic supplementation raises intake and fibre digestion. Molasses with urea and minerals are the standard dry-season supplement. Combine with green leguminous fodder or tree leaves whenever possible."],
    ["Comparison of methods", "Physical: cheap, small gain. NaOH: big gain, hazardous and costly. Urea-ammoniation: practical and effective at village level. Biological: promising, slow. For exam, give two merits and two demerits for each class. The best answer ends with the advice: combine urea treated straw with green fodder and a mineral mixture."],
    ["Safety, ethics and cost", "Wear gloves when handling alkali or urea solution; keep chemicals away from children and animals; use plastic sheeting properly; avoid wasting treated straw; estimate cost per kg of extra digestible nutrient before recommending a method. Always demonstrate the method to farmers."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Method", "Effect", "Merits", "Limits"], r: [["Chopping", "Better intake", "Cheap", "No digestibility gain"], ["Soaking or steaming", "Softening", "Simple", "Small gain"], ["NaOH", "Breaks lignin bonds", "Large gain", "Costly, hazardous, sodium"], ["Urea-ammoniation", "Adds N, loosens fibre", "Village level, effective", "Needs sealing, not for calves"], ["White rot fungi", "Lignin degradation", "Natural", "Slow, loses DM"], ["UMMB supplement", "Provides N, minerals, energy", "Easy", "Only supplements"]] },
  df: ["Ammoniation: treatment of straw with ammonia, from urea or gas, to raise nitrogen and digestibility.", "Lignin: indigestible polymer binding plant cell walls.", "Urea-molasses mineral block: lick block giving NPN, energy and minerals."],
  tm: [["Beckmann process", "Alkali treatment of straw"], ["Urea ammoniation", "Treatment of straw with urea"], ["Lignocellulose", "Lignin and cellulose complex"], ["Pleurotus", "Oyster mushroom white rot fungus"], ["Silica", "Mineral in straw lowering digestibility"], ["Densification", "Compressing roughage"], ["Cellulase", "Fibre-digesting enzyme"], ["Urease", "Enzyme releasing ammonia from urea"]],
  st: ["Straw is chopped.", "Urea solution is sprinkled.", "Straw is stacked and sealed.", "Ammonia works on fibre.", "Straw is aired.", "Animals are slowly adapted."],
  ex: ["Paddy straw treated with urea.", "Wheat bhusa chopped before feeding.", "Mushroom grown on straw."],
  k: ["Why straw is poor.", "Physical versus chemical.", "Urea treatment steps.", "Biological methods.", "Supplements."],
  m: ["Urea treated straw is not for young calves. Ammonia smell must clear before feeding. Treat the quantities here as illustrative: use the ICAR extension recipe."],
  sa: [["What does ammoniation do to straw?", "Adds nitrogen and loosens the fibre to raise digestibility."], ["Why is NaOH treatment not popular?", "It is costly, hazardous to handle and adds sodium."], ["Give a biological method.", "Treatment with white rot fungi such as Pleurotus."]],
  la: [["Describe the methods for improving the nutritive value of straw.", "Physical, chemical, biological, merits, limits."], ["Describe urea-ammoniation of straw.", "Procedure, mechanism, effects, precautions."]],
  q: [["Practical village method for straw is", ["Urea treatment", "Irradiation", "NaOH 10 per cent", "Steam explosion"], 0, "Ammoniation."],
    ["Lignin is", ["Indigestible", "Fully digestible", "A vitamin", "A mineral"], 0, "Cell wall polymer."],
    ["Ammoniation raises", ["Crude protein and digestibility", "Only moisture", "Fat", "Silica"], 0, "N added."],
    ["White rot fungi act on", ["Lignin", "Starch", "Fat", "Vitamins"], 0, "Biological treatment."],
    ["Urea treated straw is unsuitable for", ["Young calves", "Adult cows", "Buffaloes", "Goats"], 0, "Rumen not developed."]],
  f: [["UMMB", "Urea molasses mineral block"], ["NaOH", "Sodium hydroxide"], ["NH3", "Ammonia"], ["SSF", "Solid state fermentation"], ["DM", "Dry matter"]],
  d: ["Do a urea treatment demonstration.", "Revise Biochemistry of lignin.", "Calculate cost of treated straw."],
});
