unit("lpm", "Unit 4 – Zoo animals production management");

topic("lpm", "Wild and Zoo Animals: Taxonomy, Status and Conservation of Wildlife in India, and Classification of Zoos, Sanctuaries and National Parks", {
  o: ["Outline the taxonomy of important zoo animals", "Describe the status and conservation practices of Indian wildlife", "Classify zoos and protected areas", "Describe the roles of national and international organisations", "Link to the Acts governing captive animals"],
  i: "A zoo veterinarian must know which species they are dealing with, how threatened it is and which laws and institutions govern it. This chapter gives the map before the details of housing, feeding and handling.",
  s: "Taxonomy tells what an animal is, conservation status tells how it is doing, and the law tells what you may do.",
  n: [
    ["Taxonomy of important zoo animals", "Mammals: carnivores (tiger, lion, leopard, snow leopard, clouded leopard, bears: sloth and Asiatic black, wild dog or dhole, wolf, hyena, jackal, small cats), primates (monkeys, langurs, macaques, apes), ungulates (elephant, rhinoceros, wild buffalo, gaur, deer: sambar, chital, barasingha, hog deer, antelopes: blackbuck, chinkara, nilgai, four-horned antelope; wild pig and wild goats and sheep), small mammals (pangolin, porcupine, otters, civets). Birds: ratites (emu, ostrich), raptors, waterfowl, pheasants, peafowl, hornbills, parrots. Reptiles: crocodiles (mugger, gharial, saltwater), turtles and tortoises, snakes and lizards. Understand the order and family level of the main species using your zoology text."],
    ["Status of wildlife in India", "India has a diverse fauna in varied habitats: Himalayas, Terai, forests of the Western and Eastern Ghats, central highlands, deserts, wetlands, mangroves and islands. Flagship species include tiger, Asiatic lion (Gir), Indian rhinoceros, elephant, snow leopard, gharial, and blackbuck. Species status follows the IUCN Red List categories (extinct, extinct in the wild, critically endangered, endangered, vulnerable, near threatened, least concern, data deficient). Verify the present category of each species."],
    ["Threats and conservation practices", "Threats: habitat loss and fragmentation, poaching and trade, human-wildlife conflict, disease, climate change, pollution, invasive species. Conservation practices: protected areas (national parks, sanctuaries, tiger reserves, biosphere reserves, conservation and community reserves), project-based approaches (Project Tiger, Project Elephant and others), anti-poaching, habitat restoration, translocation and reintroduction, conservation breeding, awareness and community involvement, and the use of wildlife corridors. Laws are summarised in the Acts chapter."],
    ["Classification of zoos", "Zoos are classified by the central authority by area, number of animals and facilities into categories such as large, medium, small, mini and so on (verify the current classes). Types by purpose: conventional zoos, safari parks, aviaries, aquaria, snake parks, deer parks, rescue and rehabilitation centres, and breeding centres. Modern zoos stress conservation, education, research and welfare more than display."],
    ["Sanctuaries, national parks and reserves", "A wildlife sanctuary protects wildlife and habitat with limited human activity permitted (such as certain rights of use); a national park has stricter protection with no grazing and no private rights; a tiger reserve has a core area and a buffer; a biosphere reserve is a zone for conservation and sustainable use; conservation reserve and community reserve link private and community lands. Management includes patrolling, fire protection, water holes, habitat improvement, census, monitoring, veterinary care and coexistence with the people on the fringes."],
    ["Management of livestock in fringe areas", "Livestock near protected areas can compete for grazing, spread disease to wildlife (foot and mouth disease, anthrax, rinderpest in the past, tuberculosis, canine distemper) and be preyed on by carnivores, causing conflict. Measures: vaccination of livestock near protected areas, grazing control, compensation for loss, predator-proof pens, fodder and water in villages, and health monitoring of livestock and wildlife together (One Health). Ban or control movement of livestock into the core areas as per law."],
    ["National and international organisations", "National: the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change, the Central Zoo Authority, the National Tiger Conservation Authority, the Wildlife Institute of India, the Zoological Survey of India, state forest departments and wildlife boards, the Wildlife Crime Control Bureau, and the Animal Welfare Board. International: IUCN, CITES (regulates trade in endangered species), WWF, the World Association of Zoos and Aquariums, the Convention on Biological Diversity, the Convention on Migratory Species, and the Ramsar Convention for wetlands. Check their present functions."],
    ["Veterinary role", "A zoo or wildlife veterinarian takes part in preventive health, quarantine, diet planning, enclosure review, environmental enrichment, handling and immobilisation, post-mortems and disease investigation, breeding programmes, reintroduction health screening, human-wildlife conflict response, and records. The veterinarian also advises on law, welfare and biosecurity."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Protected area or body", "Main aim", "Human activity", "Example (verify)"], r: [["National park", "Strict protection of wildlife and habitat", "Very limited", "Many across states"], ["Wildlife sanctuary", "Protect animals and habitat", "Some rights allowed", "Many across states"], ["Tiger reserve", "Protect tigers with core and buffer", "Core is inviolate", "Project Tiger sites"], ["Biosphere reserve", "Conservation and sustainable use", "Zoned use", "Nilgiri, Sundarbans"], ["Zoo", "Ex situ conservation, education, research", "Visitors under rules", "Recognised by the Central Zoo Authority"]] },
  df: ["Ex situ conservation: conservation outside the natural habitat.", "In situ conservation: conservation in the natural habitat.", "Sanctuary: area protecting wildlife with limited human use.", "CITES: convention regulating international trade in endangered species."],
  tm: [["IUCN", "International Union for Conservation of Nature"], ["CITES", "Trade convention"], ["CZA", "Central Zoo Authority"], ["NTCA", "National Tiger Conservation Authority"], ["Corridor", "Link between habitats"], ["Translocation", "Moving animals to another site"], ["Flagship species", "Species representing conservation"], ["Biosphere reserve", "Conservation and use zone"]],
  st: ["Identify the species.", "Check its status.", "Know the law.", "Plan housing and care.", "Plan breeding or release.", "Coordinate with authorities."],
  ex: ["Tiger reserve with core and buffer.", "Gharial breeding centre.", "Vaccination of village cattle near a park."],
  k: ["Zoo animal groups.", "IUCN categories.", "In situ and ex situ.", "Zoo and park types.", "Organisations and their roles."],
  m: ["No formulas. Learn the categories, the types of area and the role of each organisation."],
  sa: [["Differentiate national park and sanctuary.", "A national park gives stricter protection with no private rights; a sanctuary allows some limited human use."], ["What is CITES?", "A convention regulating international trade in endangered species."], ["What is ex situ conservation?", "Conservation outside the natural habitat, as in zoos."]],
  la: [["Describe the status and conservation practices of wildlife in India.", "Diversity, threats, protected areas, projects, community, law."], ["Describe the classification of zoos and the organisations involved in captive animals.", "Types of zoos, authority roles, international bodies."]],
  q: [["The agency that recognises zoos in India is the", ["Central Zoo Authority", "Dairy Board", "Poultry Board", "Wool Board"], 0, "Zoo regulation."],
    ["Trade in endangered species is regulated by", ["CITES", "WTO only", "UNICEF", "IMF"], 0, "International convention."],
    ["Protection in situ means", ["In the natural habitat", "In a zoo", "In a lab", "In a freezer"], 0, "Habitat conservation."],
    ["A tiger reserve has", ["Core and buffer zones", "No zones", "Only a market", "Only farm"], 0, "Zoning."],
    ["The gharial is a", ["Crocodilian", "Bird", "Deer", "Monkey"], 0, "Reptile."]],
  f: [["CZA", "Central Zoo Authority"], ["NTCA", "National Tiger Conservation Authority"], ["WII", "Wildlife Institute of India"], ["WCCB", "Wildlife Crime Control Bureau"], ["IUCN", "International Union for Conservation of Nature"]],
  d: ["List the protected areas of your state.", "Make a table of five species with IUCN status.", "Visit a nearby zoo."],
  vf: ["IUCN categories of species, names of institutions and zoo classes change; verify from current official sources. This chapter outlines the structure only."]
});
