topic("lpt", "Cream, Butter and Ghee: Manufacture, Quality and Defects", {
  o: ["Describe the manufacture of cream", "Describe the manufacture of butter", "Describe the manufacture of ghee", "Describe the quality tests and defects", "Describe packaging and storage"],
  i: "Cream, butter and ghee are milk fat products, and the Indian kitchen depends on ghee. The processes show how fat is separated, concentrated, stabilised and purified.",
  s: "Butter is a water-in-oil emulsion made by churning cream; ghee is the pure fat left after removing water and solids by heat.",
  n: [
    ["Cream: types and standards", "Cream is the fat-rich layer or fraction of milk obtained by gravity or centrifugal separation. Types: table cream, whipping cream (needs about 30 per cent or more fat; verify), double cream, and cream for butter making (about 30 to 40 per cent fat). Standards of fat per cent are in the food safety regulations. Cream for butter and ghee should be fresh and handled with care: pasteurise and cool fast."],
    ["Separation of cream", "Gravity creaming is slow and used in small households. Centrifugal separators use rotation: skim milk goes out of one outlet and cream out of another. Efficiency depends on temperature (about 40 to 45 degrees C or more), speed, flow rate and the size of the fat globules. The fat per cent of the cream is adjusted by a cream screw. Skim milk is a by-product with about 0.05 to 0.1 per cent fat (verify) and is used in many products. Test the skim milk for fat to check the efficiency."],
    ["Butter: principles and process", "Butter is a water-in-oil emulsion with at least 80 per cent fat (the legal minimum; verify), up to 16 per cent water, 1 to 2 per cent salt (in salted butter) and milk solids. Steps: reception and grading of cream; neutralisation of high-acid cream (for sour cream butter if needed); pasteurisation at a high temperature (about 90 to 95 degrees C for several seconds; verify) to destroy enzymes and microbes and improve keeping quality; cooling and ageing (holding at about 4 to 8 degrees C for some hours to crystallise part of the fat, giving good churning and texture); churning (fat globule membranes break in the agitation, fat clumps into butter grains and buttermilk is released); washing of the grains with cold water; working (kneading to expel water and form a continuous fat phase and a uniform moisture dispersion); salting if required; packing. Continuous butter making (Fritz process) is common in large plants."],
    ["Types of butter", "Sweet cream butter (from fresh cream), ripened cream butter (cream fermented with a lactic starter; richer flavour; typical of Europe), salted and unsalted (white) butter, table butter (white butter in India), whey butter, desi or makkhan (churned from dahi), lactic butter, and spreads and low-fat spreads. Indian white butter from dahi churning is common at home and in small dairies."],
    ["Defects of butter and remedies", "Flavour defects: rancid (lipase action), cheesy, fishy, oily or metallic (copper and iron; oxidation of fat), stale, tallowy, yeast and mould taint, and taints from feed (garlic, onion, weeds). Body and texture defects: sticky, weak and greasy butter, crumbly or brittle (cold), leaky (too much free water), sandy (salt crystals). Colour defects: mottled (uneven salt or working) and streaky. Prevent by good quality cream, correct pasteurisation, cold-chain, clean equipment (avoid copper and iron), controlled churning temperature, proper working, adequate salt dissolving and good packaging."],
    ["Ghee: definition and methods", "Ghee is the clarified milk fat made from butter, cream or dahi by heating to remove water, with a legal minimum of fat (not less than about 99.5 per cent; verify) and low moisture. Methods: (1) desi (traditional) method: dahi is churned to give butter, which is heated slowly to boil off the water, with the residue (ghee residue) browned, then the clear fat is decanted; (2) creamery butter method: butter melted and heated; (3) direct cream method: heated cream to evaporate the water and remove the solids; (4) pre-stratification method: melted butter is settled to separate the water and curd, then heated to give a good flavour. The temperature at the end of boiling (about 110 to 120 degrees C; verify) controls the flavour and keeping quality. Filter or decant, cool and pack. Ghee flavour comes from the Maillard reaction and the lactones and carbonyls formed on heating."],
    ["Quality and defects of ghee", "Granulation of ghee (grainy texture) is normal and depends on slow cooling and fat composition; buffalo ghee has larger crystals. Defects: rancidity (hydrolytic, by lipase or moisture), oxidative rancidity, smoky or burnt flavour, tallowy flavour from oxidation, mould growth in the presence of moisture, and adulteration with vegetable oils and animal fats. Tests: Reichert-Meissl and Polenske values (indicate the share of volatile fatty acids; lower in adulterated ghee), Baudouin test for vanaspati (sesame oil marker; verify the current test), refractive index, free fatty acids, peroxide value, moisture content. Use the standard methods in your practical manual; use of vanaspati is legally controlled."],
    ["Packaging, storage and by-products", "Pack butter in parchment, aluminium foil laminates or tubs and store at below about 5 degrees C (cold store) or frozen for months. Ghee is packed in tins, glass jars, pouches or laminated cartons, away from light and air, in a cool dry place; storing ghee in copper or iron vessels hastens oxidation. By-products: buttermilk, ghee residue (used in sweets and as a feed), and skim milk. Pay attention to fat losses and yield: about 1 kg of ghee needs about 25 to 30 litres of cow milk or about 15 to 18 litres of buffalo milk (verify)."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Product", "Fat (legal minimum approx.)", "Key step", "Typical defect"], r: [["Cream", "Varies by type", "Centrifugal separation", "Souring"], ["Butter", "80 per cent", "Churning of ripened or sweet cream and working", "Rancid, cheesy, metallic flavour"], ["Desi butter", "Lower and variable", "Churning of dahi", "Short shelf life"], ["Ghee", "About 99.5 per cent", "Heating butter or cream to remove water", "Rancidity, granulation issues"], ["Skim milk", "About 0.05 to 0.1 per cent", "By-product of separation", "Fat loss"], ["Buttermilk", "Low", "By-product of churning", "Spoilage"]] },
  df: ["Churning: agitation that breaks the fat emulsion to form butter.", "Ghee: clarified milk fat prepared by heating butter, cream or dahi.", "Overrun in butter: increase in butter weight over the fat weight, not air as in ice cream.", "Reichert-Meissl value: measure of volatile fatty acids in fat."],
  tm: [["Cream", "Fat-rich fraction of milk"], ["Churning", "Making butter by agitation"], ["Working", "Kneading butter"], ["Ghee residue", "Residue after ghee making"], ["Granulation", "Crystalline texture"], ["RM value", "Reichert-Meissl value"], ["Buttermilk", "Liquid after churning"], ["Ripening", "Fermenting cream"]],
  st: ["Separate cream.", "Pasteurise and age.", "Churn.", "Wash and work.", "Pack or heat to ghee.", "Test and store."],
  ex: ["Table butter from sweet cream.", "Desi ghee from buffalo butter.", "Butter defect: metallic flavour."],
  k: ["Cream separation.", "Butter steps.", "80 per cent fat.", "Ghee methods.", "Adulteration tests."],
  m: ["Yield of butter % = weight of butter / weight of cream x 100. Fat balance: fat in cream = fat in butter + fat in buttermilk. Ghee yield from butter about 80 to 85 per cent (verify)."],
  sa: [["What is the minimum fat content of butter?", "About 80 per cent."], ["What is churning?", "Agitation of cream that breaks the emulsion and forms butter grains."], ["Name two tests for the purity of ghee.", "Reichert-Meissl value and Baudouin test."]],
  la: [["Describe the manufacture of butter and its defects.", "Cream grading, pasteurising, ageing, churning, washing, working, packing; defects and remedies."], ["Describe the methods of making ghee and the tests for its purity.", "Desi, creamery butter, direct cream; flavour; RM value, Baudouin test."]],
  q: [["Butter is a", ["Water-in-oil emulsion", "Oil-in-water emulsion", "Pure oil", "Pure water"], 0, "After inversion."],
    ["Minimum fat in butter is about", ["80 per cent", "20 per cent", "50 per cent", "99 per cent"], 0, "Legal standard."],
    ["Ghee is made by", ["Heating butter or cream", "Freezing milk", "Fermenting meat", "Churning eggs"], 0, "Clarification."],
    ["Metallic flavour in butter arises from", ["Copper and iron contamination", "Salt", "Cold", "Water"], 0, "Catalysts of oxidation."],
    ["Buttermilk is a by-product of", ["Churning", "Cheese pressing", "Egg breaking", "Slaughter"], 0, "Butter making."]],
  f: [["RM", "Reichert-Meissl"], ["FFA", "Free fatty acids"], ["PV", "Peroxide value"], ["SMP", "Skim milk powder"], ["CF", "Correction factor"]],
  d: ["Make butter and ghee in the lab.", "Calculate fat balance.", "Compare desi and creamery ghee."],
  vf: ["Legal fat and moisture limits, temperatures, milk requirement per kg of ghee, and analytical constants are textbook or regulatory values that vary; verify in your textbook and the current regulations. Test procedures and reagent strengths are in your practical manual; none are given."]
});
