topic("gyn", "Fertilisation and Its Aberrations, Fertilisation Failure and Embryonic Mortality", {
  o: ["Describe the events of fertilisation", "Describe aberrations of fertilisation", "Describe the causes of fertilisation failure", "Describe embryonic mortality: incidence, causes, diagnosis and prevention", "Differentiate early and late embryonic death"],
  i: "Most reproductive loss is invisible: eggs that never get fertilised and embryos that die before anyone knows. This chapter explains where the losses come from.",
  s: "Fertilisation failure and embryonic death together account for the bulk of the loss between service and calving.",
  n: [
    ["Events of fertilisation", "Sperm reach the ampulla, undergo capacitation and the acrosome reaction, penetrate the cumulus and the zona pellucida (binding by sperm surface proteins to zona proteins such as ZP3), and fuse with the oocyte membrane. Entry of the sperm triggers a calcium wave (oocyte activation), cortical granule release (the zona reaction that blocks polyspermy), completion of the second meiotic division, formation of the male and female pronuclei, and syngamy (fusion of the genetic material), giving a zygote. The first cleavage divisions follow, and the embryo reaches the uterus at the morula to blastocyst stage."],
    ["Early embryonic development", "In the cow the zygote divides to a 2-cell stage at about 1 day, 8 to 16 cells around day 3 to 4, morula on days 5 to 6, blastocyst on days 7 to 8, hatching from the zona on days 9 to 10, elongation of the conceptus on days 13 to 16 (it secretes interferon tau), and attachment around day 19 to 22 (timings approximate; verify). The embryo depends on uterine milk until implantation, so uterine health is critical."],
    ["Aberrations of fertilisation", "Polyspermy (more than one sperm penetrates; the zygote dies; more common in the pig), failure of oocyte activation, digyny and other chromosome anomalies (triploidy, tetraploidy), parthenogenesis, ageing of the oocyte (fertilisation of an aged egg gives a high rate of early death), ageing of sperm in the tract, and fertilisation without proper cleavage. Abnormal chromosome complements are a major cause of early embryonic losses."],
    ["Causes of fertilisation failure", "Female factors: ovulatory failure, tubal blockage (salpingitis, adhesions), poor timing of insemination (too early or too late), oocyte defects, endocrine imbalance, infection, heat stress, and age. Male factors: low sperm numbers or motility, abnormal sperm, poor semen handling for AI, wrong thawing, infection, or poor libido. Management: errors of insemination, wrong site of deposition, unhygienic technique. Embryonic stage: the first week after fertilisation is when most fertilisation failures or very early death occur."],
    ["Embryonic mortality: incidence and types", "Bovine fertilisation rate is often high (about 85 to 90 per cent) but about 20 to 40 per cent of the embryos die by day 16 to 18, often in the first week (verify). Early embryonic death (before day 15 to 17 in cattle, before maternal recognition) leads to a normal return to heat at about 18 to 24 days; late embryonic death (about day 17 to 42) gives a delayed or irregular return, often 25 to 35 days; foetal death after about day 42 is classified as abortion or resorption, depending on the species. Because the recognition of early death is hard on the farm, a high return-to-service interval in a herd is a clue."],
    ["Causes of embryonic mortality", "Genetic and chromosomal abnormalities of the embryo; hormonal causes (low progesterone, inadequate luteal function), uterine infection and inflammation (endometritis), heat stress (elevated body temperature in the first week is damaging), nutrition (energy deficit, excess rumen degradable protein with high urea; mineral deficiency), infectious agents (Campylobacter, Trichomonas, Brucella, BVD virus, IBR, Leptospira, Neospora, and others; see infectious infertility chapter), toxins and mycotoxins, stress and handling, immunological factors, and old oocytes or sperm. Repeat breeders often have embryonic mortality as a cause."],
    ["Diagnosis, treatment and prevention", "Diagnosis uses early pregnancy diagnosis by ultrasound (about day 28 to 30) followed by recheck, milk or blood progesterone and pregnancy-associated glycoproteins to detect loss, and the return-to-service intervals. Treatment is cause-specific: improve nutrition, treat uterine infection, control heat stress (shade, sprinklers, fans, cooling), vaccinate against infectious causes as per veterinary advice, avoid stress in the first weeks, and in some cases support the luteal phase hormonally (the choice and dose belong to the pharmacology and clinical notes). Prevention is a matter of herd management and keeping good records."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Stage (cow, approx.)", "Day", "Event", "Typical loss pattern"], r: [["Fertilisation", "Day 0 to 1", "Syngamy", "Failure to fertilise"], ["Cleavage", "Day 1 to 5", "Morula forms", "Chromosomal losses"], ["Blastocyst", "Day 7 to 9", "Enters uterus, hatches", "Uterine environment"], ["Elongation", "Day 13 to 16", "Interferon tau", "Failure of recognition"], ["Attachment", "Day 19 to 22", "Placentation begins", "Late embryonic death"], ["Foetal stage", "After about day 42", "Organs formed", "Abortion"]] },
  df: ["Syngamy: fusion of the male and female pronuclei.", "Polyspermy: fertilisation by more than one sperm.", "Early embryonic death: death before maternal recognition of pregnancy.", "Zona reaction: block to polyspermy after sperm penetration."],
  tm: [["Zona pellucida", "Egg coat"], ["Syngamy", "Pronuclei fusion"], ["Morula", "Solid ball of cells"], ["Blastocyst", "Hollow embryo"], ["Hatching", "Exit from zona"], ["Polyspermy", "Too many sperm"], ["PAG", "Pregnancy-associated glycoprotein"], ["Conceptus", "Embryo with membranes"]],
  st: ["Sperm bind and penetrate.", "Egg activation.", "Pronuclei fuse.", "Cleavage.", "Blastocyst and elongation.", "Maternal recognition."],
  ex: ["Return to heat at 28 days.", "Heat stress in the first week.", "BVD related early loss."],
  k: ["Steps of fertilisation.", "Polyspermy block.", "Timing of embryo stages.", "Early and late embryonic death.", "Causes and prevention."],
  m: ["Embryonic loss % = (fertilised eggs - viable embryos) / fertilised eggs x 100. Numbers in the text are approximate."],
  sa: [["What is polyspermy?", "Entry of more than one sperm into the oocyte."], ["What is early embryonic death?", "Loss of the embryo before maternal recognition of pregnancy."], ["Name two infectious causes of embryonic loss.", "Campylobacter and Trichomonas."]],
  la: [["Describe the events of fertilisation and the causes of fertilisation failure.", "Capacitation, zona penetration, activation, syngamy; female, male and management causes."], ["Describe the incidence, causes, diagnosis and prevention of embryonic mortality.", "Types, timing, causes, ultrasound, progesterone, nutrition, heat stress, infection."]],
  q: [["The block to polyspermy is the", ["Zona reaction", "Luteolysis", "Capacitation", "Ovulation"], 0, "Cortical granules."],
    ["Return to heat at 25 to 35 days after AI suggests", ["Late embryonic death", "Normal pregnancy", "Pyometra", "Anoestrus"], 0, "Delayed return."],
    ["Heat stress in the first week", ["Increases embryo loss", "Improves fertility", "Has no effect", "Stops cycles"], 0, "Embryo sensitivity."],
    ["Syngamy is", ["Fusion of pronuclei", "Ovulation", "Cleavage", "Hatching"], 0, "Zygote formation."],
    ["Interferon tau is produced by the", ["Conceptus", "Pituitary", "CL", "Mammary gland"], 0, "Ruminants."]],
  f: [["ZP", "Zona pellucida"], ["PAG", "Pregnancy-associated glycoprotein"], ["EED", "Early embryonic death"], ["LED", "Late embryonic death"], ["IFN-t", "Interferon tau"]],
  d: ["Chart embryo stages by day.", "Review herd return intervals.", "List causes of embryonic loss."],
  vf: ["Fertilisation rate, embryonic loss percentages and day-wise stages are textbook ranges and differ by report; verify. Hormonal support doses are not given."]
});
