topic("gyn", "Ultrasonography in Farm and Pet Animal Reproduction: Principle, Procedure and Applications", {
  o: ["Explain the physical principle of ultrasound", "Describe equipment and technique", "Describe findings in the non-pregnant tract", "Describe findings in pregnancy", "Describe applications and limitations"],
  i: "Ultrasound turned reproductive diagnosis from touch and guesswork into sight. A transrectal probe can show a follicle, a heartbeat and a cyst in minutes.",
  s: "Echoes returning from tissue interfaces are converted into pictures; fluid is black, tissue is grey and bone or gas is bright.",
  n: [
    ["Physical principle", "A transducer containing piezoelectric crystals produces sound waves of high frequency (above human hearing) when electrically stimulated, and converts returning echoes into electrical signals. Sound travels through tissue, and part is reflected at interfaces of different acoustic impedance. The time to return gives depth and the intensity gives brightness (B-mode; brightness mode). Fluid does not reflect, so is anechoic (black); soft tissue is hypoechoic to echogenic (grey); bone and gas reflect strongly and cast shadows or reverberation. Higher frequency gives better resolution but less depth: about 5 to 7.5 MHz for transrectal use in cattle and horses; about 7.5 to 10 MHz and above for small animals and early pregnancy (verify)."],
    ["Equipment and modes", "Machine with a linear (rectal) probe for large animals, a convex or sector probe for the abdomen of small animals, gel, a probe holder, and a cover for hygiene. Modes: B-mode (standard grey scale), M-mode (motion, for heartbeat), and Doppler (colour or spectral; blood flow in the uterine artery and in the foetus). Portable battery-powered machines are used in farms; shade the screen from sunlight. Clean and disinfect the probe between animals."],
    ["Technique in large animals", "Restrain the animal, remove faeces from the rectum, apply gel on the probe, insert it gently through the rectum, and scan the tract from the cervix to the uterine bifurcation, along each horn to the ovary. Identify the uterus (cross-section as a round structure with a folded endometrium), the ovary with follicles (anechoic round structures) and the CL (grey tissue with or without a small cavity). Record the findings and measure the follicle and embryonic structures with the on-screen callipers. Be systematic and take care not to injure the rectal wall."],
    ["Findings in the non-pregnant tract", "Follicles are anechoic and measured to find the dominant follicle; the CL appears as echogenic tissue (a cavity may be present in the early CL); cystic ovaries (follicular cyst with a thin wall; luteal cyst with a thick wall), ovarian tumours (large mass with mixed echogenicity); uterine fluid (anechoic or echogenic), pus in pyometra (echogenic fluid), thickened endometrium in endometritis or cystic changes, and uterine tumours or masses. Cycle stage can be judged from the combination of the ovarian structures and uterine tone; the follicular wave can be followed by serial scans."],
    ["Findings in pregnancy", "In the cow: the embryonic vesicle from about day 20 to 25, the embryo proper with a heartbeat at day 25 to 30, amnion, allantois and placentomes (from about day 35 to 40), foetal sex determination by the genital tubercle at about day 55 to 60 (verify); in the mare: the vesicle from day 11 to 14, heartbeat from day 22 to 25, and gender at about day 59 to 70; in the sheep and goat: from about day 25 to 30 transabdominal and counting of foetuses at about 45 to 90 days; in the sow: from day 21 to 28; in the bitch: from day 20 to 25 after ovulation, foetal heartbeat from about day 25 to 30 and count in mid pregnancy. Foetal viability is judged by the heartbeat and movements; foetal death is shown by absence of heartbeat, cloudy fluid, and collapse."],
    ["Applications in practice", "Early and accurate pregnancy diagnosis; confirmation of ovulation and timing of AI; follicle monitoring in the mare and in ovsynch protocols; diagnosis of cysts, tumours, pyometra, and mummified or macerated foetuses; twin diagnosis in the mare and its reduction; sexing of foetuses; ovum pick-up and embryo transfer; assessment of the male tract and testis; and use for evaluation of foetal wellbeing in small animals. Doppler measures blood flow to the uterus and ovary in research and clinical cases."],
    ["Limitations and safety", "Depends on operator skill, machine quality and animal factors (gas, faeces or a thick uterus). Early scans may miss pregnancy: repeat when in doubt. A positive scan at day 28 requires a recheck since early embryonic death is common. Ultrasound is safe for the animal and operator at diagnostic levels, but avoid unnecessary scanning of the early embryo with a hard probe and take care with equipment. Interpretation needs training and comparison with the tract of slaughterhouse specimens."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Structure", "Appearance", "Typical frequency or time (verify)", "Clinical use"], r: [["Follicle", "Anechoic, round", "7.5 MHz probe", "Timing AI"], ["Corpus luteum", "Echogenic tissue, may have a cavity", "Day 3 onwards", "Cycle stage"], ["Uterine fluid", "Anechoic or echogenic", "Any time", "Endometritis, pyometra"], ["Embryo (cow)", "Vesicle with embryo, heartbeat", "Day 25 to 30", "Early pregnancy"], ["Foetal sex (cow)", "Genital tubercle position", "About day 55 to 60", "Sexing"], ["Cyst", "Thin or thick walled cavity", "Any time", "Diagnosis and treatment choice"]] },
  df: ["Anechoic: no echoes, appears black, as in fluid.", "B-mode: brightness mode that gives a grey-scale image.", "Doppler: ultrasound technique that detects the motion of blood.", "Transducer: probe that sends and receives ultrasound."],
  tm: [["Anechoic", "Black fluid"], ["Echogenic", "Bright"], ["Hypoechoic", "Dark grey"], ["Transducer", "Probe"], ["B-mode", "Grey scale image"], ["M-mode", "Motion mode"], ["Doppler", "Blood flow"], ["Gel", "Coupling medium"]],
  st: ["Prepare the animal and machine.", "Insert probe gently.", "Scan systematically.", "Identify structures.", "Measure and record.", "Recheck if needed."],
  ex: ["Day 28 pregnancy scan in a cow.", "Follicle size in a mare before ovulation.", "Pyometra in a bitch."],
  k: ["Echo principle.", "Frequency and depth.", "Appearance of fluid, CL, follicle.", "Pregnancy milestones.", "Limitations."],
  m: ["No calculations. Embryo and organ sizes are measured on screen. Days are approximate."],
  sa: [["What does anechoic mean?", "No echoes returned; appears black, as in fluid."], ["Name the mode that shows heartbeat movement.", "M-mode."], ["Why repeat a scan at day 28?", "Because early embryonic loss is common."]],
  la: [["Explain the principle and technique of ultrasonography in cattle reproduction.", "Piezoelectric principle, probe, gel, transrectal technique, structures seen."], ["Describe the applications of ultrasound in farm and pet animal reproduction.", "Pregnancy, ovarian structures, cysts, pyometra, twins, sexing, embryo transfer, male tract."]],
  q: [["Fluid appears on ultrasound as", ["Black", "White", "Grey", "Red"], 0, "Anechoic."],
    ["Transrectal scanning in cattle uses a", ["Linear probe", "Cardiac probe only", "Microscope", "Thermometer"], 0, "Standard method."],
    ["Heartbeat is shown by", ["M-mode or B-mode motion", "Doppler only", "X-ray", "Palpation"], 0, "Cardiac motion."],
    ["Bone and gas cause", ["Shadowing or reverberation", "Clear pictures", "Colour", "Heat"], 0, "Strong reflectors."],
    ["Higher frequency gives", ["Better resolution, less depth", "Worse resolution", "More depth only", "No image"], 0, "Trade-off."]],
  f: [["MHz", "Megahertz"], ["B", "Brightness mode"], ["M", "Motion mode"], ["CL", "Corpus luteum"], ["TRUS", "Transrectal ultrasonography"]],
  d: ["Practise on abattoir tracts.", "Draw the echo appearance of structures.", "Record a scan log."],
  vf: ["Probe frequencies and the day numbers for scan findings are textbook ranges and depend on machine and species; verify. Operator training under supervision is required."]
});
