topic("gyn", "Postpartum Disorders: Retention of Foetal Membranes, Uterine Prolapse, Metabolic Diseases of the Puerperium, Haemorrhage, Subinvolution, Injuries, Uterine Infections and Resumption of Ovarian Activity", {
  o: ["Describe retention of foetal membranes", "Describe total uterine prolapse and its management", "Describe puerperal metabolic diseases", "Describe postpartum haemorrhage, subinvolution and injuries", "Describe postpartum uterine infection and return to cyclicity"],
  i: "The hours and days after calving are where most dairy cows are lost. A calm, clean approach to the postpartum cow saves her uterus, her milk and her next pregnancy.",
  s: "Postpartum disorders are connected: a difficult birth leads to retained membranes, metritis, ketosis and a late return to heat.",
  n: [
    ["Retention of foetal membranes (RFM)", "RFM is failure to expel the placenta within a normal period: more than about 12 hours in the cow and buffalo (verify), 3 hours in the mare (an emergency), and a few hours in other species. Incidence in the cow is about 3 to 12 per cent, higher after abortion, twins, dystocia, induction, hypocalcaemia, infections and selenium or vitamin E deficiency. Mechanism: failed separation of the foetal cotyledons from the maternal crypts (placentomes), uterine inertia and immune factors. The placenta hangs from the vulva and decays within days, with a bad smell, and may lead to metritis; many cows show no other signs; others have fever, depression and drop in milk."],
    ["Management of RFM", "Do not pull the membranes forcibly; do not manually remove adherent membranes, because of haemorrhage and infection; clean the perineum, keep the cow under observation, support with systemic treatment as per the veterinarian (antimicrobials when there is fever or metritis, and nutrition), use hormones (oxytocin early after calving; PGF2 alpha for luteolysis in selected cases) as per the clinician, and let the membranes separate and drop naturally in a few days. Gentle traction is acceptable when the membranes are loose. In the mare, treat retention at once (oxytocin and removal with care) because of the risk of metritis, laminitis and toxaemia. Prevention: correct nutrition and minerals (selenium, vitamin E), prevention of hypocalcaemia and hygiene during calving, and calm management."],
    ["Total uterine prolapse", "After calving the uterus is turned inside out and hangs from the vulva (the endometrium with caruncles is exposed in the cow); more common in cows (more so in buffalo) and ewes, after dystocia, hypocalcaemia, retained membranes and excessive traction. The risks are shock, haemorrhage, rupture of the uterine artery, injury, infection, and bladder entrapment. Treatment is an emergency: epidural anaesthesia, restrain (cow in sternal position with the hindquarters raised or standing), cleaning with warm antiseptic water, removal of adherent membranes gently, reduction of oedema if required, replacing the uterus by pushing it back, starting at the cervix, and testing that it is fully inverted (use of a bottle or a hand), a retention suture (Buhner), and supportive therapy (fluids, calcium if indicated, antibiotics and oxytocin as per the clinician). Rarely, amputation of the uterus is needed in damaged organs. Prognosis is good if replaced early; delayed cases have a poor prognosis. Recurrence is low in cows; cull repeated cases."],
    ["Metabolic diseases of the puerperium", "Parturient hypocalcaemia (milk fever): low blood calcium at or soon after calving, with muscle weakness, cold ears, recumbency, bloating and coma; contributes to uterine inertia, dystocia, retained membranes, prolapse and mastitis; the prevention lies in dietary management of the dry cow (the details are in the medicine and nutrition notes). Ketosis: negative energy balance and fatty liver in early lactation, with the smell of acetone, reduced milk and appetite, and delayed oestrus. Hypomagnesaemia, hypophosphataemia, and downer cow syndrome. Postpartum haemoglobinuria (bovine, associated with phosphorus deficiency in buffalo in India: weak, red urine and anaemia). Treatments and doses are in your medicine notes."],
    ["Postpartum haemorrhage", "Bleeding from uterine or vaginal vessels after calving: the rupture of the uterine artery (middle uterine artery) at the time of foetal expulsion (fatal internal bleeding with pale mucous membranes, a rapid weak pulse and a progressive weakness) or from tears of the birth canal (visible bleeding). Management: restrain, pack and suture accessible tears, give fluid and supportive therapy, and avoid manipulating the uterus. Prognosis for internal haemorrhage is poor. Prevent with careful handling of dystocia."],
    ["Subinvolution of placental sites", "Delayed healing and shrinking of the placental attachment sites: seen in the bitch (subinvolution of placental sites: persistent blood-tinged discharge for weeks after whelping, with the animal otherwise well) and in the cow, where the uterus remains large and flabby with prolonged lochia and delayed return to oestrus. It is often self-limiting; treat associated infection; control in the bitch is mainly by observation, and ovariohysterectomy is used where bleeding persists. In cows, regular examination and hormone support are used as directed."],
    ["Injuries incidental to parturition", "Vulval and vaginal laceration, perineal tear (first, second and third degree), cervical tear, uterine rupture (during the delivery with excessive force or after torsion; a Caesarean or laparotomy is needed; the prognosis is guarded), haematoma, vaginal bruising, rectal injury, fractures of the pelvis, nerve damage (obturator paralysis) and rupture of the prepubic tendon. Prevention: gentle technique, lubrication and recognition of limits. Treatment: surgical repair at the proper time and supportive therapy."],
    ["Postpartum uterine infection and the return to cyclicity", "Puerperal metritis (within 10 days of calving, foul watery red-brown discharge, fever, a toxic cow), clinical endometritis (after 21 days with purulent discharge), pyometra, and subclinical endometritis (see earlier chapter). Treat with systemic therapy and supportive care and use PGF2 alpha when a CL is present. Resumption of ovarian activity: Fertility depends on early cyclicity, which requires a good energy balance, health and a clean uterus. Typical herd goals: most cows should have ovulated by day 21 to 25 and have shown a heat by day 40 to 60 (verify). Record the first heat date and examine cows without a heat by day 45 to 60."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Condition", "Time", "Key signs", "Management principle"], r: [["RFM", "Over 12 h (cow)", "Membranes hanging, foul smell", "Do not pull, supportive treatment"], ["Total uterine prolapse", "Within hours of calving", "Inverted uterus outside", "Epidural, replace, suture, supportive"], ["Milk fever", "Around calving", "Weakness, recumbency", "Calcium therapy by veterinarian"], ["Postpartum haemorrhage", "At calving", "Pallor, weak pulse", "Control, suture, fluids"], ["Subinvolution", "Weeks postpartum", "Long lochia", "Observe, treat infection"], ["Puerperal metritis", "First 10 days", "Foul discharge, fever", "Systemic therapy"]] },
  df: ["RFM: retention of foetal membranes beyond the normal time.", "Total uterine prolapse: eversion of the entire uterus through the vulva.", "Subinvolution: delayed regression of the placental sites.", "Puerperal metritis: severe uterine inflammation in the first days after calving."],
  tm: [["RFM", "Retained placenta"], ["Uterine prolapse", "Everted uterus"], ["Milk fever", "Hypocalcaemia"], ["Subinvolution", "Delayed shrinkage"], ["Metritis", "Uterine infection"], ["Ketosis", "Energy disease"], ["Haemoglobinuria", "Red urine disease"], ["Lochia", "Postpartum discharge"]],
  st: ["Calving.", "Membranes pass or are retained.", "Uterus involutes.", "Infection or disease arises.", "Treat and support.", "Return to cyclicity."],
  ex: ["Cow with RFM after twins.", "Buffalo with uterine prolapse.", "Bitch with subinvolution."],
  k: ["RFM definition and rule not to pull.", "Prolapse replacement steps.", "Milk fever link.", "Haemorrhage danger.", "Metritis grades."],
  m: ["RFM incidence % = cows with RFM / cows calved x 100; typical herd targets are below about 10 per cent (verify)."],
  sa: [["When is RFM diagnosed in the cow?", "When the membranes are not expelled within about 12 hours."], ["Name the first step in treating uterine prolapse.", "Epidural anaesthesia and cleaning and restraint."], ["What is puerperal metritis?", "Severe inflammation of the uterus in the first 10 days after calving."]],
  la: [["Describe the causes and management of retention of foetal membranes.", "Definition, causes, mechanism, supportive treatment, no forced removal, prevention."], ["Describe uterine prolapse and the postpartum disorders of the cow.", "Prolapse management; milk fever; haemorrhage; subinvolution; metritis; return to cyclicity."]],
  q: [["RFM in the cow is suspected after about", ["12 hours", "1 hour", "2 weeks", "1 hour in mare"], 0, "Textbook limit."],
    ["Forced removal of adherent membranes causes", ["Haemorrhage and infection", "Better milk", "Heat", "Nothing"], 0, "Avoid."],
    ["Total uterine prolapse is an", ["Emergency", "Normal finding", "Heat sign", "Vaccine reaction"], 0, "Shock risk."],
    ["Hypocalcaemia can lead to", ["RFM and prolapse", "Bigger milk yield", "Heat", "Anoestrus only"], 0, "Weak uterus."],
    ["Selenium and vitamin E deficiency is linked to", ["RFM", "Rumen bloat", "Foot rot", "Tick fever"], 0, "Nutrition."]],
  f: [["RFM", "Retention of foetal membranes"], ["TUP", "Total uterine prolapse"], ["PPH", "Postpartum haemorrhage"], ["SIPS", "Subinvolution of placental sites"], ["MF", "Milk fever"]],
  d: ["Plan a postpartum checklist.", "Draw steps to replace a prolapse.", "Record RFM incidence."],
  vf: ["Time limits for RFM, incidence figures and herd targets differ by textbook; verify. Treatment drugs (oxytocin, PGF2 alpha, calcium and antimicrobials) and doses are not given. Do not attempt a uterine replacement without supervised training."]
});

topic("gyn", "Comparative Clinical Reproductive Anatomy and Endocrinology of the Male; Congenital and Genetic Defects; Puberty and Sexual Maturity", {
  o: ["Describe the male reproductive tract of domestic animals", "Describe the endocrine control of male reproduction", "Describe spermatogenesis and its regulation", "Describe congenital and genetic defects", "Describe puberty and factors affecting it"],
  i: "A bull is half of the herd genetics, and in AI he is most of it. A single infertile sire can ruin a season. The anatomy and hormones of the male are the base of every breeding soundness exam.",
  s: "Normal sperm production needs a cooler testis, functioning accessory glands and a hormone axis that works in sequence.",
  n: [
    ["Testis, scrotum and thermoregulation", "The testes lie in the scrotum, 2 to 4 degrees C below body temperature (verify), which is needed for spermatogenesis. Regulation: the pampiniform plexus (countercurrent heat exchange between the testicular artery and veins), the tunica dartos and cremaster muscle (raise or lower the testes), and sweating of scrotal skin. The testis has seminiferous tubules (with germ cells and Sertoli cells) and interstitial tissue (Leydig cells making testosterone). In the bull the testes hang vertically and are oval; in the boar they are in a perineal position; in the stallion they are horizontal; in the ram and goat they hang in a pendulous scrotum. Scrotal circumference in the bull is a good predictor of sperm output and age at puberty in daughters (the heritability is high; verify)."],
    ["Epididymis, ductus deferens and accessory sex glands", "The epididymis (head, body, tail) carries spermatozoa, concentrates them, matures them and stores them (about 8 to 14 days of transit; verify). The ductus deferens carries the sperm to the urethra at the ampulla. Accessory glands: ampullae (all except the pig and dog), vesicular glands (seminal vesicles: fructose-rich secretion, large in ruminants and boars), prostate (body and disseminated parts), bulbourethral glands (Cowper's glands; pre-ejaculatory fluid; large in the boar). The dog has only a prostate (no vesicular glands). The accessory secretions form seminal plasma, providing nutrients (fructose and sorbitol), buffers and ions."],
    ["Penis and prepuce", "The bull and ram have a fibro-elastic penis with a sigmoid flexure, and the ram has a urethral process; the stallion has a musculocavernous penis that enlarges by blood engorgement; the boar has a fibro-elastic penis with a corkscrew tip; the dog has a bulbus glandis and os penis. The prepuce protects the free part of the penis. The penile deviation of the bull, the sigmoid flexure and penis anatomy are relevant for surgical correction and for the teaser bull. Preputial diverticulum in the boar harbours urine and bacteria."],
    ["Hormonal control of male reproduction", "The hypothalamus releases GnRH in pulses, causing the pituitary to release LH and FSH. LH acts on Leydig cells to produce testosterone, which acts locally and systemically (spermatogenesis, secondary sexual characters, libido, accessory glands). FSH acts on Sertoli cells (with testosterone) to support spermatogenesis and inhibin secretion; inhibin acts as feedback on FSH; testosterone and oestradiol give negative feedback on GnRH and LH. In the bull, the first rise in LH pulse frequency leads to the rise in testosterone and puberty. Seasonality in the ram, goat and stallion is controlled by photoperiod through melatonin."],
    ["Spermatogenesis and the cycle", "Spermatogonia divide by mitosis, primary spermatocytes undergo meiosis I, secondary spermatocytes meiosis II, and the round spermatids undergo spermiogenesis (the formation of the acrosome, tail and condensation of nuclear chromatin) to produce spermatozoa released into the tubule lumen (spermiation). The cycle of the seminiferous epithelium takes about 13.5 days in the bull and the whole process about 61 days (verify) plus epididymal transit; therefore an insult to the testis shows in the semen after about 6 to 8 weeks. Daily sperm production is about 6 to 9 billion in the adult bull (verify), related to testicular weight."],
    ["Congenital and genetic defects of the male tract", "Cryptorchidism (undescended testis; unilateral or bilateral; heritable in the horse, pig, dog and others; the retained testis is at higher risk of tumour and is hot, so sperm is not produced; castrate and do not breed from the affected animal and its parents), testicular hypoplasia (underdevelopment, heritable in some breeds; seen in Swedish Highland cattle), segmental aplasia of the Wolffian ducts, persistent frenulum of the penis, hypospadias, penile deviation and spiral deviation (corkscrew penis), short scrotum, hernias (inguinal and scrotal), epididymal and ductal defects, intersexuality and chromosomal disorders (XXY Klinefelter-type in the bull and tortoiseshell tomcat; 60,XX sex reversal), and abnormal sperm defects with a genetic basis. A breeding soundness examination aims at detecting these."],
    ["Puberty and sexual maturity in males", "Puberty is the age at which the male produces sufficient motile sperm to impregnate a female; sexual maturity is when he reaches full sperm output and libido. Approximate ages (verify): bull 9 to 14 months in Bos taurus and later in zebu (about 14 to 24 months), buffalo bull 18 to 30 months, ram 5 to 7 months, buck 4 to 6 months, boar 5 to 7 months, stallion 12 to 24 months, dog 6 to 12 months, tomcat 6 to 9 months. In the bull, puberty is reached at a scrotal circumference of about 28 cm (a rule of thumb; verify) and ejaculation of semen with at least 50 million sperm with 10 per cent motility. Sexual maturity is reached some months to a year later."],
    ["Factors affecting puberty and maturity in males", "Breed and genotype, nutrition (a high plane hastens it; over-fattening harms the testis), season and photoperiod, climate and heat stress, disease and parasites, exposure to females, social effects, age, and mineral and vitamin deficiencies (zinc, vitamin A). Bulls on poor plane in India often mature late, and a managed rearing programme with growth monitoring and scrotal measurements helps. Selection of young bulls on scrotal circumference increases fertility in their offspring."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Part", "Function", "Species note", "Clinical point"], r: [["Testis", "Sperm and testosterone", "Position differs by species", "Scrotal circumference measured"], ["Pampiniform plexus", "Cooling of testicular blood", "All scrotal species", "Heat damage if impaired"], ["Epididymis", "Maturation and storage", "8 to 14 days transit", "Defects cause infertility"], ["Vesicular gland", "Fructose-rich fluid", "Absent in the dog", "Vesiculitis in bulls"], ["Penis", "Copulation and semen delivery", "Sigmoid flexure in ruminants and boar", "Deviations, injuries"], ["Hormones", "GnRH, LH, FSH, testosterone, inhibin", "Seasonal in ram and stallion", "Feedback loops"]] },
  df: ["Pampiniform plexus: vascular network that cools testicular arterial blood.", "Spermiogenesis: transformation of round spermatids into spermatozoa.", "Cryptorchidism: failure of one or both testes to descend into the scrotum.", "Scrotal circumference: circumference of the scrotum at its widest, a measure of testicular size."],
  tm: [["Sertoli cell", "Nurse cell of germ cells"], ["Leydig cell", "Testosterone cell"], ["Spermatogenesis", "Sperm production"], ["Spermiogenesis", "Sperm maturation in testis"], ["Cryptorchidism", "Undescended testis"], ["Inhibin", "FSH inhibitor"], ["Pampiniform plexus", "Heat exchanger"], ["Cowper's gland", "Bulbourethral gland"]],
  st: ["GnRH pulses.", "LH to Leydig cells.", "Testosterone.", "FSH to Sertoli cells.", "Spermatogenesis.", "Epididymal maturation."],
  ex: ["Bull with cryptorchidism.", "Ram effect of season.", "Young zebu bull late puberty."],
  k: ["Thermoregulation.", "Accessory glands.", "Hormonal axis.", "Spermatogenesis duration.", "Defects and puberty."],
  m: ["The spermatogenic cycle and epididymal transit times explain the 6 to 8 weeks lag in semen quality after a testicular insult."],
  sa: [["What does the pampiniform plexus do?", "Cools the testicular arterial blood by countercurrent heat exchange."], ["Which cell makes testosterone?", "Leydig cell."], ["Which species lacks vesicular glands?", "The dog."]],
  la: [["Describe the anatomy and endocrinology of the male reproductive tract of the bull.", "Testis, scrotum, epididymis, glands, penis, hormonal axis."], ["Describe spermatogenesis, puberty and the congenital defects of the male tract.", "Stages and duration, factors affecting puberty, cryptorchidism, hypoplasia, deviations."]],
  q: [["Testosterone is produced by", ["Leydig cells", "Sertoli cells", "Spermatids", "Epididymis"], 0, "Interstitial cells."],
    ["Spermatogenesis needs the testis to be", ["Cooler than the body", "Hotter", "Frozen", "Dry"], 0, "Thermoregulation."],
    ["Cryptorchidism is", ["Undescended testis", "Testicular tumour", "Hernia", "Cyst"], 0, "Definition."],
    ["The dog has", ["Only the prostate as an accessory gland", "Seminal vesicles", "Ampulla and vesicles", "No glands"], 0, "Species fact."],
    ["Inhibin comes from", ["Sertoli cells", "Leydig cells", "Pineal gland", "Adrenal"], 0, "FSH feedback."]],
  f: [["SC", "Scrotal circumference"], ["GnRH", "Gonadotrophin releasing hormone"], ["LH", "Luteinising hormone"], ["FSH", "Follicle stimulating hormone"], ["T", "Testosterone"]],
  d: ["Study male tract specimens.", "Draw the hormone feedback loop.", "Measure scrotal circumference on a model."],
  vf: ["Temperature differences, sperm production, spermatogenic cycle lengths, puberty ages and scrotal circumference thresholds are textbook values that vary by breed; verify. Heritability and genetic basis of defects should be checked."]
});

topic("gyn", "Sexual Behaviour, Libido, Sperm Transport, Erection and Ejaculation; Coital Injuries and Vices in Male Animals", {
  o: ["Describe sexual behaviour and libido", "Explain erection and ejaculation", "Describe sperm transport in the male tract", "Describe coital injuries", "Describe sexual vices and their management"],
  i: "A bull with perfect semen but no desire to mount is useless. Libido and the mechanics of mating are part of fertility, and they are often where the problem is.",
  s: "Mating behaviour is a sequence: courtship, mounting, erection, intromission and ejaculation, driven by sight, smell, touch and hormones.",
  n: [
    ["Sexual behaviour in males", "Sequence: detection of a female in heat (smell, flehmen response, vocalisation), courtship (nudging, chin resting, licking, genital sniffing), mounting, erection, intromission (a single thrust in the bull and ram, several in the stallion, a long copulatory tie in the dog, a long ejaculation in the boar), ejaculation, and dismount. Behaviour differs: the bull ejaculates in a single thrust in seconds, the stallion mounts several times, the boar copulates for 5 to 20 minutes (verify), and the dog locks (copulatory tie) for 10 to 30 minutes. Individual and species behaviour is of practical importance in semen collection."],
    ["Libido, serving capacity and factors", "Libido (sex drive) is the willingness to mount and serve; serving capacity is the number of services in a period under test (a test with several females for a given time). Factors: testosterone, genetic and breed differences (higher in Bos taurus than Bos indicus), age and experience (young bulls need to learn), nutrition (energy, protein, minerals), health (lameness, pain, foot problems), social hierarchy and the mix of bulls in the herd, season and heat stress, previous bad experience (injury), the female's stage of cycle, and environmental factors (floor, noise). Libido tests: reaction to a restrained female in oestrus (time to mount, number of services) in a fixed time."],
    ["Erection and protrusion", "Erection in the stallion, boar and dog is by blood engorgement of the cavernous spaces (parasympathetic vasodilation from the pelvic nerves: nitric oxide and cyclic GMP; plus the blocking of venous outflow by the contraction of the ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus muscles). In ruminants and the boar, erection is mainly by straightening of the sigmoid flexure by relaxation of the retractor penis muscle, with a smaller engorgement of the corpus cavernosum. The glans extends from the prepuce at intromission. Defects: failure of erection (impotentia coeundi from nerve or vascular problems, pain, injury, or psychological factors)."],
    ["Emission and ejaculation", "Emission is the movement of sperm from the epididymis and ampullae into the pelvic urethra by contraction of the smooth muscle of the tail of epididymis, ductus deferens and ampulla (sympathetic nerves); the accessory secretions are added. Ejaculation is the forceful expulsion of semen through the urethra by the rhythmic contraction of the urethral and bulbospongiosus muscles (somatic nerves; pudendal). Semen is ejaculated in fractions in some species: the boar and stallion in a pre-sperm, sperm-rich and post-sperm fractions; the bull ejaculates about 4 to 8 ml (verify) in one thrust; the ram about 1 ml; the boar 200 to 300 ml; the stallion 50 to 100 ml; the dog in three fractions (prostatic fluid fraction at the end)."],
    ["Sperm transport in the male tract", "Spermatozoa are non-motile when released from the testis and are carried in the tubular fluid to the epididymis by fluid flow and peristaltic action of the tubules and the rete testis; in the epididymis they mature (they gain motility and fertilising ability). On emission, contraction carries them to the urethra and out. Sperm storage in the tail of the epididymis allows many ejaculates to be collected after a rest; frequent ejaculation reduces the number. Disease of the epididymis or ducts blocks transport, giving azoospermia."],
    ["Coital injuries", "Penile injuries: haematoma of the penis (rupture of the tunica albuginea, called fracture of the penis in the bull, caused by the bull slipping or the female moving at mating; swelling over the sigmoid flexure; surgical drainage and sexual rest; prognosis guarded), penile laceration and preputial prolapse, paraphimosis, phimosis, persistent frenulum, preputial injuries and balanoposthitis, rupture of the retractor penis muscle, deviation of the penis, and hair rings. Injuries to the female by an oversized male or by rough service (vaginal and vulval tears) are also coital injuries. Prevention: floors, correct male-to-female matching, supervised service, and use of AI."],
    ["Vices and sexual disorders", "Homosexual behaviour (in cattle bulls kept together; not a pathological problem in itself but a management issue), masturbation and self-service (the stallion and the bull rubbing the penis against the belly; considered a vice in the horse), failure to mount (lack of libido, inexperience, pain, or poor environment), failure to ejaculate, and premature ejaculation. Aggression and dangerous behaviour in the bull and stallion need careful handling and safe restraint. Treatment: correct management, rest, training and, when necessary, hormonal or surgical help; hormones such as testosterone are used under veterinary direction because they harm spermatogenesis."],
    ["Safe handling and training of breeding males", "Train young bulls with a dummy or steer, use a nose ring and a stick or pole for control, handle gently, never allow rough play and teasing, and provide exercise. Bull handlers need training and safe facilities (a strong pen, escape routes). Stallions are best handled by experienced people with a controlled mounting environment. Keep all males free from stress, with good feet and nutrition."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Species", "Copulation style", "Semen volume (approx.; verify)", "Practical point"], r: [["Bull", "Single quick thrust", "4 to 8 ml", "Use artificial vagina or electro-ejaculator"], ["Ram and buck", "Single thrust", "About 1 ml", "AV works well"], ["Boar", "Long, 5 to 20 min", "200 to 300 ml", "Gloved hand technique"], ["Stallion", "Several thrusts, long", "50 to 100 ml", "Phantom mount, AV"], ["Dog", "Copulatory tie", "In 3 fractions", "Manual stimulation"], ["Cat", "Brief, barbed penis", "Small", "Electro-ejaculation if needed"]] },
  df: ["Libido: sexual drive or desire of a male to mate.", "Serving capacity: number of services a male performs in a standard test period.", "Impotentia coeundi: inability to copulate.", "Flehmen: lip-curl response in a male after sniffing urine or genital secretions."],
  tm: [["Libido", "Sex drive"], ["Serving capacity", "Number of services"], ["Flehmen", "Lip curl"], ["Emission", "Movement of sperm to urethra"], ["Ejaculation", "Expulsion of semen"], ["Paraphimosis", "Penis cannot retract"], ["Phimosis", "Cannot protrude penis"], ["Impotentia coeundi", "Cannot mate"]],
  st: ["Detect the female.", "Courtship.", "Mount and erection.", "Intromission.", "Emission and ejaculation.", "Dismount."],
  ex: ["Bull with low libido after foot disease.", "Fractured penis in a bull.", "Stallion with masturbation vice."],
  k: ["Behaviour sequence.", "Libido factors.", "Erection mechanisms.", "Emission versus ejaculation.", "Penile injuries."],
  m: ["No calculations. Serving capacity is counted as services per period in a test."],
  sa: [["Define libido.", "The sexual drive or willingness of a male to mate."], ["What is a fractured penis in a bull?", "Rupture of the tunica albuginea with haematoma."], ["What is impotentia coeundi?", "Inability to copulate."]],
  la: [["Describe the sexual behaviour and libido in the bull and the factors that affect them.", "Behaviour sequence, libido tests, factors, training."], ["Describe erection, emission, ejaculation and the common coital injuries.", "Mechanisms, nerves, fractions, injuries of penis and prepuce, treatment principles."]],
  q: [["Emission is controlled mainly by", ["Sympathetic nerves", "Parasympathetic only", "Hormones only", "No nerves"], 0, "Smooth muscle contraction."],
    ["The boar copulates for about", ["5 to 20 minutes", "5 seconds", "2 days", "1 second"], 0, "Long copulation."],
    ["A copulatory tie occurs in the", ["Dog", "Bull", "Ram", "Stallion"], 0, "Bulbus glandis."],
    ["Penile haematoma in bulls is called", ["Fracture of the penis", "Phimosis", "Cryptorchidism", "Hypospadias"], 0, "Rupture of tunica."],
    ["Libido is increased by", ["Testosterone", "Insulin only", "Calcium only", "Vitamin C"], 0, "Hormonal."]],
  f: [["IC", "Impotentia coeundi"], ["IG", "Impotentia generandi"], ["SC", "Serving capacity"], ["AV", "Artificial vagina"], ["EEJ", "Electro-ejaculator"]],
  d: ["Design a libido test.", "List causes of failure to mount.", "Plan safe handling facilities."],
  vf: ["Semen volumes, durations of copulation and the details of nerve pathways vary by textbook; verify. Treatment of penile injuries is surgical and learned in the surgery notes."]
});
/* ================= GYN part 6: Unit 3 andrology and AI (2) ================= */
