topic("surg", "General Anaesthesia: Induction, Injectable and Total Intravenous Anaesthesia, Inhalant Anaesthesia and Monitoring", {
  o: ["Define general anaesthesia and describe its stages", "Describe induction methods", "Describe injectable anaesthetics and TIVA", "Describe inhalant anaesthetics and machines", "Describe monitoring"],
  i: "General anaesthesia is a controlled and reversible poisoning of the brain. The skill lies in holding the depth where there is no pain or movement without harming the heart and lungs.",
  s: "A general anaesthetic gives unconsciousness, analgesia and relaxation. It is given by injection or by inhaled gas, and the animal is monitored for depth, breathing, circulation and temperature until fully awake.",
  n: [
    ["Stages and depth", "Guedel's classical stages describe ether anaesthesia: stage I analgesia, stage II delirium or excitement, stage III surgical anaesthesia (planes 1 to 4), stage IV overdose. Modern practice watches the eye position, jaw tone, reflexes, heart rate and breathing instead. A light plane gives movement and a rise in heart rate with a cut; a deep plane gives falling pressure and slow breathing."],
    ["Methods of induction", "By injectable drug through a vein (smooth and fast, standard in dogs and cats), by inhalant by mask or chamber (small animals and exotics), and by intramuscular drug when no vein is available. Horses and cattle are cast with a sedative or a rope and then induced; a large animal must be guided to the ground safely. An endotracheal tube secures the airway, prevents aspiration and delivers gas. In ruminants, intubate early because of regurgitation."],
    ["Injectable agents", "Barbiturates (thiopental, pentobarbital) are potent and cause respiratory depression; thiopental is cleared by redistribution, so recovery is quick after a single dose but long after repeated doses, and sight hounds recover slowly. Propofol is a non-barbiturate with rapid recovery, and cats may react to repeated doses. Alfaxalone is a neurosteroid with a smooth induction. Etomidate keeps the heart stable. Ketamine, a dissociative, is covered in the next chapter. Chloral hydrate and magnesium sulphate in older practice in horses were crude. Drugs are chosen by the patient's health, and doses are for the veterinarian."],
    ["Total intravenous anaesthesia (TIVA)", "TIVA keeps the animal anaesthetised by an intravenous drug alone, given as repeated small boluses or by constant infusion. It suits short procedures, field work without a gas machine, and cases where inhalant gas must be avoided. Combinations such as a sedative, ketamine and a muscle relaxant are used in horses, and a triple drip is used in large animals in the field. Drugs that accumulate lengthen recovery, so TIVA needs well-chosen drugs and good airway control."],
    ["Inhalation anaesthesia", "Advantages: depth is easily changed, recovery is fast, the airway is controlled and oxygen is supplied. Agents: isoflurane, sevoflurane, desflurane (in use), halothane (older, hepatotoxic and sensitises the heart to catecholamines), and nitrous oxide as an adjunct, with caution in gas-filled spaces such as bloat. The minimum alveolar concentration (MAC) measures potency; a low MAC means a potent agent. Effects: dose-related depression of respiration and circulation, with vasodilation and hypotension, and relaxation of muscles."],
    ["Anaesthetic machine and breathing systems", "The machine has a gas source (oxygen cylinder), a regulator, a flowmeter, a vaporiser that delivers a set concentration of agent, a breathing system, and a scavenging system. Non-rebreathing systems (Bain, T-piece, Magill) suit small animals and use high flow. Circle rebreathing systems with a carbon dioxide absorbent suit larger animals and save gas. Check for leaks and the soda lime colour before use. Waste gas must be scavenged to protect staff."],
    ["Monitoring", "Look at the eye position and reflexes, jaw tone, mucous membranes, capillary refill, heart rate and rhythm, pulse quality, respiration rate and depth, temperature and urine output. Equipment: pulse oximetry (oxygen saturation), capnography (end-tidal carbon dioxide, a measure of ventilation), non-invasive or invasive blood pressure, electrocardiogram and temperature probes. A person must be responsible for monitoring, and records are kept at intervals. Hypothermia is common, so warm the patient."],
    ["Recovery", "Move the animal to a quiet, padded and warm place. Keep the endotracheal tube in until the swallowing reflex returns, with the cuff deflated when removing. In horses, recovery is the high-risk time; assisted recovery with a head and tail rope or a recovery pool is used in some centres. Give analgesia and watch breathing and temperature until the animal is standing and alert. Ruminants should be kept in sternal posture to avoid bloat and aspiration."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Agent", "Group", "Strength", "Caution"], r: [["Thiopental", "Barbiturate", "Fast induction", "Slow recovery in sight hounds, tissue damage if given outside the vein"], ["Propofol", "Alkylphenol", "Smooth, quick recovery", "Apnoea, cats and repeated doses"], ["Alfaxalone", "Neurosteroid", "Smooth, quick", "Apnoea"], ["Isoflurane", "Inhalant", "Rapid depth change", "Hypotension, respiratory depression"], ["Sevoflurane", "Inhalant", "Very fast induction", "Cost, hypotension"], ["Halothane", "Inhalant", "Older agent", "Heart sensitisation, liver injury"]] },
  df: ["MAC: minimum alveolar concentration of an inhalant that prevents movement in half of patients.", "TIVA: total intravenous anaesthesia.", "Capnography: measurement of carbon dioxide in exhaled gas.", "Pulse oximetry: non-invasive measurement of haemoglobin oxygen saturation."],
  tm: [["Vaporiser", "Delivers set agent concentration"], ["Circle system", "Rebreathing with absorber"], ["Bain circuit", "Non-rebreathing system"], ["Soda lime", "CO2 absorber"], ["Scavenging", "Removing waste gas"], ["SpO2", "Oxygen saturation"], ["EtCO2", "End-tidal carbon dioxide"], ["Cuff", "Seal on the tube"]],
  st: ["Premedicate and preoxygenate.", "Induce.", "Intubate and connect.", "Maintain on inhalant or TIVA.", "Monitor continuously.", "Recover with support."],
  ex: ["Propofol induction and isoflurane maintenance for a dog.", "TIVA in a field horse.", "Capnography during a long operation."],
  k: ["Stages of anaesthesia.", "Induction methods.", "TIVA idea.", "Inhalant advantages and MAC.", "Monitoring tools."],
  m: ["No calculations or drug doses are given. MAC values are not listed because the numbers differ by species; take them from your textbook."],
  sa: [["What is TIVA?", "Maintenance of general anaesthesia using intravenous drugs alone."], ["List advantages of inhalation anaesthesia.", "Easy control of depth, rapid recovery, secure airway with oxygen supply."], ["Name three monitoring tools.", "Pulse oximeter, capnograph and blood pressure monitor."]],
  la: [["Describe the methods of induction and the injectable anaesthetics.", "Routes, agents, advantages and cautions."], ["Describe inhalation anaesthesia and the monitoring of the patient.", "Agents, machine, breathing systems, parameters, equipment and recovery."]],
  q: [["Capnography measures", ["Exhaled carbon dioxide", "Blood glucose", "Oxygen in water", "Heart size"], 0, "Ventilation."],
    ["A low MAC means the agent is", ["Potent", "Weak", "Unsafe always", "Irritant only"], 0, "Less is needed."],
    ["Soda lime in a circle system removes", ["Carbon dioxide", "Oxygen", "Water vapour only", "Nitrogen"], 0, "CO2 absorption."],
    ["Halothane sensitises the heart to", ["Catecholamines", "Insulin", "Calcium", "Light"], 0, "Arrhythmia risk."],
    ["Intubation protects against", ["Aspiration", "Pain", "Fever", "Bleeding"], 0, "Cuffed tube seals the airway."]],
  f: [["TIVA", "Total intravenous anaesthesia"], ["MAC", "Potency measure of inhalants"], ["Aspiration", "Inhaling gut contents"], ["Plane", "Level of surgical anaesthesia"], ["SpO2", "Oxygen saturation by pulse oximeter"]],
  d: ["Draw an anaesthetic machine and label it.", "Learn the stages table.", "Practise a monitoring sheet."],
  vf: ["Agent availability and cautions change over time and by species; verify with your pharmacology text and a clinician. Stages of anaesthesia as taught for ether do not fit modern agents exactly. No doses are given."]
});

topic("surg", "Dissociative Anaesthesia, Anaesthesia in Avian, Wild, Zoo, Exotic and Laboratory Animals, Capture Myopathy, Emergencies and Reversal", {
  o: ["Define dissociative anaesthesia and describe ketamine and tiletamine", "Describe anaesthesia in birds, wild, zoo, exotic and laboratory animals", "Describe capture myopathy", "Describe anaesthetic emergencies and their management", "Describe toxicity, antidotes and reversal agents"],
  i: "The skills in the previous chapters do not travel unchanged to a pigeon, a deer or a rat. Each group has its own risks, and emergencies happen to even careful anaesthetists.",
  s: "Dissociative drugs separate the brain's conscious areas from sensation, giving a cataleptic state. Special species need special methods and extra attention to temperature, breathing and stress. Emergencies are best met by preparation, monitoring and quick recognition.",
  n: [
    ["Dissociative anaesthesia", "Ketamine and tiletamine block the N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor. The animal is cataleptic with open eyes, muscle tone remains or rises, reflexes persist, and good somatic analgesia with poor visceral analgesia results. Heart rate and blood pressure tend to rise. Salivation and seizures can occur, and muscle relaxation is poor, so ketamine is combined with sedatives (alpha-2 agonists, benzodiazepines) to smooth induction and recovery. Respiration may be irregular. Use is contraindicated or cautious in animals with heart or kidney disease, raised pressure in the brain or eye, or seizure history. Ketamine is a controlled drug; follow law and store safely."],
    ["Clinical use", "Combinations are used in dogs, cats, horses (after sedation), ruminants (small doses with sedatives), pigs and exotic species. Intramuscular use makes it valuable for fractious or small animals, wild animals darted from a distance, and field work. Recovery should be quiet, as stimulation can cause emergence delirium."],
    ["Avian anaesthesia", "Birds have air sacs and no diaphragm, which means that anaesthetic gases pass through the lung in one direction and agents change quickly; so depth can change rapidly. Inhalants (isoflurane) are preferred. Birds lose heat fast and do not tolerate fasting for long, so fast only briefly and keep warm. Do not restrain tightly over the keel because this stops breathing. Intubation is done without a cuff, or with a very small one, because the tracheal rings are complete. Monitoring: heart rate, respiratory pattern, reflexes, and temperature. A poultry-level field method uses injectables under veterinary guidance."],
    ["Wild and zoo animals", "Capture by darting or trap needs correct drug choice, a good dart system, safe distance, an experienced team and the ability to track the animal. Potent opioids (etorphine) have been used for large mammals under strict control, with reversal agents ready; ketamine and alpha-2 combinations are used for many species. Stress, heat, chasing and struggling increase the risk of death. The syllabus asks only for awareness and a visit to a facility, so the details belong to wildlife veterinarians with legal permission."],
    ["Exotic and laboratory animals", "Small rodents, rabbits and reptiles have high metabolic rates (mammals), fast heat loss, and small blood volumes, so they need warmth, accurate small doses and careful handling. Rabbits are stress sensitive, sensitive to airway obstruction and may have fast heart rates. Reptiles depend on environment temperature: keep them in their preferred range, and expect slow drug action in the cold. Laboratory work follows ethics committee rules (CPCSEA in India) with humane endpoints. Inhalants by chamber or mask are common."],
    ["Capture myopathy", "A syndrome of wild animals, especially ruminants and birds, after chasing, capture or restraint: severe exertion, stress, high body temperature and lactic acid lead to muscle damage, myoglobinuria and kidney injury, sometimes heart damage. It may appear hours to days after capture with stiffness, weakness, ataxia, a dark urine, collapse or sudden death. Prevent by low-stress capture, short pursuit times, cooling, quiet handling, and early release. Treatment is supportive and the outlook is poor in severe cases; selenium and vitamin E have been tried and their value is uncertain, so verify."],
    ["Anaesthetic emergencies", "Respiratory depression or apnoea: check the airway, stop the agent, ventilate with oxygen, and consider reversal. Airway obstruction or a blocked tube: reposition, suction, replace the tube. Hypotension: reduce the depth, give fluids, and use supportive drugs as directed. Bradycardia and arrhythmias: check depth, oxygen, potassium and drug effects. Cardiac arrest: begin cardiopulmonary resuscitation without delay (airway, breathing, compressions at a rate per your protocol), and give emergency drugs as instructed. Hypothermia, malignant hyperthermia (especially in pigs with a genetic trait, triggered by halothane and stress), regurgitation and aspiration, and awareness are other problems. An emergency tray, oxygen and a person trained in resuscitation make the difference."],
    ["Toxicity, antidotes and reversal agents", "Alpha-2 agonist effects are reversed by alpha-2 antagonists (yohimbine, atipamezole, tolazoline); opioid effects by naloxone (or partial reversal with butorphanol); benzodiazepines by flumazenil; neuromuscular blockers by anticholinesterases such as neostigmine with an anticholinergic. Ketamine and propofol have no specific antagonist; support is the answer. Local anaesthetic toxicity needs seizure control, oxygen and cardiovascular support; lipid emulsion has a role in some centres. Reversal returns the pain, so analgesia may be needed afterwards. Doses are for a veterinarian."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Drug effect", "Reversal", "Remark"], r: [["Alpha-2 agonists", "Atipamezole, yohimbine, tolazoline", "Pain returns after reversal"], ["Opioids", "Naloxone", "Short action; renarcotisation can occur"], ["Benzodiazepines", "Flumazenil", "Used when needed"], ["Neuromuscular block", "Neostigmine with anticholinergic", "Needs ventilation until recovery"], ["Ketamine, propofol", "None specific", "Support and ventilate"], ["Local anaesthetic toxicity", "No antidote", "Seizure and heart support"]] },
  df: ["Dissociative anaesthesia: state of catalepsy and analgesia produced by NMDA antagonists.", "Capture myopathy: muscle disease of wild animals after stress and exertion.", "Malignant hyperthermia: inherited disorder with rapid rise in temperature and muscle rigidity triggered by some anaesthetics.", "Reversal agent: drug that opposes the effect of another drug."],
  tm: [["Catalepsy", "Rigid trance with open eyes"], ["NMDA", "Receptor blocked by ketamine"], ["Air sacs", "Bird respiratory organs"], ["Darting", "Remote drug delivery"], ["Myoglobinuria", "Muscle pigment in urine"], ["Naloxone", "Opioid antagonist"], ["Flumazenil", "Benzodiazepine antagonist"], ["Apnoea", "Stopped breathing"]],
  st: ["Choose the agent for the species.", "Prepare for stress and heat.", "Induce and monitor closely.", "Recognise an emergency early.", "Act on airway, breathing, circulation.", "Reverse and recover with support."],
  ex: ["Ketamine with a sedative in a dog.", "Anaesthesia of a pigeon with inhalant gas.", "Capture myopathy in a chased deer."],
  k: ["Ketamine features.", "Bird anaesthesia points.", "Capture myopathy signs.", "ABC in an emergency.", "Reversal agents."],
  m: ["No dose calculations are included. Wildlife drug combinations are legally controlled."],
  sa: [["What is capture myopathy?", "A muscle disease of captured wild animals due to stress and exertion, with myoglobinuria and collapse."], ["Name the reversal agent for alpha-2 agonists.", "Atipamezole, yohimbine or tolazoline."], ["Why is a bird's anaesthesia depth quick to change?", "Air sacs and a one-way flow of gas through the lung make agent exchange rapid."]],
  la: [["Describe dissociative anaesthesia and its clinical use.", "Mechanism, effects on body systems, use, limits, combinations."], ["Describe anaesthetic emergencies and their management.", "Respiratory, circulatory, airway, hyperthermia; steps, drugs, reversal."]],
  q: [["Ketamine acts on the", ["NMDA receptor", "GABA only", "Opioid receptor only", "Alpha-2 receptor"], 0, "NMDA antagonist."],
    ["Capture myopathy follows", ["Stress and exertion", "Overfeeding", "Vitamin excess", "Cold only"], 0, "Chase and capture."],
    ["Naloxone reverses", ["Opioids", "Benzodiazepines", "Alpha-2 agonists", "Local anaesthetics"], 0, "Opioid antagonist."],
    ["Pigs with malignant hyperthermia react to", ["Halothane and stress", "Water", "Oxygen only", "Vaccines"], 0, "Triggers include halothane."],
    ["In an arrest, the first priority is", ["Airway and breathing", "Suturing", "Feeding", "Recording only"], 0, "ABC."]],
  f: [["NMDA", "N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor"], ["CPCSEA", "Indian committee on animal experiments"], ["Etorphine", "Potent opioid used in wild animals"], ["Emergence delirium", "Agitation on waking"], ["Renarcotisation", "Return of opioid effect after antagonist wears off"]],
  d: ["List emergencies and first actions.", "Compare reversal agents.", "Plan a visit to a zoo or wildlife facility."],
  vf: ["Wildlife drugs and reversal agents are legally controlled, and species responses differ widely. Capture myopathy treatment is poorly standardised. No doses are given here; use your anaesthesia manual and a wildlife veterinarian."]
});
