topic("med", "Deficiency Diseases II: Calcium, Phosphorus, Magnesium, Iodine and Related Bone Disorders", {
  o: ["Describe calcium and phosphorus metabolism disorders", "Describe rickets, osteomalacia and osteodystrophy fibrosa", "Describe magnesium deficiency", "Describe iodine deficiency and goitre", "State diagnosis, control and prevention"],
  i: "Calcium, phosphorus and magnesium run the skeleton, the muscles and the nerves. A cow on a phosphorus-poor pasture, a dog on all-meat diet and a goat kid in iodine-deficient hills all pay the price in different ways.",
  s: "Mineral balance depends on the amount, the ratio and the vitamin D status. Deficiency shows in bone (soft, deformed, fractured) and nerve and muscle (tetany, weakness) or in endocrine glands (goitre).",
  n: [
    ["Calcium and phosphorus: the ratio", "A calcium to phosphorus ratio of about 1 to 1 up to 2 to 1 is the usual teaching target (check species-specific values). Absorption of both depends on vitamin D; parathyroid hormone and calcitonin regulate calcium. Imbalance arises from low calcium (all-meat diets in dogs and cats), low phosphorus (poor pastures, cattle on straw), a high phosphorus bran diet (bran disease in horses), or low vitamin D (little sunlight, in housed animals). Diagnosis uses diet, serum calcium and phosphorus and radiographs."],
    ["Rickets and osteomalacia", "Rickets affects young growing animals: swollen painful joints, a rosary at the rib junctions, bowed legs, lameness, delayed tooth eruption and poor growth, with defective mineralisation of the growth plate. Osteomalacia affects adults: bone softening, shifting lameness, fractures and pica (chewing bones and soil, an indicator in cattle). Causes: deficiency of vitamin D, calcium or phosphorus. Care: correct the diet and vitamin D supply, avoid handling that fractures bones, and give sunlight exposure. See also the vitamin chapter."],
    ["Phosphorus deficiency and related diseases", "Common in cattle and buffalo grazing on phosphorus-poor soils and dry pastures in India. Signs: pica (depraved appetite, chewing bones, soil, wood and leather), poor growth, delayed puberty, anoestrus, repeat breeding, low milk yield, stiffness and brittle bones. Postparturient haemoglobinuria occurs in cattle and buffalo in some tracts, with intravascular haemolysis; low phosphorus is a risk factor. Diagnosis by serum inorganic phosphorus and diet analysis. Prevention by an area-specific mineral mixture and good green fodder."],
    ["Osteodystrophia fibrosa", "Fibrous osteodystrophy results from secondary hyperparathyroidism, with bone resorption and replacement with fibrous tissue. Causes: high phosphorus, low calcium (horses on bran: bran disease, big head; pigs and dogs on meat diets) and oxalates in tropical grasses (setaria, buffel) that bind calcium; kidney disease causes secondary renal hyperparathyroidism. Signs: swelling of facial bones (big head), shifting lameness, loose teeth, and fractures. Care: correct the Ca to P ratio and the diet; response is slow."],
    ["Magnesium deficiency (hypomagnesaemic tetany)", "Grass tetany (grass staggers) occurs in lactating cows and ewes on lush spring pasture high in potassium and nitrogen and low in magnesium. Signs: restlessness, excitability, muscle tremors, stiff gait, convulsions and sudden death. Wheat pasture staggers and transport tetany are related. Hypomagnesaemia in calves (milk diets). Diagnosis: low serum magnesium, low urine and cerebrospinal fluid magnesium, and history. It is an emergency: intravenous or subcutaneous magnesium preparations are given by the veterinarian with care, because rapid injection may kill. Prevention: magnesium supplementation of feed and avoiding heavy fertiliser on pasture."],
    ["Iodine deficiency and goitre", "Iodine is required for thyroid hormones. Deficiency (endemic in hilly areas and floodplains away from the sea, India's sub-Himalayan belts, and with goitrogens in some plants such as cabbage and brassicas, cassava) produces goitre (enlarged thyroid) and in the foetus stillbirths, hairless piglets and calves, weak lambs, retained placenta, and infertility. In adults, hypothyroid signs: dull coat, weight gain and low production. Diagnosis by thyroid enlargement, low thyroxine and urinary iodine. Prevention by iodised salt and iodine-containing mineral mixtures; excess iodine is also harmful."],
    ["Calcium, magnesium and nutritional tetanies", "Hypocalcaemia occurs in lactating animals, including bitches (eclampsia), and is detailed in the next chapter. Wheat pasture poisoning, tetanies of lactation and transport tetany involve low calcium and magnesium. Always consider both ions when a dairy animal convulses."],
    ["Diagnosis, control and prevention", "Diagnose by pattern of disease in a herd, history of feeding (type of fodder, supplements, soil), serum and urine tests, bone radiographs and response to correction. Control by area-specific mineral mixtures, balanced rations with the right Ca to P ratio, good quality green fodder and legumes, and access to sunlight. Avoid over-supplementation."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Disorder", "Cause", "Typical species", "Key sign"], r: [["Rickets", "Vitamin D, Ca, P lack", "Young animals", "Bowed legs, rosary"], ["Osteomalacia", "Same, adults", "Adult cattle", "Pica, shifting lameness"], ["Bran disease", "High P, low Ca", "Horses", "Facial swelling"], ["Phosphorus deficiency", "Poor pasture", "Cattle, buffalo", "Pica, anoestrus"], ["Grass tetany", "Low Mg", "Lactating cows, ewes", "Tremor, convulsion"], ["Goitre", "Iodine lack", "All species", "Thyroid enlargement"]] },
  df: ["Pica: eating non-food substances due to mineral deficiency.", "Osteodystrophia fibrosa: bone replaced by fibrous tissue from hyperparathyroidism.", "Grass tetany: hypomagnesaemic tetany on lush pasture.", "Goitre: enlargement of the thyroid gland."],
  tm: [["PTH", "Parathyroid hormone"], ["Calcitonin", "Lowers blood calcium"], ["Vitamin D", "Aids calcium absorption"], ["Pica", "Depraved appetite"], ["Tetany", "Muscle spasm"], ["Goitrogen", "Substance that causes goitre"], ["Rosary", "Rib swellings"], ["Oxalate", "Plant compound binding calcium"]],
  st: ["Check diet and ratio.", "Observe bone and nerve signs.", "Test serum minerals.", "Image the bones.", "Correct the diet.", "Use area mineral mixture."],
  ex: ["Pica in a phosphorus-deficient buffalo.", "Grass tetany in cows on spring pasture.", "Goitre in newborn kids in a hill area."],
  k: ["Ca to P ratio.", "Rickets versus osteomalacia.", "Phosphorus deficiency signs.", "Grass tetany.", "Goitre prevention."],
  m: ["Ca to P ratios: about 1 to 1 up to 2 to 1 as a teaching range. Exact requirements are in feeding standards."],
  sa: [["Differentiate rickets and osteomalacia.", "Rickets affects growing bone; osteomalacia affects adult bone."], ["What is grass tetany?", "A hypomagnesaemic tetany of lactating animals on lush pasture."], ["What causes goitre?", "Iodine deficiency or goitrogens that reduce thyroid hormone synthesis."]],
  la: [["Describe phosphorus deficiency in cattle and buffalo.", "Causes, signs, diagnosis, control, link to infertility and haemoglobinuria."], ["Describe the disorders of calcium and phosphorus metabolism.", "Ratio, rickets, osteomalacia, osteodystrophy fibrosa, causes and prevention."]],
  q: [["Pica in cattle often signals", ["Phosphorus deficiency", "Copper excess", "Iodine excess", "Selenium excess"], 0, "Craving bones and soil."],
    ["Big head in horses is due to", ["High phosphorus, low calcium", "Low iodine", "Vitamin E deficiency", "Copper excess"], 0, "Bran disease."],
    ["Grass tetany relates to", ["Magnesium", "Iron", "Zinc", "Cobalt"], 0, "Hypomagnesaemia."],
    ["Iodine deficiency leads to", ["Goitre", "Rickets", "Pica", "Bloat"], 0, "Thyroid enlargement."],
    ["Vitamin D helps absorb", ["Calcium", "Copper", "Sodium", "Chloride"], 0, "Calcium and phosphorus."]],
  f: [["Postparturient haemoglobinuria", "Haemolysis in the postpartum period linked to low phosphorus"], ["Hypomagnesaemia", "Low blood magnesium"], ["Secondary hyperparathyroidism", "Excess PTH from calcium imbalance"], ["Iodised salt", "Common iodine supply"], ["Hairless piglets", "Sign of maternal iodine deficiency"]],
  d: ["Make a table of mineral problems.", "Study mineral tracts of your district.", "Learn the area-specific mixtures."],
  vf: ["Ratios, safe intakes and the details of postparturient haemoglobinuria differ between sources. Intravenous magnesium and calcium preparations are for veterinarians; none are given."]
});

topic("med", "Vitamin Deficiencies: A, D, E, B Complex, K and C", {
  o: ["Describe the functions of the vitamins and deficiency signs", "Describe diseases linked to vitamin A, D, E and K deficiency", "Describe B-complex deficiencies including thiamine and B12", "Describe vitamin C in animals that need it", "State diagnosis and prevention"],
  i: "Vitamins are small molecules the body cannot make in enough quantity. Deficiency diseases appear when the diet fails, when intestinal bacteria are disturbed or when a drug or an antagonist blocks the vitamin.",
  s: "Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are stored, so deficiency develops over weeks to months. Water-soluble vitamins (B complex, C) are not stored in large amounts, so deficiency shows sooner. Ruminants make B vitamins and K in the rumen and vitamin C in the liver, so their deficiencies are rarer than in non-ruminants.",
  n: [
    ["Vitamin A", "Needed for vision, epithelial integrity, bone growth and reproduction. Deficiency (dry season, poor green feed, long storage of fodder): night blindness, xerophthalmia (dry cornea and ulceration), keratinisation of epithelia leading to infections, rough coat, reduced fertility, abortions, weak or blind calves, convulsions from raised cerebrospinal fluid pressure and papilloedema, and in poultry, nasal and ocular discharge and gout-like deposits. Diagnosis: history, signs, liver and serum retinol. Prevention: green fodder, carotene-rich feed and supplement, as vitamin A is toxic in excess."],
    ["Vitamin D", "Regulates calcium and phosphorus absorption and bone mineralisation. Deficiency in animals not exposed to sunlight and on low vitamin D diets: rickets, osteomalacia, soft beak and leg weakness in poultry, thin-shelled eggs. Sun-cured hay and UV exposure give vitamin D. Dogs and cats cannot synthesise enough in the skin and rely on diet. Excess leads to calcification of soft tissues and is toxic (some rodenticides contain cholecalciferol)."],
    ["Vitamin E", "A chain-breaking antioxidant that protects cell membranes, working with selenium. Deficiency: white muscle disease, exudative diathesis (chicks), encephalomalacia (crazy chick disease) in chickens, mulberry heart and hepatosis dietetica in pigs, steatitis (yellow fat disease) in cats on oily fish diets, poor fertility, and weak newborns. Diet high in polyunsaturated fatty acids increases the need. Prevention: supply with selenium and good quality fresh feed."],
    ["Vitamin K", "Needed to make clotting factors in the liver. Deficiency causes bleeding: in poultry from antibiotics that kill gut bacteria or from sulphonamides; in cattle and sheep on mouldy sweet clover (dicoumarol), and from anticoagulant rodenticides in dogs and pigs. Signs: haemorrhages, delayed clotting, haematomas and bleeding after procedures. Diagnosis by prolonged clotting time and history; care is vitamin K therapy, as decided by the veterinarian."],
    ["Thiamine (B1)", "Needed for carbohydrate metabolism, especially in nerves. Polioencephalomalacia in ruminants (blindness, head pressing, convulsions), associated with thiaminase-producing rumen bacteria, high sulphur intake or amprolium; also bracken fern and Equisetum poisoning in horses (thiaminase), and fish-diet thiamine deficiency in cats and foxes (Chastek paralysis), with neurological signs. Response to thiamine is dramatic when early."],
    ["Riboflavin, niacin, pantothenic acid, pyridoxine, biotin, folic acid, choline", "Riboflavin (B2) deficiency in chicks: curled toe paralysis, poor growth; niacin deficiency: black tongue in dogs, pellagra-like dermatitis and diarrhoea in pigs and chicks; pantothenic acid: dermatitis, goose-stepping in pigs, scabby eyelids in chicks; pyridoxine: convulsions and anaemia; biotin: foot and skin lesions, fatty liver and kidney syndrome, cracking of hooves; folic acid: anaemia and poor feathering; choline: perosis and fatty liver. Poultry and pigs on grain diets are most exposed, so premixes supply these vitamins."],
    ["Vitamin B12 and cobalt", "Vitamin B12 contains cobalt; deficiency in ruminants is due to lack of cobalt. In pigs and poultry, a lack in the diet causes poor growth and anaemia. In dogs, intestinal disease and genetic defects in some breeds lead to malabsorption. Diagnosis by serum B12 and response."],
    ["Vitamin C", "Most domestic animals (not guinea pigs, primates and a few others) can synthesise ascorbic acid, so deficiency is rare; stress, heat and disease may raise needs, and it may help immune function. In guinea pigs, deficiency causes scurvy with haemorrhages and joint pain. Diagnosis and prevention: supply in diet for susceptible species, noting that vitamin C solutions are unstable. In India, supplementation is common in poultry in heat stress."],
    ["General diagnosis and prevention", "Look at diet, season, storage time, and any drug or disease interfering with vitamin supply. Confirm with blood or liver levels, clotting tests and response to treatment. Prevent with balanced diets with a vitamin premix, high-quality fresh fodder, correct storage of feed, sunlight exposure, avoidance of antagonists, and routine supplementation in high-risk periods. Do not give large doses of fat-soluble vitamins without veterinary advice."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Vitamin", "Main role", "Deficiency sign", "Note"], r: [["A", "Vision, epithelium", "Night blindness, xerophthalmia", "Toxic in excess"], ["D", "Ca and P absorption", "Rickets, osteomalacia", "Sunlight helps"], ["E", "Antioxidant", "White muscle disease, exudative diathesis", "Works with selenium"], ["K", "Clotting", "Bleeding", "Sweet clover, warfarin"], ["B1 thiamine", "Nerve carbohydrate metabolism", "Polioencephalomalacia", "Thiaminase, sulphur"], ["C", "Collagen, antioxidant", "Scurvy in guinea pigs", "Most species make it"]] },
  df: ["Xerophthalmia: dry eye due to vitamin A deficiency.", "Polioencephalomalacia: cerebrocortical necrosis in ruminants, thiamine-related.", "Exudative diathesis: subcutaneous oedema in chicks from vitamin E and selenium deficiency.", "Dicoumarol: anticoagulant from mouldy sweet clover."],
  tm: [["Retinol", "Vitamin A"], ["Cholecalciferol", "Vitamin D3"], ["Tocopherol", "Vitamin E"], ["Phylloquinone", "Vitamin K1"], ["Thiaminase", "Enzyme destroying thiamine"], ["Biotin", "B vitamin for skin and hoof"], ["Scurvy", "Vitamin C deficiency"], ["Premix", "Vitamin and mineral additive mix"]],
  st: ["Review the diet and storage.", "Note the signs.", "Test blood or liver.", "Respond to supplementation.", "Correct the ration.", "Review the herd."],
  ex: ["Night blindness in cattle in a drought.", "A chick with curled toes.", "A cow with polioencephalomalacia."],
  k: ["Fat-soluble versus water-soluble.", "Vitamin A deficiency.", "Vitamin E and selenium.", "Thiamine.", "Vitamin K antagonists."],
  m: ["No calculation. Vitamin requirements are in feeding standards."],
  sa: [["Name signs of vitamin A deficiency.", "Night blindness, xerophthalmia, infection, infertility and weak newborns."], ["What is exudative diathesis?", "Subcutaneous oedema in chicks due to vitamin E and selenium deficiency."], ["Which vitamin is related to clotting?", "Vitamin K."]],
  la: [["Describe vitamin A deficiency.", "Functions, causes, signs by species, diagnosis, prevention and toxicity."], ["Describe the B-complex deficiencies of animals.", "Thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, pantothenic acid, biotin, B12, folic acid, choline."]],
  q: [["Night blindness is due to", ["Vitamin A deficiency", "Vitamin K deficiency", "Vitamin D deficiency", "Vitamin C deficiency"], 0, "Retinal function."],
    ["Mouldy sweet clover contains", ["Dicoumarol", "Aflatoxin", "Cyanide", "Nitrite"], 0, "Anticoagulant."],
    ["Curled toe paralysis is due to", ["Riboflavin deficiency", "Biotin excess", "Vitamin A deficiency", "Vitamin D excess"], 0, "Chicks."],
    ["Cats on oily fish diets may develop", ["Steatitis from vitamin E deficiency", "Rickets", "Goitre", "Scurvy"], 0, "Yellow fat disease."],
    ["Guinea pigs need dietary", ["Vitamin C", "Vitamin K only", "Vitamin B12 only", "Vitamin D only"], 0, "They cannot make it."]],
  f: [["Steatitis", "Inflammation of fat"], ["Chastek paralysis", "Thiamine deficiency in carnivores on raw fish"], ["Crazy chick disease", "Encephalomalacia from vitamin E deficiency"], ["Pellagra", "Niacin deficiency disease"], ["Papilloedema", "Swelling of the optic disc"]],
  d: ["Make a table of vitamins and diseases.", "Learn sources of each vitamin.", "Study storage losses of feed."],
  vf: ["Species-specific vitamin needs and the roles of some B vitamins in clinical disease differ by text. Vitamin preparations and doses are for veterinarians and feeding standards; none are given."]
});

topic("med", "Diseases of Neonates and Alternative, Integrated and Ethnoveterinary Medicine", {
  o: ["Describe the physiology and risks of the newborn period", "Describe neonatal septicaemia and failure of passive transfer", "Describe neonatal diarrhoea and other neonatal diseases", "Describe the principles of neonatal care and prevention", "Describe the role of alternative and ethnoveterinary medicine"],
  i: "The first days of life decide whether a calf, kid or foal will join the herd or die in the shed. Most neonatal deaths are preventable and begin with the first drink of colostrum. Ethnoveterinary medicine, on the other side, is an important knowledge system in villages, and a modern veterinarian must understand it honestly.",
  s: "Newborns have limited reserves, immature organs and no immune memory, and depend on colostrum for antibodies, energy and warmth. Disease results from failure of passive transfer, infection, cold and poor care. Traditional remedies are useful as a research and health resource, but evidence, safety and integration into modern practice need care.",
  n: [
    ["Neonatal physiology and risk", "At birth, the newborn must start breathing, regulate temperature, begin to suckle and pass meconium. Ruminant, equine and porcine newborns have no antibodies transferred through the placenta (the placenta blocks them), so colostrum absorption within the first hours (the gut closes to antibody absorption within about 24 hours) is the key. Risks: hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, dystocia-related asphyxia, congenital defects and infection through the navel."],
    ["Failure of passive transfer", "Low colostral antibody intake or absorption: late or too little colostrum, poor quality colostrum (heifer, premature milking), weak or injured calves and poor maternal condition. Result: calves are susceptible to septicaemia, diarrhoea and pneumonia. Diagnosis: serum total protein or immunoglobulin level at 24 to 48 hours. Prevention: feed good quality colostrum within the first hours, at the right volume as taught by your teacher; colostrum banks and supplements in difficult cases."],
    ["Neonatal septicaemia and navel ill", "Entry through the navel, gut or respiratory tract of bacteria such as E. coli, Salmonella, Klebsiella and Streptococcus. Signs: weakness, poor suckling, fever or low temperature, depression, swollen navel, joint swelling (joint ill), eye changes, and sudden death. Diagnosis: blood culture, clinical signs and serum protein. Care: early treatment by the veterinarian, fluids, warmth and nursing. Prevention: colostrum, clean calving area, navel disinfection, and hygiene."],
    ["Neonatal diarrhoea", "The commonest killer of calves, lambs, kids and piglets in the first month. Causes: enterotoxigenic E. coli (first week), rotavirus and coronavirus (1 to 2 weeks), Cryptosporidium (1 to 4 weeks), Salmonella, Clostridium perfringens types, coccidia (older), and nutritional causes (overfeeding of milk, cold or dirty milk). The pathology is secretory or malabsorptive diarrhoea with loss of fluid, bicarbonate and electrolytes, which leads to dehydration, metabolic acidosis, hyperkalaemia and death. Care: oral electrolyte solutions, continued milk feeding in many cases, intravenous fluids in severe cases, warmth and specific therapy by the veterinarian, with strict hygiene. Prevention: dam vaccines, colostrum, clean housing, and avoiding crowding."],
    ["Other neonatal diseases", "Hypothermia and hypoglycaemia, neonatal pneumonia, congenital defects and genetic disease, neonatal isoerythrolysis in foals and piglets, perinatal asphyxia (neonatal maladjustment syndrome in foals), white muscle disease, selenium and iodine deficiency signs, tetanus via the navel, omphalitis, urachal problems, meconium retention in foals, atresia and hernias, and starvation of piglets (crushing, weak piglets, agalactia of the sow). Cats and dogs: fading puppy and kitten syndrome from infection, hypothermia and low birth weight."],
    ["Principles of neonatal care", "Warm, dry, clean environment; assisted suckling; colostrum within the first hours; navel treatment; observation of the dam and newborn; weighing and records; early recognition of weak animals; fluids and glucose under veterinary guidance; and separation of sick neonates. Teach the farmer to watch for the signs of the 'unthrifty newborn' because outcomes are best with early care."],
    ["Alternative, integrated and ethnoveterinary medicine: scope", "Ethnoveterinary medicine (EVM) is the traditional knowledge, practices and remedies used by communities to manage animal health, mostly plant-based, with massage, bonesetting, dietary measures, rituals and mineral remedies. Integrated medicine means evidence-based use of traditional or complementary approaches alongside modern veterinary medicine. Systems include Ayurveda (Pashu Ayurveda), Siddha, Unani, homoeopathy and acupuncture. India has a large documented knowledge base, and national and state agencies have projects to document and validate remedies."],
    ["Examples and the evidence", "Examples documented in field surveys: turmeric, neem, aloe, tulsi, and local herbs for wounds, mastitis, diarrhoea, and fever; bark and seed remedies for worms; massage and bandaging for fractures. Some have evidence for activity (turmeric for inflammation, neem for ectoparasites), but many lack controlled trials. Concerns: variable preparation, unknown dose, contamination, drug interactions, delaying effective treatment for dangerous disease, residues in milk and meat, toxicity, and the legality of herbal products. Homoeopathy and acupuncture have limited or mixed evidence for most conditions; verify in current reviews."],
    ["The veterinarian's approach", "Respect the knowledge, document it, test it with sound methods, and use it where evidence and safety support it, particularly for mild conditions and where modern drugs are not available. Never delay proven treatment for serious or notifiable disease. Maintain records, observe withdrawal times, and follow the laws and regulations on drugs, residues and veterinary practice. Research includes screening for antimicrobial and anthelmintic activity and safe formulations, which may help address antimicrobial resistance."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Neonatal problem", "Age typical", "Key cause", "Principle"], r: [["Failure of passive transfer", "First day", "Late or low colostrum", "Early colostrum, test serum protein"], ["E. coli diarrhoea", "First week", "ETEC", "Hygiene, colostrum, fluids"], ["Rotavirus diarrhoea", "1 to 2 weeks", "Virus", "Fluids, hygiene"], ["Cryptosporidiosis", "1 to 4 weeks", "Protozoa", "Fluids, hygiene"], ["Navel ill", "First days", "Umbilical infection", "Navel care"], ["Hypothermia", "First hours", "Cold, wet", "Warmth, dry"]] },
  df: ["Colostrum: first milk rich in antibodies.", "Passive transfer: acquisition of antibodies from colostrum.", "Ethnoveterinary medicine: traditional knowledge of animal health care.", "Integrated medicine: combining evidence-based traditional and modern medicine."],
  tm: [["Meconium", "First faeces of the newborn"], ["Omphalitis", "Navel infection"], ["FPT", "Failure of passive transfer"], ["ETEC", "Enterotoxigenic E. coli"], ["EVM", "Ethnoveterinary medicine"], ["Ayurveda", "Indian traditional medicine"], ["Fading puppy", "Neonatal loss syndrome"], ["Maladjustment", "Neonatal foal syndrome"]],
  st: ["Give colostrum early.", "Dry, warm and clean housing.", "Treat the navel.", "Watch for weak neonates.", "Rehydrate scouring animals.", "Record and review."],
  ex: ["A calf with E. coli diarrhoea.", "A foal with maladjustment syndrome.", "A farmer using turmeric paste on a wound."],
  k: ["Colostrum and gut closure.", "Neonatal diarrhoea pathogens by age.", "Navel ill.", "EVM scope.", "Safety concerns."],
  m: ["No calculation. Colostrum volumes and timings are in your livestock management text."],
  sa: [["Why is colostrum critical?", "Newborn ruminants and horses get no antibodies through the placenta and depend on colostrum."], ["Name the pathogens of neonatal diarrhoea in calves.", "E. coli, rotavirus, coronavirus, Cryptosporidium and Salmonella."], ["Define ethnoveterinary medicine.", "Traditional knowledge and practice of animal health care by communities."]],
  la: [["Describe the diseases of neonates and their prevention.", "Failure of passive transfer, septicaemia, diarrhoea, other diseases and care."], ["Discuss the role and limitations of ethnoveterinary medicine.", "Scope, examples, evidence, safety, integration and the veterinarian's approach."]],
  q: [["Antibodies reach calves mainly through", ["Colostrum", "Placenta", "Skin", "Water"], 0, "No placental transfer."],
    ["Calf diarrhoea in the first week is often due to", ["ETEC", "Coccidia", "Fluke", "Mange"], 0, "Enterotoxigenic E. coli."],
    ["The main danger of neonatal diarrhoea is", ["Dehydration and acidosis", "Itch", "Fever only", "Hair loss"], 0, "Fluid loss."],
    ["Navel ill can cause", ["Joint ill", "Rickets", "Bloat", "Cataract"], 0, "Septicaemia."],
    ["Integrated medicine requires", ["Evidence and safety", "Only tradition", "No records", "No regulations"], 0, "Evidence-based approach."]],
  f: [["Cryptosporidium", "Protozoan causing neonatal diarrhoea"], ["Pashu Ayurveda", "Ayurveda for animals"], ["Neonatal maladjustment", "Neurological syndrome of newborn foals"], ["Gut closure", "End of antibody absorption in the newborn"], ["Agalactia", "Absence of milk"]],
  d: ["Make a table of neonatal diarrhoea by age.", "Document local herbal remedies with permission.", "Learn the colostrum management steps."],
  vf: ["Timing of gut closure, ages of peak of each pathogen and colostrum volumes differ by species and text. Evidence for traditional remedies is variable and changing; verify with recent reviews. No drugs or doses are given."]
});

