topic("med", "Wildlife Acts, Zoo Rules and National and International Organisations", {
  o: ["Name the main Indian laws for wildlife and zoos", "Describe the role of the Central Zoo Authority", "Name the main international conventions and bodies", "Describe the veterinarian's duties in a zoo", "Explain the permissions needed for capture, transport and display"],
  i: "Wild animals do not belong to anyone in the usual sense. The law treats them as a public trust, and a veterinarian working with them must know what is allowed, what needs permission and what is a criminal offence. Many a good intention has landed a student in trouble because of a missed licence.",
  s: "India protects wildlife mainly through the Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972 and its amendments, administered by forest departments with the Central Zoo Authority regulating zoos. International trade is controlled through CITES. Veterinarians must keep records, follow recognised standards and work with the wildlife authorities in disease investigations and rescue.",
  n: [
    ["The Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972", "The Act protects wild animals, birds and plants and regulates hunting and trade. Its schedules list species by the level of protection, with the highest protection for the species in Schedule I and Part II of Schedule II, with severe penalties. It provides for sanctuaries, national parks, conservation reserves and community reserves, prohibits hunting except in narrow circumstances, regulates trade and possession of animal articles, and controls the taking and keeping of wild animals. Amendments over the years have changed schedules, penalties and added provisions for zoos and CITES. Always use the current text and schedules; the details here are a summary only."],
    ["Central Zoo Authority and zoo rules", "The Central Zoo Authority of India (CZA), set up under the Wildlife Act, evaluates and recognises zoos, sets standards for housing, upkeep, veterinary care, record keeping and display, and coordinates conservation breeding and animal exchange. No zoo may operate without recognition. Zoos must follow the Recognition of Zoo Rules (norms for enclosures, quarantine, veterinary facilities, hospital and necropsy rooms, staff, education and safety). Zoo veterinarians keep health and mortality records and report disease outbreaks and unusual deaths according to rules."],
    ["Other relevant Indian laws", "The Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act, 1960 and the rules made under it (including rules for performing animals and for the capture of animals) protect animals including those in captivity. The Environment (Protection) Act and the Biological Diversity Act touch on biodiversity and access to biological resources. The Prevention of Food Adulteration and the FSSAI rules matter for food from animals. The Animal Quarantine regulations govern importing animals and products. The Epidemic and Animal Disease rules, together with state acts on contagious diseases, enable control of outbreaks. Check the latest text of each."],
    ["Permissions for capture, translocation and display", "Capturing, tranquilising, transporting, exchanging or displaying a wild animal in India requires written permission from the Chief Wildlife Warden, with specific conditions about the purpose, method, welfare and the receiving facility. Records of each animal (acquisition, identification, breeding, disease and disposal) are maintained. Dead specimens are the property of the state: carcasses and parts are disposed of or preserved only as the law allows. Exchanging animals between zoos needs approvals and health certificates. Rescue and rehabilitation also follow state protocols."],
    ["International conventions and organisations", "CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora) regulates the trade through appendices; the IUCN Red List classifies the conservation status of species; the World Organisation for Animal Health (WOAH, formerly OIE) sets standards for animal health and disease reporting including wildlife disease; the Convention on Biological Diversity and the Convention on Migratory Species support conservation; the WAZA (World Association of Zoos and Aquariums) and regional zoo associations set welfare and ethics guidelines; TRAFFIC monitors the wildlife trade. FAO and WHO are also involved in zoonoses and One Health."],
    ["National organisations", "At national level, the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change and the State Forest Departments administer wildlife law. The Wildlife Institute of India (Dehradun) does wildlife research and training. The National Tiger Conservation Authority and Project Tiger, Project Elephant and Project Snow Leopard focus on key species. The Wildlife Crime Control Bureau fights illegal trade. The Zoological Survey of India and the Bombay Natural History Society support research. Rescue centres and wildlife veterinary units work with the forest departments, and the Indian Veterinary Research Institute and ICAR institutes support disease investigation."],
    ["The veterinarian's duties in practice", "A zoo or wildlife veterinarian carries out preventive health care, treatment and handling under approved protocols; keeps accurate records; reports notifiable diseases and unexplained deaths immediately; performs and documents post-mortems; works with forest officers on seizures, rescue, rehabilitation and release; helps with man-animal conflict cases; and never retains, sells or moves wild animal parts. Ethics and welfare come first: the least stressful method of handling is chosen, and euthanasia follows recognised guidelines."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Law or body", "Level", "Main purpose", "Vet involvement"], r: [["Wildlife (Protection) Act 1972", "National", "Protect species, regulate trade", "Permissions, rescue, records"], ["Central Zoo Authority", "National", "Zoo standards and recognition", "Hospital, quarantine, necropsy"], ["PCA Act 1960", "National", "Prevent cruelty", "Welfare, certification"], ["CITES", "International", "Control of trade", "Health certificates"], ["WOAH", "International", "Animal health standards", "Disease reporting"]] },
  df: ["Schedule: a list of species attached to the Wildlife Act, sorted by degree of protection.", "CITES: international convention regulating wildlife trade.", "Translocation: moving a wild animal from one place to another.", "Chief Wildlife Warden: official who gives permission under the Wildlife Act."],
  tm: [["Schedule I", "Highest protection"], ["CZA", "Central Zoo Authority"], ["CITES", "Trade convention"], ["IUCN", "Conservation union"], ["WOAH", "Animal health organisation"], ["Translocation", "Moving wild animals"], ["Sanctuary", "Protected area"], ["Rescue centre", "Facility for injured wildlife"]],
  st: ["Check the species schedule.", "Seek the written permission.", "Follow handling norms.", "Keep records.", "Report disease.", "Dispose of carcasses by law."],
  ex: ["Capture of a leopard in a village.", "Exchange of animals between zoos.", "Post-mortem of a zoo tiger."],
  k: ["Wildlife Act.", "CZA.", "CITES.", "Permissions.", "Veterinarian's duties."],
  m: ["No calculations. Schedules and penalties change; use the current Act."],
  sa: [["What is the CZA?", "The Central Zoo Authority, which recognises and regulates zoos in India."], ["What is CITES?", "A convention that controls international trade in endangered species."], ["Who permits capture of a wild animal?", "The Chief Wildlife Warden."]],
  la: [["Describe the legal framework for wildlife and zoos in India.", "Wildlife Act, CZA, PCA Act, rules and permissions."], ["Describe the national and international organisations in wildlife conservation and animal health.", "CITES, IUCN, WOAH, WII, NTCA, TRAFFIC."]],
  q: [["The Wildlife Protection Act was passed in", ["1972", "1960", "1899", "1910"], 0, "Year of the Act."],
    ["Recognition of zoos in India is by", ["Central Zoo Authority", "ICAR", "FSSAI", "BIS"], 0, "CZA."],
    ["International trade of endangered species is controlled by", ["CITES", "FAO only", "IVRI", "ICMR"], 0, "Convention."],
    ["WOAH was formerly known as", ["OIE", "WHO", "FAO", "IUCN"], 0, "Name change."],
    ["Capture of a wild animal needs permission from", ["Chief Wildlife Warden", "Village head", "Panchayat clerk", "Police station"], 0, "Legal requirement."]],
  f: [["Conservation reserve", "Protected area category"], ["Studbook", "Breeding records"], ["Wildlife Crime Control Bureau", "Anti-trafficking agency"], ["Man-animal conflict", "Clashes between wildlife and people"], ["Seizure", "Confiscation of illegal specimens"]],
  d: ["Make a list of the schedules and examples.", "Draw a chart of the permission steps.", "Collect the current CZA norms."],
  vf: ["Acts, schedules, penalties and rules are amended often. This is a summary only; check the current text with your teacher or the official sources. Not legal advice."]
});

