topic("med", "Viral Diseases of Ruminants II: Bluetongue, Sheep Pox, Goat Pox, PPR and Classical Swine Fever", {
  o: ["Describe bluetongue and its vectors", "Describe sheep pox and goat pox", "Describe peste des petits ruminants", "Describe classical swine fever and African swine fever", "Describe diagnosis and control, including vaccination"],
  i: "Small ruminants and pigs are the livelihood of millions of poor households in India. Their viral diseases cause sudden, heavy losses and are controllable with vaccines that every veterinary student should know.",
  s: "Bluetongue (Orbivirus, Culicoides midges), sheep and goat pox (Capripoxvirus), PPR (Morbillivirus) and classical swine fever (Pestivirus) are the main viral killers of sheep, goats and pigs in India. All have vaccines, except African swine fever.",
  n: [
    ["Bluetongue", "An Orbivirus (many serotypes) spread by Culicoides biting midges; sheep are the most severely affected, cattle are carriers and goats mild. Signs in sheep: fever, swollen face and lips, congested and ulcerated mouth with a blue-red tongue (the cyanotic tongue is infrequent), salivation, lameness from coronitis, 'wool break', and death from pneumonia and weakness. Cattle: usually silent but cause abortion, congenital defects (hydranencephaly), and spread. Diagnosis: RT-PCR, virus isolation, ELISA, AGID. Control: vector control, housing at night, vaccines (serotype-specific) and movement restrictions. Season: warm, wet months."],
    ["Sheep pox and goat pox", "Capripoxviruses, causing the most serious pox diseases of livestock. Fever, then skin lesions that pass from macule to papule, vesicle, pustule and scab, mostly on hairless skin, and internal lesions in the lungs and digestive tract in severe cases; mortality is high in lambs and kids. Diagnosis: clinical picture, electron microscopy, PCR, virus isolation and serology. Control: vaccination with live attenuated vaccines (specific strains), isolation, burning or burying of carcasses, and restrictions of animal markets. Differentiate from orf (contagious ecthyma), which causes scabby lesions around the lips and nose and is a zoonosis, and from pseudo-pox and dermatophilosis."],
    ["Peste des petits ruminants (PPR)", "Morbillivirus (similar to rinderpest) in sheep and goats: high fever, depression, ocular and nasal discharge that turns mucopurulent and crusts the nostrils, erosive stomatitis, foul diarrhoea, bronchopneumonia, dehydration, high mortality (especially in kids and young goats). Lesions: erosions in mouth and gut, 'zebra stripes' in the colon and caecum, pneumonia. Diagnosis: RT-PCR, antigen capture ELISA, serology. Control: live attenuated vaccine (a single dose gives long immunity), isolation, movement control; PPR is the target of a global control and eradication programme under the FAO and OIE (target of eradication by 2030), and India runs a national control programme. Differentials: pasteurellosis, CCPP, bluetongue, sheep and goat pox."],
    ["Classical swine fever (hog cholera)", "A Pestivirus related to BVDV that infects pigs and wild boar. Acute: high fever, loss of appetite, huddling, conjunctivitis, constipation then diarrhoea, purple discolouration of the skin of ears, abdomen and legs, staggering gait, convulsions and death in 5 to 15 days with high mortality. Chronic and congenital (persistent infection): thin, stunted pigs, immunotolerant carriers. Lesions: petechiae on the kidney (turkey egg kidney), lymph node haemorrhage, spleen infarcts, and 'button ulcers' in the large intestine. Diagnosis: RT-PCR, antigen ELISA and fluorescent antibody tests. Control: vaccination with live attenuated vaccine, biosecurity and in some countries stamping out. Differentiate from African swine fever and erysipelas."],
    ["African swine fever", "A large DNA virus (Asfivirus) transmitted by contact, contaminated feed (swill), pork products and soft ticks. It is clinically like classical swine fever but there is no vaccine and no treatment, and mortality is nearly 100 percent in domestic pigs. It entered India in 2020 in the north-east and is a major threat. Control: strict biosecurity, no swill feeding, movement control, culling and disposal, compensation, and rapid reporting. ASF is notifiable."],
    ["Other viral diseases of small ruminants and pigs", "Orf (contagious ecthyma; Parapoxvirus), a zoonosis causing scabby lesions on the lips and teats; Visna-maedi and caprine arthritis encephalitis (lentiviruses); Nairobi sheep disease; Rift Valley fever (mosquito-borne, abortion storms, zoonotic; exotic to India); Porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome (PRRS), Aujeszky's disease (pseudorabies), swine influenza, transmissible gastroenteritis, porcine epidemic diarrhoea and porcine parvovirus. Many of these are tested in pig and sheep practice and have vaccines or herd management tools."],
    ["Principles of control", "Report suspect cases. Isolate affected animals. Use vaccines where they exist and follow the schedule. Reduce vector exposure for arboviruses. Prevent contact with other flocks and with markets. Clean and disinfect premises and equipment. Dispose of carcasses by burial or burning. Educate farmers on early reporting. Use post-vaccination serology to check coverage."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Disease", "Virus", "Species", "Key sign", "Control"], r: [["Bluetongue", "Orbivirus", "Sheep, cattle", "Swollen face, mouth ulcers, coronitis", "Vector control, vaccine"], ["Sheep and goat pox", "Capripoxvirus", "Sheep, goats", "Pox lesions on skin", "Live vaccine"], ["PPR", "Morbillivirus", "Goats, sheep", "Discharges, mouth erosions, diarrhoea", "Live vaccine"], ["CSF", "Pestivirus", "Pigs", "Fever, purple skin, button ulcers", "Live vaccine, biosecurity"], ["ASF", "Asfivirus", "Pigs", "Like CSF, nearly 100% mortality", "Biosecurity, culling"]] },
  df: ["Vector: living carrier that transmits an infectious agent.", "Capripoxvirus: poxvirus genus of sheep, goat and lumpy skin disease.", "Button ulcer: round raised ulcer in the intestine in CSF.", "Eradication: permanent removal of a disease from a region or the world."],
  tm: [["Culicoides", "Bluetongue vector"], ["Capripox", "Sheep and goat pox genus"], ["PPR", "Goat plague"], ["Button ulcer", "CSF lesion"], ["Turkey-egg kidney", "CSF kidney appearance"], ["Swill", "Kitchen waste for pigs"], ["Orf", "Contagious ecthyma"], ["Eradication", "Global PPR aim"]],
  st: ["Recognise the clinical pattern.", "Collect swabs, blood and tissues.", "Confirm in the laboratory.", "Isolate and vaccinate.", "Dispose of carcasses.", "Surveil the area."],
  ex: ["Goat kids with crusted noses and diarrhoea.", "Sheep flock with swollen faces in the monsoon.", "Pigs with purple ears."],
  k: ["Bluetongue vector.", "Capripox lesions.", "PPR vaccine.", "CSF lesions.", "ASF difference."],
  m: ["No calculations."],
  sa: [["What is the vector of bluetongue?", "Culicoides midges."], ["How is PPR prevented?", "By the live attenuated vaccine and movement control."], ["How does ASF differ from CSF?", "ASF has no vaccine and is caused by a DNA virus."]],
  la: [["Describe PPR and its control.", "Virus, signs, lesions, diagnosis, vaccine, eradication programme."], ["Describe classical swine fever and African swine fever.", "Viruses, signs, lesions, diagnosis, control."]],
  q: [["Bluetongue virus is spread by", ["Culicoides", "Ticks", "Fleas", "Lice"], 0, "Midges."],
    ["PPR is caused by a", ["Morbillivirus", "Parapoxvirus", "Orbivirus", "Asfivirus"], 0, "Related to rinderpest."],
    ["Button ulcers occur in", ["Classical swine fever", "FMD", "Bluetongue", "Orf"], 0, "Large intestine."],
    ["Orf is also called", ["Contagious ecthyma", "Sheep pox", "Ephemeral fever", "Blue tongue"], 0, "Parapoxvirus."],
    ["African swine fever has", ["No vaccine", "A cheap vaccine", "A tick-free cycle", "A live vaccine"], 0, "Biosecurity only."]],
  f: [["Hydranencephaly", "Cavities in brain in newborns, from bluetongue"], ["Coronitis", "Inflammation of the coronary band"], ["Wool break", "Fall of wool after fever"], ["Stamping out", "Culling and disposal"], ["Swill feeding", "Feeding kitchen waste"]],
  d: ["Prepare a table of pox lesions by stage.", "Compare CSF and ASF.", "Study the national PPR plan."],
  vf: ["Serotypes, vaccines and national programme timelines (PPR eradication target, ASF situation) change; verify with current sources. No doses are given. Rift Valley fever and some others are exotic to India."]
});

topic("med", "Rabies and Viral Diseases of Horses: Equine Influenza, EIA and Equine Herpes", {
  o: ["Describe rabies pathogenesis, forms and diagnosis", "Describe the principles of rabies control and human exposure", "Describe equine influenza", "Describe equine infectious anaemia", "Describe equine herpesvirus (rhinopneumonitis) infections"],
  i: "Rabies is the deadliest disease in this book and India has the largest number of human rabies deaths in the world, mostly from dog bites. Horses carry their own set of respiratory and blood-borne viruses that are treated seriously in racing and breeding circles.",
  s: "Rabies (Lyssavirus) is a fatal encephalitis of all mammals spread by bite. Equine influenza (Influenza A H3N8) is a respiratory disease of horses. EIA (lentivirus) is a persistent, notifiable infection with recurrent fever and anaemia. Equine herpesviruses 1 and 4 cause respiratory disease, abortion and neurological disease.",
  n: [
    ["Rabies: pathogenesis", "The virus enters through a bite (saliva), replicates locally in muscle, then travels along peripheral nerves to the spinal cord and brain at a rate of a few millimetres a day, so the incubation period is long and variable (weeks to months, rarely longer), and shorter with bites close to the head. It spreads from the brain back along nerves to the salivary glands and other organs. After clinical signs appear the disease is almost always fatal. Wildlife reservoirs and stray dogs maintain it, and in India the dog is the main reservoir and the source for human cases."],
    ["Rabies: clinical forms", "Furious form: behavioural change, restlessness, aggression, biting at objects, abnormal appetite for non-food items (pica), altered voice (bellowing in cattle, hoarse bark in dogs), hypersalivation, inability to swallow, convulsions, paralysis and death. Dumb (paralytic) form: early paralysis of the jaw (dropped jaw), throat and limbs, with little aggression; common in cattle and dogs. Cattle may have bellowing, tail rigidity, straining and sexual excitement; horses show colic-like signs, self-mutilation and ataxia; cats are fierce. The animal does not fear water, hence 'hydrophobia' as a human sign is the difficulty of swallowing."],
    ["Rabies: diagnosis and handling", "Intra vitam diagnosis is unreliable. After death: examine the brain (Negri bodies in neurons of the hippocampus and Purkinje cells, now supplemented by the fluorescent antibody test (FAT), rapid immunohistochemistry, RT-PCR and virus isolation or mouse inoculation). Never handle a suspect animal with bare hands; submit the head on ice and keep staff protected. Observation of a biting dog for 10 days is the classical approach: if it stays healthy, it was not shedding at the time of the bite."],
    ["Rabies: control and human exposure", "Vaccination of dogs (the main control step, with coverage of at least 70 percent in the target population), sterilisation and registration (the Animal Birth Control programme), removal of rabid animals, wildlife vaccination in some countries, quarantine and import rules, and responsible ownership. Pre-exposure vaccination is recommended for veterinarians, students and other high-risk workers. Post-exposure: immediate and thorough washing of the wound with soap and water for 15 minutes, antiseptic, medical consultation, vaccination series and rabies immunoglobulin when indicated, as in national guidelines. The aim of 'Zero by 30' is to end human deaths from dog rabies by 2030 (WHO, FAO, WOAH and the Global Alliance); India has a national action plan for rabies elimination. Schedules are not given here; use the national guidelines."],
    ["Equine influenza", "Influenza A (H3N8 now; H7N7 historical) is highly contagious, spreading by aerosol and contact in racing stables, shows and sales. Signs: sudden high fever, dry harsh cough, serous then mucopurulent nasal discharge, depression, muscle soreness, enlarged lymph nodes; recovery in 1 to 3 weeks if rested, but secondary bacterial pneumonia is common if the horse is worked early. Diagnosis: nasal swab by RT-PCR or antigen test, paired serology. Control: vaccination (with updated strains) per programme, quarantine for new arrivals, rest and good ventilation. Equine influenza spread across India in 1987 and in 2008 to Australia from imported horses, which show how quarantine matters."],
    ["Equine infectious anaemia", "A lentivirus (EIAV) in the equine family (horses, donkeys, mules) transmitted by biting flies (horse flies, stable flies), by infected blood on needles, instruments and by the placenta. Persistent infection for life. Acute: fever, depression, thrombocytopenia, bleeding. Chronic or recurrent: cycles of fever, weight loss, anaemia, jaundice, ventral oedema, weakness. Inapparent carriers. Diagnosis: Coggins test (AGID) or ELISA for antibodies. No treatment and no vaccine; reactors must be isolated or euthanised by regulation (it is notifiable, and positive horses are removed or segregated in a way that depends on the country). Control: testing at trade, racing and breeding, no shared needles and fly control."],
    ["Equine herpesviruses and other equine viral diseases", "EHV-1 and EHV-4 cause equine rhinopneumonitis: fever, cough, nasal discharge in young horses (EHV-4 mainly); EHV-1 abortion storms in the last third of gestation (healthy foal born dead or weak) and the neurological form (equine herpesvirus myeloencephalopathy) with hindlimb ataxia, urinary retention and recumbency. Latency in the trigeminal ganglion and reactivation with stress. Diagnosis: nasal swab PCR, foetal tissues, serology. Control: vaccination of pregnant mares per schedule, isolation of abortion cases, stress reduction, hygiene, and quarantine of neurological outbreaks. Other equine viruses: equine encephalitides (EEE, WEE, VEE, West Nile), Japanese encephalitis, equine viral arteritis (a venereal and respiratory virus, notifiable in many countries), African horse sickness and equine coital exanthema."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Disease", "Virus", "Key sign", "Test", "Control"], r: [["Rabies", "Lyssavirus", "Behaviour change, paralysis, death", "FAT, Negri bodies, RT-PCR", "Dog vaccination, ABC, PEP"], ["Equine influenza", "Influenza A H3N8", "Fever, dry cough", "RT-PCR", "Vaccination, quarantine"], ["EIA", "Lentivirus", "Recurrent fever, anaemia", "Coggins AGID", "Test and segregate or destroy"], ["EHV-1", "Herpesvirus", "Abortion, ataxia", "PCR", "Vaccine, isolation"]] },
  df: ["Negri bodies: eosinophilic inclusions in neurons of rabid animals.", "FAT: fluorescent antibody test.", "PEP: post-exposure prophylaxis.", "Coggins test: agar gel immunodiffusion test for EIA."],
  tm: [["Negri bodies", "Rabies inclusions"], ["FAT", "Fluorescent antibody test"], ["PEP", "Post-exposure treatment"], ["Dumb rabies", "Paralytic form"], ["Coggins", "EIA test"], ["H3N8", "Equine influenza subtype"], ["EHM", "Herpes neurological disease"], ["ABC", "Animal birth control"]],
  st: ["Handle with protection.", "Observe or isolate.", "Submit brain after death.", "Confirm with FAT or PCR.", "Vaccinate in-contacts.", "Educate on PEP."],
  ex: ["Dog bites in a village.", "Racing stable with coughing horses.", "Horse positive on Coggins."],
  k: ["Rabies forms.", "Negri body.", "Post-exposure care.", "EIA test.", "EHV-1 forms."],
  m: ["No calculations. Vaccination and PEP schedules follow national guidelines."],
  sa: [["What are Negri bodies?", "Cytoplasmic inclusions in neurons of rabid animals."], ["What is the first aid for a dog bite?", "Wash the wound thoroughly with soap and running water for 15 minutes and seek medical care."], ["What does the Coggins test detect?", "Antibodies to equine infectious anaemia virus."]],
  la: [["Describe rabies in animals and its control.", "Pathogenesis, forms, diagnosis, vaccination, human exposure, national programme."], ["Describe equine infectious anaemia and equine influenza.", "Cause, transmission, signs, diagnosis, control."]],
  q: [["Rabies virus travels along", ["Nerves", "Blood only", "Lymph only", "Bile"], 0, "Neurotropic."],
    ["Negri bodies are found in", ["Hippocampus neurons", "Liver", "Skin", "Muscle"], 0, "Rabies diagnosis."],
    ["EIA is diagnosed by", ["Coggins test", "Tuberculin test", "CMT", "Mallein"], 0, "AGID."],
    ["Equine influenza vaccine aims to protect against", ["Respiratory disease", "Colic", "Tetanus", "Strangles"], 0, "Prevention."],
    ["First step after a bite is", ["Wash with soap and water", "Apply chilli", "Wait", "Bandage tightly"], 0, "Immediate wash."]],
  f: [["Hydrophobia", "Human rabies sign of fear of water"], ["Fixed virus", "Laboratory rabies virus used for vaccines"], ["Street virus", "Naturally circulating rabies virus"], ["Abortion storm", "Many abortions at once"], ["Equine encephalitis", "Mosquito-borne brain disease"]],
  d: ["Chart the rabies pathogenesis.", "Prepare a dog vaccination drive plan.", "List the notifiable equine diseases."],
  vf: ["Incubation periods, vaccine schedules, PEP protocols and national programme targets are summaries; check the current national and WHO guidelines. No doses are given. Rabies handling needs training and pre-exposure vaccination."]
});

